ion being discussed and as I interpreted it a request for said information.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2302-snakesandsuns","name":"Snakesandsuns","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2302_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"224"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T09:43:02-07:00","id":1295852,"title":"","content":"Changing the subject...<br /> <br /> Luthe, like Mahalanka and the Nameless Lair, felt in 2nd very much just like &quot;the base of such-and-such&quot;. Now it, like them, feels like an interesting place on its own merit, which counts such-and-such-an-elder amongst its notable NPCs, rather than being purely about that Elder.<br /> I liked the vision of all these different kinds of bizarre beastmen, and the and music they make.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T09:45:40-07:00","id":1295855,"title":"","content":"The spiky armour I liked conceptually (though it's hard to imagine it silver, rather than black and adorned with Reiklander skulls and fur), but because it was based partially on Stamina charms not available at the time, I found the charmset a bit difficult to grok.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T11:04:03-07:00","id":1295872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Snakesandsuns</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1295824#post1295824\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the simple act of providing a link to the relevant information</div> </div> </div> </div>I've not been describing reactions to this book based on guesswork.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Snakesandsuns</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I interpreted it a request for said information.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't understand why a question such as &quot;is it different&quot; needs to be interpreted.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 03-11-2019, 02:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T11:07:34-07:00","id":1295874,"title":"","content":"Okay, now for the actual last of the full Dominion write-ups, the much-vaunted Caul.<br /> <br /> <br /> First off we\u2019ve got a bit of expansion on the mythology of it, at least from the Lunar perspective; more than merely being a place where Gaia and Luna walked together, it\u2019s referred to as a place born from the Primordial\u2019s dream of her Incarnae lover, hence some of the oddities inherent to the place. There\u2019s a striking description of what the significance of the place is to the Lunar Exalted, the strange idea that they experience it as a kind of spiritual sibling, as well as an insight into what they feel from the shrine cities that is distinct from the elemental breakdowns significant to the Dragon Blooded. It puts me in mind of prior Editions trying to match Garou elements to Lunars with an idea that they\u2019re guardians of nature on Luna\u2019s behalf for the sake of their lover; it never really fit to their character or the mythology of the setting, but something in the bones of it was compelling to me, and I think that taking it from an attitude applying to the whole of Creation and concentrating it down to a singular place (which thus gets to be really weird) helps to retain the more worthwhile parts of that, give them somewhere that they can really shine, and free up the Lunars elsewhere. Plus, it reflects the attention back on the Lunars themselves, given how the important thing isn\u2019t so much as Gaia as Gaia\u2019s perspective on their patron.<br /> <br /> Having brought the Dragon Blooded up, there\u2019s also the point about how it is sacred to them as well, having once been a point of common cause between the two; it solidifies a lot of my sense of the place having a significant common history between the two (which I find to compound the tragedy of how they\u2019re currently fighting over it). The idea of them having worked to fix it more to Creation\u2019s reality is more abstractly mythical than my own notions that they needed to defend it from their fellow Exalted (although it doesn\u2019t strictly exclude it), and serving as a basis for its disappearance, we finally get a timeline for the place; so it vanished some time around the middle of the Shogunate, and reappeared some 260 years into the Realm era. Nice and clean.<br /> So yeah, I\u2019d say that\u2019s a good starting premise for both sides wanting to hold power over the Caul; that it\u2019s more than just a strategic benefit, more than just resources to exploit, for each side that\u2019s invested in it it\u2019s basically a Crusade. That makes it distinctly compelling, and terrifying.<br /> <br /> Yes, for all that Sha\u2019a Oka is more of the open war upon the Realm type, it\u2019s still pointed out that his style and his assets still restrict his capacity to fight in direct army clashes. I imagine what sets this apart from the usual, more attrition minded Silver Pact member is the scope; that a population stretched across the entirety of the Caul is persistently harassing the Realm occupation, and that the Lunars themselves won\u2019t fall back from it as they might among the typical Dominions. And there have probably still been times before the current crisis in which the Lunars\u2019 forces seized and held territory, even if just to create a period in which the pilgrimage path would be denied to their enemies. Maybe also an element of how, even when the road to the Last City was under Realm control, it might be persistently surrounded on all sides by hostile forces. So it ends up somewhere between the typically more flexible Dominions and those that are cultivated to engage in active field warfare. At least before the current events in which they got to drive the Realm forces completely back into Faxai, and place each of the other cities under a Lunar general\u2019s control.<br /> <br /> Huh, wonder what turtlewolves are.<br /> <br /> Now we\u2019re getting a bit of insight into the mortal inhabitants of the Caul, noted to be descended from a mix of West and Southwestern settlers\u2026 ah yes, it took me a moment to recall how Oka was supposed to be the only survivor of the Caul\u2019s disappearance, meaning that the current societies would only be a few centuries old. Given what might be known or guessed about the two Directions, I suppose that the sudden appearance of a huge, verdant landmass would have been extremely attractive to people from all over, before they necessarily knew much about it. Still, it\u2019s interesting to consider the point about them possibly knowing the island better than Oka, for all that they\u2019ve been there no longer.<br /> <br /> Bit of description of their culture\u2026 I wonder if the rites that they conflict over are a more mystically substantial thing or purely religious (with the idea that they dispute something like the order in which they\u2019re told just for that reason still being very believable and somewhat familiar to me). Clans raise their children in common rather than divided into nuclear families\u2026 huh, they live in a gender segregated manner that has something to do with their rituals, wonder what that\u2019s about. But it\u2019s something that actually appeals to me, on grounds of being able to invoke some elements of the portrayal of the original Air Nomad culture in Avatar. I also like some of the descriptions of some of the manners in which they live, such as carrying fires on backs, people who swim before they can walk, and using giant apes as war mounts.<br /> <br /> Yes, there seems to be a recurring theme of how people from the Caul are set apart, with this inclusion of the point about how they\u2019ve got their own separate languages, with a further layer in secret ones employed by each gender (with the amusing note of the masculine one having been lost in trade to a god). And the people haven\u2019t been there very long, so the idea would seem to be that something about the Caul changes people (which wouldn\u2019t be an out of place element for a landmass created in tribute to somebody like Luna). Makes me wonder if that\u2019s a possibility in areas under Realm control, that the authorities have continuously tried to curtail. Makes me wonder if part of how the Lunars see the Realm profaning the island is with their attempts to impose uniformity.<br /> <br /> The theme is reinforced with the point about them being evasive with non-Caulborn, although something that strikes me here is the notion that people being born from outsiders while living on the Caul is a rare occurrence; I wonder if that means that those brought for Realm settlements have a high turnover rate, or that something in the nature of the place means that people who have not fully embraced it have greatly reduced fecundity, either from mindset or biology. Still, it sounds like one could get some interesting narratives from one making the transition from being treated evasively, to being treated merely aloofly, to departing to fully live among the Caulborn.<br /> <br /> The more I read about them, the more intriguing I find the idea that the Caulborn have taken up these attitudes and practices in such a relatively short amount of time. Makes me think of the original settlers who came in thinking it was just another land, and wound up getting more than they bargained for, and needed to adapt to the requirements of their new home/god. Each of the described bans gives me an intriguing idea about what might occur for violating them, with the point about hatred of burying the dead in particular making me wonder if the people fear profaning the land, or that it will do something to give their dead back. And the point about needing to be sure that something is a person before you speak to it makes me think that this whole place could really take up a lot of imagery from the Foggy Swamp, with the idea that you might end up communing with some image of your past or future.<br /> <br /> Aaaand the point about interactions with the architecture take it from intriguing to pretty damn creepy; there\u2019s something really unique and freakish in the idea that marks into stonework won\u2019t stay in the same place. Despite its brevity, I actually find the story of Ragara Dolara being haunted by his name like that to be a very effective horror story, and think that a slightly longer version of it could be something that would really stick with people; I can damn well see the man encountering his doppleganger just standing in the middle of the road with that knife, and it\u2019s incredibly unsettling.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s interesting how the nature of their relations to the Realm are shaped by a combination of the typical experiences of colonised people and the peculiarities of the Caul, with the mere act of undertaking the pilgrimage being enough to endear Dragon Blooded to the natives. It makes me think that, in the eyes of the Caulborn, whatever one\u2019s motives in undertaking those rituals, to do so upon the island makes one akin to the people who live and breathe the place. It feels like it would make for an interesting growing discourse and cultivated relationship for any Dragon Blooded players who undertake the pilgrimage. From the other end, I think it\u2019s a good nuance to Realm policies that they find the aid of the Caulborn in undertaking the pilgrimage to be important enough to not want to turn them against imperial forces, and try to take a lighter hand with them. Similar is the point of how the Caul, of all places, forms a bit of a break between Immaculate Order practice and official Realm power; that it\u2019s a place where the dogma of the monks can end up overriding servitude to the secular Realm government, and even any reverence that they might have for the Caul itself (it being seen as a place where the Dragon Blooded originated), being unable to tolerate a place where the people adhere to such distinctive rites. I think it really helps convey that there\u2019s something very different about the Caul.<br /> <br /> So, what about how the people view the Silver Pact? Yyyeesss, it makes sense that they wouldn\u2019t necessarily have a reverence for the Lunars, seeing as it\u2019s likely that most of the Caulborn place no particular stock in Luna themselves, but I\u2019ll admit that I\u2019m surprised that only a minority have any dealings. Mind, looking at more of it, I think it does more good for the portrayal of the Caulborn <i>and</i> the Lunars to present both sides as having diverse relations to one another. That the Black Lion is esteemed by basically all of them makes a lot of sense, not just because their ancestors would have likely seen him first (and perhaps may have been the first new people he saw on his return), and he\u2019s probably the only person in the world who\u2019s as intertwined with the Caul\u2019s nature as themselves. Otherwise, I think it really reinforces the mystery of the place to give several of the Caulborn clans either achievements against the Lunars or assets valuable enough to compel Lunars to treat with them on their terms, even apart from finding allies to fight the Realm. For some reason, I keep needing to remind myself that the portrayal of the Lunars less invested in the people is more callous than hostile (although I have to imagine that some among the Lunars who come there, either for study or to join Oka, find the practices and caginess of the Caulborn to be extremely frustrating).<br /> <br /> There are said to be a few beastfolk clans, resulting from alliance with the Lunars, of which we get three: The Kongar lionfolk, apparently one of the oldest societies on the Caul from their long association with His Divine Lunar Presence, and with whom they have an interesting sounding relationship; they regard Oka as truly divine, and such an embodiment of the Caul that they make a point of recording omens surrounding him, but don\u2019t actually worship the man. They\u2019re referred to as being in possession of good wood and metal crafts, and build cities in the jungles, which I imagine would be partially a consequence of them being there for so long. The Ninga hawkfolk seem to have a bit more Caulborn weirdness, such as their own unusual language (a little bit ahead, they\u2019re referred to as having the Musical Voice Merit, which conveys the point that they\u2019re non-aquatic beastfolk whose manner of communication and self-expression is modified) and forbid themselves particular words and phrases at times and seasons. They also have an interesting relationship with a Lunar, with Sandswept Garda Empress not being a native member of their people, but being adopted as a clan matriarch. And the Yamalu boarfolk, whose Lunar patron was lost to an Immaculate warrior, but who maintain her martial traditions. I like how their process for choosing leaders doesn\u2019t just emphasize strength, but also courage and cleverness, gives a sense that what they\u2019re looking for is more rounded (although I still feel drawn to these the least). No, the ones that I think more of are the beastfolk that aren\u2019t affiliated with the Pact at all, with the idea that several of them apply their unique advantages to undertaking the Caul\u2019s rites and shape their own taboos; the mothfolk in particular sound fun, both for the image of them and the idea that they don\u2019t believe in the sun. Just the overall sense that the Caul is a place, more than any other, in which transformation into beastfolk could occur spontaneously.<br /> <br /> Now, onto the Kongar city of Morovath. Nice to see the previous idea about shifting marks on architecture extend to even an inhabited city, with some good lunar cycle imagery to create a city that shifts back and forth between inspiring beauty and forbidding grimness depending on the time of the month. I especially like how the transformations aren\u2019t supposed to be all at once, or even a gradual thing; that they can shift at unpredictable paces, with relocations just from being out of sight for a second. Ah, so the Kongar did not build the city, they merely rediscovered it amidst the jungles and are given peacekeeping authority by Oka over the rest of the inhabitants, the other Caulborn settlers or the children of Lunars come to join the cause; I have to imagine there\u2019s something particularly stressful in being Moon-Touched and not only being dragged along to the Caul, but having to live in a city where most of the rest of the inhabitants will shun you, or at best speak very obscurely to you. The descriptions of how the city\u2019s resources (and surrounding landscape) are dedicated towards war with the Realm is all well and good, but I don\u2019t think anything about the city could top the idea that artistic expression is matched to the phase, especially the idea that the words stay the same, you just sing them differently.<br /> <br /> Then there\u2019s one of the main events of the island, the shrine cities themselves. Starts off by reiterating the value of the pilgrimage to the Dragon Blooded, while noting that there\u2019s no record or memory of what the Last City of Feng-Yi could provide to Lunars, and they\u2019ve never managed to hold all five since the Usurpation. It gets into a description of each of the cities, and do I care for this to be how I\u2019m introduced to the ones besides Faxai\u2026 no. It\u2019s written here as though to serve as somebody\u2019s introduction to the concept, it\u2019s sufficient. It wouldn\u2019t be the first time in Exalted I first heard of something in its more summarised form in a different book to its main description, so in a sense it\u2019s even a bit nostalgic.<br /> <br /> That out of the way, we\u2019re reintroduced to Faxai and its quirk of the land being so sacred with Earth Essence that no buildings are touched on the surface; it makes sense to point to how its defences remain sufficient to repulse Lunar assault, but it\u2019s curious how it doesn\u2019t describe the starting rite of the pilgrimage, considering the rest.<br /> <br /> Such as Garianghis of the Wood Shrine, which sounds like it can invoke some nice imagery of Cambodian temple ruins with how it\u2019s so overgrown and overshadowed by an enormous tree. We get a reference to Third Daughter of the Leaves\u2019 turtlewolves, and I\u2019m guessing from the description that they aren\u2019t beastfolk, but rather the offspring of her chimeric spirit shape; I want to picture\u2026 wolves with snapping turtle beaks, and spiky armoured shells. I\u2019ve been enamoured of the image of a sacred picture of the world untouched by humanity since I read wiki entries of the Emerald Dream from Warcraft, so I like something reminiscent of that in the Lunar experience of the shrine. That and the idea that they might be drawn to the city in hopes of meeting Luna again, though my favourite detail of that is the idea that it\u2019s so important that the otherwise nasty sounding Third Daughter won\u2019t deny it to her fellows, even if she\u2019s personally more interested in figuring out a way to use its powers to assault the dreams of all Terrestrials on the Caul.<br /> <br /> Damn, Houshou\u2019s gimmick of burning itself down every night only to reappear in the morning might knock Morovath out of the spot of freakiest point on the place, especially with the idea that the shrine\u2019s guardian monks remain inside the conflagration and, unlike the Lunars, don\u2019t withstand the flame, they just come back as though they\u2019re part of the scenery. Narrating the experience of watching the city burn sounds like it could make for a particularly memorable game scene, even for somebody visiting the Caul for purposes other than pilgrimage; I\u2019m imagining playing somebody there to visit Skathra Venomchild, and as the sun goes down she jauntily gives them fair warning that they might want to depart before everything starts spontaneously combusting, and then transitioning the action out through the streets and watching the finale from afar. Followed up by Skathra and her Lunars going around hunting colonists for sport, because the Silver Pact still needs a <i>few</i> monsters, and some of Raksi\u2019s slot has been freed up.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I\u2019m just imagining Jural and Lukha actually managing to subdue Bhadri, and the former invites his boy home to meet the parents and present the captive fae empress in tribute, and Lukha becomes a bit disturbed to see what his lover is dedicated to.<br /> <br /> Sekima\u2019s experience of entering a ruined city and perceiving the memory of what it was like when alive sounds really cool, as does the idea of Lunars getting a unique capacity to make edits by interaction with the memory forms. I think that Garda-Empress\u2019 plan for the place is the most creative and focused on the long-term; she doesn\u2019t seem to expect to hold the shrine indefinitely, so she\u2019s trying to ensure that in the event that Terrestrials return, she\u2019ll find the memory of the city poisoned against them specifically. This one also strikes me as being where the exclusion of Solars would be most keenly felt; I\u2019m imagining a mixed circle of the three types of Exalted, where the Lunar and Dragon Blood enter and start interacting with things and people, and the Solar is stuck waiting outside awkwardly, watching Circlemates going around touching and talking to nothing.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve got to admit, Melilune\u2019s premise of walking into the city\u2019s reflection and talking to the images of other Exalted pilgrims feels very similar to Sekima\u2019s quality. I guess the thing to take from it is that the reflection city is seemingly something that actually remains alive and continues moving forwards, rather than just being a memory. Still, it doesn\u2019t quite leap out to me the way that the others do. It all finishes off with a sidebar of the ways in which some of the Caulborn interact with the cities; I think the most of the idea that Houshou\u2019s ashes are collected for body art and medicinal purposes, while the point about the people using Sekima to visit the memories of their own family members throws off some of my certainty about how the place is supposed to work.<br /> We get a short section about the experience of the wilderness, and how the form of lands beyond the shrine cities and pilgrim\u2019s road is inherently malleable and difficult to travel, with geographic features and spatial relationships capable of warping without warning. I enjoy the point about how getting around the Caul requires developing a certain intuition for its moods; even with the line about how native travellers carry seeds to perform impromptu auguries or checks on whether or not to continue on their current path, the main sense it gives me is that for Caulborn and those Lunars who have been around for a while, it\u2019s mostly a matter of not skipping a beat whenever your path <i>does</i> suddenly change, and maybe even being able to navigate those shifts purposefully, rather than never experiencing them.<br /> <br /> And finally a passage covering the subject of the local spirits and fae, which I\u2019ve been especially looking forward to because I want to see me some of that Pearl Court! Judge Nehemeth was already pretty great, but combine her with this current rendering of the nobility and she\u2019ll be unstoppable!<br /> <br /> Huh, so the Caul\u2019s depopulation during its disappearance had implications for spirits as well; I hadn\u2019t considered that, although it makes sense. So with a land that needed to start over from scratch, you\u2019ve got a lot of elementals that sprung anew at an unusual rate from its substances, and very little actual activity from gods owing to things like the Lunars being untrustworthy of basically any that might be assigned there by Yu-Shan, not even about fearing them being actual spies, just that there basic reporting might give things away to the enemy. Plus an inability to form coherent divine policy for the place; it\u2019s own inherent fluidity must be bad enough to try and formulate its destiny, let alone being home to such a concentration of hostile Celestial Exalted. The lack of divine supervision probably doesn\u2019t really make a contribution to the nature of the Caul, but I imagine that it makes planning some things around it more difficult; mind, it makes me wonder if a few of the rituals undertaken by the Caulborn might substitute for some cosmological contributions that local gods might have been able to make.<br /> <br /> And for the fae\u2026 they\u2019re referred to in very basic terms as coming out of the odd bordermarch that springs up in the wilderness, there\u2019s no mention of Judge Nehemeth, and the description of how no really powerful raksha are known to operate kind of precludes her presence. Darn. Still, at least we get notes on how the land is unusually resistant to shadowlands, and a favourite for demons to slip into, being free from Hell but getting to enjoy it lacking some of Creation proper\u2019s oppressive presence.<br /> <br /> Last is a point on how the spirits relate to the local Exalted. The Dragon Blooded are said to have a harder time with them, as their wilder character makes Immaculate intimidation tactics less effective and they\u2019re more likely to get into a fight, something that has to be a big shock to a lot of younger Dynasts who ever journeyed there for pilgrimage or military service. Their characteristics end up favouring the Lunar mythology (having, as it does, so many methods for appealing to a spirit\u2019s idiosyncrasies, as well as a common enemy in the Immaculate Philosophy). There\u2019s a final note on how the spheres of spirits who\u2019ll ally with nobody becomes even more hostile, but I couldn\u2019t say if that implies anything about their function in the call, or merely supplementing its inherent nature with their own snares.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And so that\u2019s the long-cultivated hype of the Caul finally given a complete picture. I take away two major things from it: First, that I think it effectively retains a lot of the implications that one might have wanted from the very old \u201chome to a Lunar alternative to the Loom of Fate\u201d thing, while dropping a lot of the baggage that may have held, and giving it a background a bit closer to the Lunars. The other is that I really like the approach to the Caulborn, because it looks at the pulp fiction premise of a strange and isolated land being inhabited by freaky natives, takes the basic essence and aesthetic quality of it, and does its very best job of shaking loose all of the ugly implications from the likes of colonialism and orientalism and the like, and doing a lot to replace it with some basic humanity. And really, they\u2019re an especially good fit for the Caul, as they help prevent its oddities from becoming too ungrounded by giving them a human face with which to interact and process any experiences that might come up. In the end, I think the Caul came off very well, a special little gem in the setting from which one could present a very different kind of story. <br /> <br /> I had intended to roll the mini Dominion entries up in this post as well, but writing on the Caul took longer than I expected (I\u2019m already in excess of 4000 words), and there are another three pages of them, so I\u2019ll save them for a little bit later. For now, it\u2019s just the next Artifact.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Our first level 5 Artifact since Arms of the Chosen sees the return of Death at the Root. This grimcleaver was always a source of frustration to me, something that seemed to have an intriguing backstory and premise, let down by the underwhelming manner in which Artifacts, especially the 5-dot ones, were realised before. Well, Evocations have provided a much more impressive framework for realising the powers of such weapons, so I have high hopes that it will finally be able to live up to its name.<br /> <br /> Note: I recall finding the backstory to Death at the Root to be something with potential; I cannot recall specifically what the backstory was.<br /> <br /> Herein, the origin of Death at the Root is given as the Lunar geomancer and architect Shu Ri-Li having been so enraged that the fruits of his life\u2019s labours was being appropriated by the treacherous Dragon Blooded that he destroyed the instrument of his artifice and refashioned it with a huge blade of moonsilver. Hence he was in possession of a weapon capable of denying and sabotaging his new enemies by being able to rend the very dragon lines to power his former works and tear down irreplaceable infrastructure. To fit a story of that scope, this weapon is given a particularly impressive collection of deeds, such as sabotaging the water purification manses of the River of Tears, striking a war manse to temporarily disable the Realm Defence Grid (a level of interaction with it that no prior Edition attributed to anybody), and being used by the Lunar who saved it from the Realm\u2019s grasp to break down the wards of its own storehouse. Now that I\u2019m sufficiently hyped up for it, let\u2019s dive in.<br /> <br /> From the outset, the grimcleaver gets the significant power to shatter hearthstones without needing the usual skills or examination for weak spots, which sounds useful for just tearing through any stockpiles of particularly precious gems. And then we get its most basic, freely available power, by which its strike is capable of completely shutting down a manse or demesne\u2019s supernatural properties for the length of a scene; even that can probably be very useful for cases in which an enemy assumed they could rely upon the enhancements or safety provided by those powers, and suddenly finds themselves standing alone. The holy site may come back online at the scene\u2019s end, but they might be around anymore to benefit from it.<br /> <br /> The next Evocation, Welling Sap Strike, gives Death at the Root utility even when not around manses, being able to take in spilt Essence from even decisively attacked enemies to give itself an aura for the scene adding to its raw damage and Overwhelming values for withering attacks, which is super cheap and can be stacked repeatedly. It\u2019s still something that wants to be used in supernatural sites, as severing them also provides some of that bonus, and the Merit rating of them can up the limit of how many times the benefit can be stacked. One more Evocation after that lets it Instantly add that bonus to decisive attacks as well.<br /> <br /> Dragon\u2019s Egg Cracked at Essence 2 provides a greater benefit to the more basic powers of the axe by letting it basically tie them together; shattering a hearthstone on an enemy\u2019s person with a gambit to instantly sever their manse/demesne attunement, or suppressing the magic site to provide a bonus to the attack on hearthstones that came from it. It also starts becoming capable of attacking sorcery, as attacks on a sorcerer strip away motes they\u2019ve been gathering, or the Lunar Charm Spell-Rending Talon gets to be used for three as the target magic gets chopped up.<br /> Cutting the World-Root at Essence 3 enhances the first power to extend its suppression of magical sites to a matter of days, which can cause a kind of shockwave effect for Resonant wielders whose attack rolls high enough, as any of the manse\u2019s linked hearthstones are shattered from afar. The power World-Soul Harvest can be used to absorb some of the power of a place supressed in that fashion, equipping the grimcleaver with a simulacrum of its hearthstone.<br /> <br /> Essence 4\u2019s Broken Circle Ruin allows for the site-breaking powers to be used on sorcerous workings as well, by default ruining Terrestrial ones permanently while suppressing Celestial and Solar ones as though they were demesnes or manses, unless it was a working generated with a manse as one of the Means, in which case even the Celestial Circle can be undone, and even the Solar is suppressed for at least four months. The difficulty that has to be overcome can be fairly high though; disrupting Solar workings requires a whopping <i>nine</i> successes.<br /> <br /> And Death at the Root\u2019s ultimate power, Manse-Razing Strike. By means of that Charm, a manse or demesne that has been suppressed (and any defenders probably dealt with by the gathered bled energies) can be taken apart with an extended action over the course of days, as the wielder carves its geomancy to pieces. I\u2019ve not commented much on theme or implications of powers yet because I\u2019ve just been soaking in the intricacies, but this one is comparatively simple, so it leaves a lot of room to imagine. Imagine a Lunar thoughtfully going around the chambers of a manse, every now and then stopping to swing the gigantic axe head into some of its features and structures. Perhaps as the process goes on, it becomes more and more furious, rougher, indelicate, until in the end you\u2019ve got something like a hearthstone room so heavily gouged it was as though a mighty clawed animal dug into it like clay. It was probably a particularly impressive sight whenever Shu Ri-Li deconstructed his own works. On the mechanical side, one thing I\u2019ll note is that failure is given a consequence of immunising the site to the power, but no terminus is listed for the extended action. I\u2019m wondering if that\u2019s an oversight, or the intention is that one can continue rolling towards success indefinitely, but you blow it for good if you ever drop the mote commitment.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Yes, I think this rendering of the Artifact does very well. What I like in particular are the qualities to help it function more as a weapon with some flexibility to its application, while still being one that is brought mostly against mystical places and constructions. I also like how, for all its power, it\u2019s ultimately a very understated Artifact; the manse or demesne destruction isn\u2019t in something overt and flashy, it\u2019s more like being a surgeon with a very big scalpel. Death at the Root meets with my approval.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T11:47:41-07:00","id":1295884,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1295872#post1295872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I've not been describing reactions to this book based on guesswork.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't understand why a question such as &quot;is it different&quot; needs to be interpreted.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure others understand why you seem to be so upset by someone posting a link to try to be helpful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25050-fodor","name":"FODOR","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T12:08:45-07:00","id":1295891,"title":"","content":"I experience it as not really having been listening to, and talking down to me, things that are highly provocative from my personal history even when I'm not a bit tense from the overall weight of things.<br /> <br /> A weight that was not really helped by an original query amounting to &quot;hey, I know you've repeatedly said that you're experiencing pressure from how much there is to get through, but how would you like <i>even more</i> to get through?&quot;, which wasn't exactly a good read of the room.<br /> <br /> To which my response was an inquiry to try and determine whether or not it was something that had been seen already, so that I could know whether or not refusing to burden myself further with it would amount to missing out on anything.<br /> <br /> Which gets interpreted as me asking for even more to be dropped in on me. As well as not knowing how to find it myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T12:22:02-07:00","id":1295897,"title":"","content":"I think you might be reading some negativity and ill intent into an innocent attempt at a small kindness in honesty, obviously it came in a form that didn't come across well to you but as an outsider it certainly doesn't seem intentional.<br /> <br /> Also I'm not sure feeling all that burden for a hobby is healthy, man. If you feel under pressure to do it and it's harming your fun I'm sure people wouldn't get upset about things being read at a slower pace, or not at all if you so chose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25050-fodor","name":"FODOR","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T16:59:43-07:00","id":1295978,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I can't say I actually care if you &quot;keep pace&quot;, Isator. This ain't your job.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2334-aliasi","name":"Aliasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2334_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-11T16:59:46-07:00","id":1295979,"title":"","content":"Okay, very end of the Dominions chapter. It\u2019s been a good one, so I don\u2019t need very much from these little ones at the end. Looking at how short they are\u2026 would probably only need two or three lines to convey thoughts on each of them, unless any have concepts that <i>really</i> send me on a cascading train of thought.<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay then, Black Winter Boneyard first; shadowland home of the necromancer Smiling Rat, who maintains security with a private army of zombies, skeletal constructs and phantasmal monsters while he develops his necromancy, which he\u2019s happy to teach to just about anybody. Sounds like a good source for a mentor in that magic for people who wouldn\u2019t want to deal directly with the Underworld. Also good to refer a bit to a Lunar who is slightly on the outs with the Silver Pact.<br /> <br /> The Shattersea Bastion, a series of ruined Shogunate forts situated across some rocks of the White Sea\u2019s shore, home to Shadow-Rending Razor and his cult of moon-worshipping assassins. Curious to see a reference to one of those old Luna aspects when the rest of the write-ups have divested them, but at this point it doesn\u2019t really come across as a highly prominent thing, just one of the personas that would appeal to this group in particular. Glad to see another reference to a court of lesser moon-gods and the idea of them providing blessings to enhance the mortals, and the description of the unknowing nearby fishing village gives me a certain gothic feel to the whole thing.<br /> <br /> New Dakuo is a city under the control of the Shadow-Fang Vanguard, and their\u2026 allies. Well, the idea that they would form a covenant with a Shogunate army for the sake of protecting a mortal populace is sufficient to make me greatly revise my opinions of the Vanguard, and maybe a bit of why they\u2019re shunned by their fellows. I wonder whether it would be better to play the leading Dragon Blood trying to stonewall his Lunar counterpart\u2019s successor as an attempt to consolidate power, or because the new guy needs to earn respect.<br /> <br /> The Eye of the Killing Storm is a sacred city of a group of Southeastern steppe nomads who worship a Lunar. Blood Nail, that name sounds familiar from earlier on, but I can\u2019t quite place it\u2026 anyway, nice to see Prasad\u2019s expansion causing more people trouble, and that the Pure Way still cares enough to try and deal with Lunars and their worshippers.<br /> <br /> Mount Namas, that was definitely mentioned some time before- ah, of course, it\u2019s the Dominion of the Seven Fang Vanguard\u2019s leaders, an ancient mountain that has been a site for war and death spirit meetings since time immemorial. I like the picture of Seven Obsidian Leopard as being so out of place in the modern Age that it\u2019s better to spend all the time among violent gods and ancient ghosts.<br /> <br /> Luz Liura is a Wyld zone located in the middle of the Southern desert, creating a private little patch of green for Klesamra Lotus-Seed, her hermit crabfolk followers and the fae she\u2019s made common cause with. I get a tickle from this one playing off of old ideas about how Lunars relate to the Wyld and its inhabitants, even if many in the Silver Pact are supposed to think that it\u2019s crossing a line to toss raksha against the Realm. I\u2019m also all for Fair Folk noble names.<br /> <br /> Fortress of the Fulgurite Spire is a more isolated class of Western dominion, which makes it fitting as the home for that weirdo Ul the Burning Eye. I\u2019m not sure whether his human experimentation with diseases on his own population is necessarily all right just because he has a lot of magic in place to ensure no casualties\u2026 I suppose it wouldn\u2019t go too much farther to presume that he secured the consent of the people before doing so. I think the description of the fort\u2019s self-removal defences is pretty cool, and I like imagining the Realm trying to launch lightning assaults on it before it can get away.<br /> <br /> And finally the Star Jasmine Pavilion, the luxurious estate of the hedonistic Wings of Ivory, who really managed to make the most of a deed name fitting his outlook for his tern spirit shape; it gives me this comical images of some of his ternfolk attendants relaying reports on those sea birds being annoying vermin, and he firmly insists that they\u2019re majestic and beautiful creatures. His manner is a bit similar to Leviathan\u2019s, but I\u2019d say he\u2019s sufficiently different in his motives for keeping to himself, and more likely to be angry at Realm expansion because of how it infringes upon his lifestyle rather than any outrage concerning their history or excesses.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> And that\u2019s it, that\u2019s the Dominions chapter. There\u2019s been some really great stuff in here; it recalls to me the best of Masters of Jade, wherein the subject matter (in this case how Lunars relate to and occasionally use mortal populations for conflict with or refuge from the Realm) is used as an opportunity to reveal more of the setting to us, with creative portraits and focused write-ups. But it even surpasses some of that to me, with the depth of human character to the societies, the variety of their formats and how they\u2019re written, and the multi-faceted conflicts that can be found all across Creation. It\u2019s a wonderful chapter, even for those who wouldn\u2019t be interested in the agendas of the Silver Pact itself, and it\u2019s been my pleasure to read it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Last Artifact now, Weirdflame, and I\u2019m truly intrigued to see what might come in the twin firsts of an Artifact flame piece that is both moonsilver and 5-dots.<br /> <br /> <br /> Hmm, interesting backstory; the idea of going out to the Wyld to set up a workshop, using the local life forms as raw materials, and when the thing finally falls apart under the strains of chaos converting it into a weapon, it sounds very reminiscent of some old points about Lunars having a preference for manufacturing tools that can be out of reach and deconstructed behind them. The manner in which the weapon is described as having been used sounds like a real horror show, although its physical design sounds quite lovely.<br /> <br /> Huh, Spark of Madness sounds like a more direct intrusion into the wielder\u2019s personality than most 5-dot Artifacts, but I guess that\u2019s what you get for harnessing raw Wyld. So yeah, damage your own mental health for a day in order to stoke its internal flames sufficient to shoot continuously and without firedust. Oh, and it already gets into violent warping\u2019s of targets, being able to make a not very hard gambit to do things like twist a person into a terrifying monstrosity, twist their limbs and body parts into non-functional, malignant forms, or burn away all of their distinguishing features, with resonant wielders getting the option to actually burn them as they do it. Some of these sound like they\u2019d actually be a good model for the powers of other beings, such as Erembour\u2019s horn. And Lunars can go and focus their natural transformative powers, good and bad, through the piece getting, to do so at range and without needing to share their blood.<br /> <br /> Ah jeez, with a later Evocation, the wielder can get a powerful improvement to attempts to emotionally hurt people that they\u2019ve twisted with the weapon\u2019s flame, to the point of being able to drive them hysterical about the state that they\u2019re in. Huh, I hadn\u2019t actually looked at the duration of the transformation power, but\u2026 I\u2019m guessing there\u2019ll be a later Evocation to make them longer than a day, if it\u2019s possible for the inflicted Hysteria to be matched to the alteration to last longer than a few days.<br /> <br /> Twisting Ash Brand has a nice description of how it achieves its effect, that idea of spraying a cloud of multi-coloured smoke, it sounds very showy and ominous, looks good as a basis for setting them up to be violently transformed with the reduced gambit difficulty. Ah, and here\u2019s the one for extending the transformations; really love the imagery of a perversion of the phoenix symbolism, and I don\u2019t think the basis for making the afflictions ongoing is all that harsh, not for what you\u2019d be doing to them (and if you ever want the motes back without withdrawing the curse, you can always go put them out of their misery).<br /> <br /> Come to think of it\u2026 ah yes, here\u2019s where we get the good stuff that can arise from not tying all mutations, good and bad, under the same subsystem, so that even the bad ones feel like they need to do something useful.<br /> <br /> And the last of its powers written (although at Essence 4, probably not the furthest it can go), Wyld Nightmare Inferno; create a delightfully large conflagration of mutating flame, that weakens the integrity of people\u2019s bodies against the other transformative attacks, and can even inflict such corruption itself if it damages them enough. I especially like the point about the wielder being immune to it while under the influence of the first Evocation, that image of an individual striding through rainbow streaked flame with a mad glint in their eye.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Weirdflame was a good one to end on, a nice method for Lunar\u2019s to harness power from the Wyld, another example of a rather fearsome and appalling weapon, and a bit of an inspiration as moonsilver devil casters go. I\u2019m imagining a few more of them already; one involves shooting lightless spectral flames from stealth, being able to create zones of darkness by drawing nearby light sources to feed the weapon, and culminating in being able to build up a big charge that gets fired off at an opponent, which in addition to burning them, envelops them in darkness. I\u2019ve got a whole strix stealth hunter theme in mind for it, it would be distinct from soulsteel.<br /> <br /> <br /> Not much to say about the Artifacts in summary, other than they continue to do a good job of fulfilling the promise of Evocations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 03-11-2019, 08:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T00:00:50-07:00","id":1296060,"title":"","content":"Blood Nail was the berzerker whose friend made spiky armour around them, that's probably where you remember them from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T04:02:19-07:00","id":1296089,"title":"","content":"Okay, I'm up a lot earlier than I have been on other days of going over Charms, and I've lost the last couple of days to certain other engagements so I finished the Dominions and Artifacts a lot later than I intended, so there's only one thing for it: All of Stamina and half of Strength today, the rest plus the characters chapter tomorrow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T07:41:45-07:00","id":1296131,"title":"","content":"Looking back, I think that Second Edition Stamina was one of the more solid Attributes, with a decent combination of mechanical rigour and a few unique elements, so I\u2019ll be very interested to see how that\u2019s built upon in this new milieu.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> For its Heart\u2019s Blood, there is Hero\u2019s Heart Challenge, finally giving Lunars a means of acquiring human shapes through contests of physical skill. I find the fact that it requires not just victory (which is probably not too hard in most cases), but accepting a Major Tie of respect to effectively prevent it from being a go-to form for the sacred hunt, even within the range of targets that probably wouldn\u2019t acquiesce to a physical contest in the first place. It means that the shapes you acquire can be something that can format your characterisation, and possibly even create impulses and vulnerabilities that might not be wanted, and thus precludes taking on that particular shape; conversely, it might create a book door to some story hooks, as a player\u2019s character forms a significant investment in some random guard. And for the player who wants to get a bit deeper into the roleplaying side of it, they can always reserve their judgement for whether or not to form the Tie for the other character\u2019s actual performance, trying to genuinely assess in the course of playing through the contest whether their character would find the target respectable.<br /> <br /> <br /> That taken care of, we start Stamina proper off with the Defence Charms, beginning with Impenetrable Beast-Armour, which seems rather decent to me as a starter with the capacity to gain at least 3 extra soak or their Stamina in Hardness, even if it is only against a single attack (and even for that, I do enjoy the image of a Lunar\u2019s skin functioning as a kind of reactive armour). Like the Dexterity Charms, I appreciate how it suggests a number of formats for the armouring to take, so that people don\u2019t need to have their cosmetics dictated to them.<br /> <br /> Speaking of Dexterity, Durant Quicksilver Adaptation seems to return some of its spirit of mild cheating, with a permanent upgrade to the Stamina Excellency to allow it to be applied to enhance soak when an attack has already successfully hit, so that they don\u2019t need to commit to knocking the withering damage down a few pegs until they know they\u2019ll need it. And there\u2019s an interchange between the two Attributes as Steel Paw Style permits Stamina to be used for Parry calculation as a permanent upgrade, albeit with the exclusion of light weapons. I can\u2019t imagine that\u2019s a balance issue, so I\u2019ve got to admire their commitment to maintaining the imagery behind the change, as the deflection style of a knife or rapier would be the one out of all of them that wouldn\u2019t fit to the modified form of meeting force with force.<br /> <br /> Den Warden Method provides an unconventional enhancement to defend other, as a Lunar whose Parry is overcome becomes able to provide their own soak and Hardness (including magical bonuses of the time) as a substitute for that of their ward against the attack intended for them. Mind you, it\u2019s still an attack that will roll damage against the protected target, so it probably can\u2019t be the Lunar literally imposing their body before the attack; I\u2019d be inclined to view it as a sturdy Lunar in defence of another being able to extend that hardiness mystically through the principle of their defence (it kind of puts me in mind of some Earthbending stuff, that element of physicality imposed through attitude). It has a Protean benefit for shapes with what I believe is a new Merit, Immense Guardian\u2026 no, it\u2019s actually something that the core had particular to the yeddim (as far as I\u2019m willing to check back), which basically allows the animal to provide the effect of this Charm, so that it basically has an alternative effect of penalising the damage roll against the ward (which I\u2019m choosing to see as a matter of an attack that gets past something like a yeddim being forced to be somewhat clumsier).<br /> <br /> Unmoving Aurochs Defense\u2026 ah, this one wasn\u2019t previewed officially as such, but I recall playtesters mentioning the principle of it, the idea of a Lunar being able to prevent themselves from being moved by an external force by literally modifying their weight. Given how it can be applied at basically any point where the possibility of being knocked back is reasonably certain, it sounds like a pretty absolute defence against that tactic. I like how it says that the increase is literal enough to actually make the integrity of the Lunar\u2019s footing an issue, which apart from sometimes being a basis for good stunts (I\u2019m picturing a Lunar upon a tightrope who would sooner be heavy enough to make it snap than knocked off altogether, because at least with the former you can hold on and swing down), sounds like it could be leveraged to make the denial of knockback less of a concern, by choosing the right spot to make the defence into a liability.<br /> <br /> Into Essence 2 with Enduring Mammoth Bastion, which seems a rather intricate defence; the Lunar gets to compare only elements of an enemy\u2019s Initiative in their damage roll to Hardness to determine whether it can be attempted, ignoring bonuses to damage from other sources; even if it\u2019s a magical attack made without rolling Initiative, if the attacker\u2019s Initiative is lower, then that will be what is compared to the Hardness. It has an Initiative cost, but only if the Lunar is themselves armoured. I\u2019m not sure how viable it is with the order in which Charms can be activated, but it gives me a picture of a kind of attack arms race; the Lunar raises Hardness after an attack hits, the enemy supplements their damage with magic, and the Lunar gets to say that extra damage doesn\u2019t count and their Hardness wins. It feels like a decent amount of Stamina is also going to be about denial of opposing advantages.<br /> <br /> As an extension of my point about Lunar adaptive armour, Invulnerable Moonsilver Carapace has been one that I always liked, admittedly because I\u2019ve always pictured it as resembling Greed from Fullmetal Alchemist armouring himself in carbon nanotubes or whatever. I actually want to compare it directly to its prior form\u2026 right, so it\u2019s a much less immediately extreme Charm now, being lower Essence and not providing a huge soak boost that could <i>stack</i> with armour. But it also has its own benefits that I think bring it out ahead; it\u2019s scene long for a commitment of ten motes rather than costing six motes for each round that you use it, it still provides the benefits of heavy Artifact armour with the added benefit of soak that will hold up to the Lunar\u2019s Stamina in points against effects that would otherwise ignore it, and while it won\u2019t stack with pre-existing armour, the description of using the best of both their traits makes me think it\u2019s possible that when it\u2019s fused with a pre-existing one, it might alleviate the mobility penalty? It definitely lets you keep using the Evocations of your magical armour while possibly improving its traits <i>and</i> releasing the attunement cost so that the Lunar isn\u2019t burdened with committed motes. And to top it all off, the Charm can be repurchased in the form of Evocations for the extruded armour (which can potentially make them cheaper than the powers of true Artifacts), with themes from things like personality, spirit shape and Caste (and I\u2019d say those can be some fairly diverse themes). So\u2026 it\u2019s basically a Lunar version of Glorious Solar Plate now, but with a few differences (the limit based on soak ignoring effects being a big one, as well as being available at earlier Essence), and I think that between those and the potential offered for Evocations in spirit shapes, it ends up distinguishing itself admirably, and ultimately being a good realisation of the concept. To top it off, while a Simple Charm, it can be reflexively activated alongside Deadly Beastman Transformation.<br /> <br /> Moonsilver Thew Exertion allows the mass alteration concept to start being applied to grapples, as the Lunar gets to reroll failed dice in either resisting an enemy\u2019s control roll (representing becoming too heavy to shift) or in making their own (representing becoming a difficult press on the opponent), and also allowing resistance to be made at all against the grapple attempts of attackers with Legendary Size, even if it doesn\u2019t provide the power to make such attempts back at them; I\u2019m getting a fun picture of a tyrant lizard that tries to hoist a Lunar aloft, only to be caught off guard by how much heavier they are than they look. And Stone Rhino\u2019s Skin provides some more adaptive defence, as a successful hit or a hazard that is not evaded can be made to subtract 1s on its damage roll from its successes (although the Charm is made to cost more when exerted against decisive attacks). In the spirit of the attacker who is disheartened or unbalanced when they strike somebody that is a stone wall against them, the Charm also robs a bit of Initiative from an attacker if the result deals no damage (which can also be a potential sweetener if the success removal effect didn\u2019t play out).<br /> <br /> Frenzied Bear Fortification gets a start on a bit of that Lunar berserker quality that I\u2019m hoping to see in a big way later on, as they can make it that the value of their wound penalties becomes added to their soak and Hardness, the latter of which won\u2019t stack with armour or animal shapes, but will with other Charms; plus, being animals that are Berserkers themselves allow the wound penalty to be subtracted from the Overwhelming quality of withering attacks. Unyielding Silver Sentinel then goes and makes the protection against knockback even more comprehensive, by being able to pay 2 Initiative and commit 3 motes to allow that effect to persist for the length of a scene, so long as the Lunar is never crashed.<br /> <br /> Hmm, up to Essence 3 already. Quicksilver Aegis Embodiment continues the element of Lunar imperviousness, as invoking the Instant Charm makes soak straight up impossible to reduce, as well as allowing it to apply against normally unsoakable attacks (withering ones, not for how decisive attacks are unsoakable as a rule). Like some of the prior Charms, it has an Initiative cost that is waived if used while unarmoured, which also makes its Perilous keyword non-applicable.<br /> <br /> And we see a return of what I always found to be one of the more flavourful Charms, Weapon-Trapping Body Dominion. It makes use of some of the new mechanics, as it functions as a clash attack made with Stamina and Resistance, with half of Hardness in bonus dice, which can not only ward off the attack, but doubles up by disarming them as the weapon becomes lodged in the Lunar\u2019s body; pulling it out is possible, but takes either a disarm gambit, or a feat requiring Strength 5. Furthermore, the Charm can be effective even against enemies that attacked with their limbs or claws or the like, as the striker becomes caught in the Lunar\u2019s flesh in the form of an automatically successful grapple that rolls Stamina + Brawl, Martial Art or Resistance for its control roll, which also gets to benefit from double 9s. The drawback of all of this is that it entails a certain ready access for the Lunar\u2019s body, i.e. that it can\u2019t be used while wearing any armour that wasn\u2019t made from the Lunar themselves. Still, while I can\u2019t remember the older version of the Charm precisely, I think this one manages to be fairly sophisticated, making good use of new systems to give the process a bit more back and forth, and with a nice, freaky benefit for the event of being attacked by somebody using their fists.<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see\u2026 Steadfast Yeddim Meditation allows the Lunar to add their Hardness to the value of their soak against an attack if it\u2019s made by an enemy with lower Initiative, and can deny them the Initiative they get just for having landed the attack if their damage is reduced to its minimum, which also gets to be a bit cheaper if the Lunar is unarmoured. I want to reserve full judgement for that caveat until I see whether Solars got similar benefits, but I\u2019m really liking how it turns up so far.<br /> <br /> And Wounded But Never Down is looking like some kind of cross between a heightened defence and a healing power\u2026 when decisive damage is rolled, Lunar gets to spend motes to negate the damage applied up to a reasonably high limit, setting it aside as a special pool. It\u2019s not an Instant Charm, so the motes for holding off that damage become committed, and the negated damage heals at a not very slow rate of one level every two days, which can still be subjected to magic that accelerates or applies healing. It can only be used once per scene unless reset by incapacitating an enemy with an Initiative resetting attack, and can be stacked up to Essence times to keep dumping damage into the pool, which can otherwise last indefinitely. If the Lunar drops the commitment, damage remaining in the pool returns to their health track. And if they activate it while in a Berserker animal shape, their wound penalty can subtract from the Charm\u2019s hefty 5 Initiative cost. I\u2019ve often wondered at the idea of a power to provide a means of not exactly stopping or healing injury, but setting it aside for a while; I think that this one does an admirable job of it, particularly with the reset condition giving the sense that it\u2019s pushed by somewhere between a steadfast duty and a stubborn anger.<br /> <br /> Unstoppable Juggernaut Incarnation feels like a bit of Lunar emulation of their patron, at least as that deity was presented in Glories of the Most High (or maybe they had a Charm like this before, I don\u2019t know; I\u2019m pretty sure Luna did), as they get to invoke a defence against a decisive attack or environmental hazard that will ensure that it can\u2019t deal any more damage to them than would cross over wound penalty types. It\u2019s costly at ten motes, but against the right kind of attack it can probably be incredibly frustrating. It\u2019s also a bit of a gambit, as it has to be invoked before knowing if the attack would have inflicted that much damage in the first place, but relieves that burden a bit by such a failure not consuming its use limit; otherwise it can only be done once a day.<br /> <br /> Getting into Essence 4 now, with the delightfully names Indestructible God-Monster Incarnation. Invoked for a scene and including three levels of anima in the cost (which is referenced in the flavour text with the striking image of the anima peeling away with skin to present hints of an indestructible body), it provides a scene long enhancement of half of Essence added to soak and Hardness, improving the soak enhancement of the Stamina Excellency by allowing each mote spent to increase it by two (and also breaking the cap a little bit), and discounting a number of preceding Charms for similar effects by one mote. The drawback is that these benefits are reserved only for the unarmoured Lunar.<br /> <br /> Unflagging Predator Vitality lets the Lunar simply halve the damage provided by a withering attack, but the real benefit is in the special protection it provides to the Lunar\u2019s Initiative; under the auspices of the Charm, a withering attack won\u2019t be able to crash the Lunar, so long as the enemy inflicting it doesn\u2019t have more than the Lunar\u2019s current Willpower above them in Initiative, a protection that can be afforded to other means of an enemy stripping Initiative (although it won\u2019t work for the Lunar spending their own Initiative). I think there\u2019s a nice image to this one, of a certain relentlessness to a Lunar unless faced with an enemy of such a greater advantage that something in the Essence that defers to them. It can\u2019t be used with armour, but if in the shape of an animal with Impenetrable Armour, an enemy need to have even more Initiative (generally going to be nine or ten) to successfully crash them.<br /> <br /> Ahh, and now one I\u2019m very excited to see updated, Wound-Mastering Body Evolution. Heh, it\u2019s actually a more straightforward Charm than even several of the preceding ones; the Lunar who suffers a* decisive attack gets to incorporate the insight into the enemy\u2019s killing internet to give themselves twice their Essence in a bonus to their soak, isn\u2019t susceptible to Overwhelming damage, and a whopping <i>twenty</i> Hardness. It costs one Initiative every round that it\u2019s up to maintain for the scene, but so long as you keep the pressure on the enemies whose attacks will basically start bouncing off of you\u2026 Anyway, I think that it\u2019s a good choice to really streamline the concept of this Charm into a costly basis for vastly improved defence. The Second Edition concept of being able to immunise yourself a specific weapon or form of attack was\u2026 interesting, but <i>so</i> damn weird, and probably way too finickity to keep track of over time. No, better to focus it down to the essentials. <br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, Defence Charms look good overall, a nice sense of adaptive defences. Still, a more utilitarian category than a deeply flavourful one, so not much else to say about it.<br /> Wasn\u2019t too hard to get through that, but\u2026 keep breaking them up. I can make good time if I just don\u2019t take three hours before looking at any of the rest. Back in a tick!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T08:08:40-07:00","id":1296137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that Second Edition Stamina was one of the more solid Attributes, with a decent combination of mechanical rigour and a few unique elements</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely. Almost all the Full Moons I saw used a lot of Stamina charms, because it was effective, but it was also interesting. Not just soak and regen, but poison and disease, wilderness survival, etc.<br /> <br /> Anyway, on the 3rd ed Defence charms, one thing I liked is that there were a few defence charms that are either cheaper when unarmoured, or only work when unarmoured (though I like the first more than the second). That's something I noted in the Dragonblood book as well. It's good balance, and encourages you to fight unarmoured, which is generally a rather suboptimal choice. So it ends up being a more stylistic choice. Wearing armour is probably still marginally better, but not so much that an unarmoured Stamina-focused Lunar is a terrible choice.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T10:34:41-07:00","id":1296176,"title":"","content":"I think you're miscounting the Initiative required to crash the Lunar in an Impervious Hide Form with Vitality.<br /> <br /> The calculation is (Temp WP. + Essence +5), so the minimum in an Impervious Hide form is 9 just for its Essence 4 Minimun. It is more likely to be around 14 (Temp WP 5) at the lower end, I think.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">https://swordofcreation.net/</a> : A secondary forum focusing on archiving homebrew, both mechanical and fluff, and receiving critique on it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/108-flare","name":"Flare","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar108_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T12:16:23-07:00","id":1296197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296137#post1296137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wearing armour is probably still marginally better, but not so much that an unarmoured Stamina-focused Lunar is a terrible choice.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is also a great boon for characters in animal forms, which I like. Makes fighters that prefer to take on beast forms some good options to make up for some animal\u2019s poor soak &amp; hardness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2302-snakesandsuns","name":"Snakesandsuns","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2302_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"224"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T13:47:38-07:00","id":1296228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flare</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296176#post1296176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're miscounting the Initiative required to crash the Lunar in an Impervious Hide Form with Vitality.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I was only referring to the additional Initiative required by the Protean effect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T13:50:01-07:00","id":1296229,"title":"","content":"Next up with Endurance Charms, which I warrant will cover a somewhat wider range than dealing with fatigue and injury and the like.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Of course it starts out with Ox-Body Technique, which I\u2019m going to go ahead and compare directly to the Solar version. The basic principle is the same, a Charm that can be purchased repeatedly to grant extra health levels, with the exact quantity per purchase based on Stamina. The difference is that the levels the Lunar acquires all have deeper wound penalties; for instance, two -2 levels for Lunars compared to a -1/-2 for Solars. The only part where the straight numbers are a bit preferable are where the Lunar is at Stamina 5, getting four health levels to a Solar\u2019s three (although it still has no 0 health levels and includes a -4). Hhhhowever, I think this is offset a bit by the surroundings; a Lunar has more incentive to and benefit from buying up their Stamina in the first place, so it\u2019s a matter of them probably getting the best version in more cases, which will do double duty of allowing them to maximize the purchases (compared to the Solar version, which still has the cap on how many you can get based on Resistance). Furthermore, Lunars are dotted with powers based on turning wound penalties into strengths, so in some respects getting a few more -4 levels is actually a boon, as you\u2019ll get to maximize those benefits without dancing <i>too</i> close to incapacitation. So you\u2019ve got the mechanical underpinnings, from which one can build a certain notion that Lunar Stamina is focused on leaning into the pain.<br /> <br /> Ah good, there\u2019s one of those hibernation Charms, Bear Sleep Technique; put oneself into a mode of stasis, in which rate of healing is increased by Essence + Stamina (so tripled at the very lowest), as well as needing no food and water. It\u2019s an Indefinite Charm, but apparently can\u2019t be ended voluntarily, requiring the Lunar to stick it out either until fully healed or after a pre-determined length of time has passed. Being aware of surrounding phenomena (which can allow one to wake early) requires a roll made at a steep penalty which is said to only be capable of being enhanced by Excellencies, although I can recall that somewhere back up there was at least one Charm that wasn\u2019t restricted in that manner, and it\u2019s not necessary to roll if it\u2019s somebody messing around with the Lunar physically, so it\u2019s always possible to rouse a Lunar from that state with a hard smack across the face. Last of all, it only takes being at Stamina 5, while Essence remains at 1, for that hibernating state to not qualify towards one\u2019s aging. Considering the conditions they operate in, this seems like a good solid Charm for a lot of Lunars to go to ground for a while and come back out, fresh as a daisy.<br /> <br /> Ah, almost forgotten about self-healing with Halting the Scarlet Flow. It\u2019s more of a dedicated combat regeneration now, and is prevented from rendering hibernation redundant, by not being possible to use outside of combat. Still, it might be a bit less finickity than it used to be, with an invocation and flat payment of six motes keeping it going for turns equal to Stamina (so long as they\u2019re not crashed early), during each of which a non-aggravated wound will heal so long. Use has to be chosen carefully though, since it can only be used once per day, and even then won\u2019t reset if the Lunar is still injured at the start of the next day, or didn\u2019t get proper sleep (so that\u2019s me right out). I find that the regeneration might have originally been there primarily as another thing to reference the Gauru, and the combat dynamics of Exalted have always complicated it, so this level of specificity seems fitting to me. On the other hand, it\u2019s still only Essence 1, so I wonder if later Charms might end up expanding upon it.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, they were always supposed to have a special facility for recovering from crippling injuries, and that gets a look with Maimed But Unbroken. The Lunar gets to alleviate the penalties imposed by such injuries, or other crippling effects like the Charm Joint-Wounding Attack, and as time passes (four to two rounds in combat, or per scene out of it) the penalty will be reduced by another point as the injury rapidly heals, being dispelled completely once the penalty is at zero. It is, however, still too early to deal with crippling in the form of limbs or organs being removed or destroyed completely, and is also ineffectual against any crippling effects that don\u2019t entail penalties.<br /> <br /> Oh, the regeneration already gets a boost just for getting to Stamina 4 with Undying Battle Vigor, as they get the Permanent benefit of extending the Charm\u2019s length by one turn each for either dealing at least three points of damage higher than what would be necessary to incapacitate, or by waving the benefit of Initiative Break. It doesn\u2019t strictly say that those conditions need to be achieved while Halting the Scarlet Flow is already been in effect, but I feel as though that\u2019s the spirit of the thing.<br /> <br /> It rolls back to the bottom again with Rugged Beast Endurance, a day-long Charm by which a Lunar is able to adapt to their current environment; while under its effect, they get a temporary specialty in each Survival and Resistance to cope with their current climate, as well as getting to ignore penalties to rolls that the specialties would benefit. It needs to be invoked again if there\u2019s ever a reason to want to change one\u2019s adaptation, but doing so while the first use is active means that it gets to forego its Willpower cost. A repurchase at Essence 3 and Stamina 4 (quite a step up for one initially with 1s in both) allows its protection to also half any damage from an adapted climate\u2019s environmental hazards, which extends to similar effects produced by things like magic. And when in an animal shape native to the environment, it also provides a mote discount to any Charms enhancing the benefited Survival or Resistance rolls. I could see this evolving into the old Directional Mastery Charm, but for the moment I have a preference for this Charm as it is with the more versatile application and less absolute process.<br /> <br /> Hehehe, I was wondering if we\u2019d actually be getting a Charm for effectively disposing of forty cakes, and here it is with All-Consuming Crucible, an all around power for enhancing the act of eating; in addition to having no limit to the amount of food one can put down (as well as being able to do so with unusual rapidity when under duress, using a Stamina + Athletics or Survival roll), it becomes possible to safely live off of any source of mundane organic material, and even adds non-Charm dice to rolls to resist a consumed poison. Once they get to Stamina 5, the Lunar can eat basically anything (even if they sometimes need other means to break it down into bite-sized pieces), and mundane eaten or drank poison becomes worthless. It\u2019s a small thing, but I do love something in the idea of a character who can eat what they please in whatever quantity, more than usual with some of my recent media consumption, and the idea of getting to make a roll to very rapidly dispose of a large mass is just the cherry on top. I\u2019ll tell you, I\u2019ve got a wolverine-totem Lunar with this Charm\u2019s name on her.<br /> <br /> Starts at the bottom again with Disease-Purging Essence, a very simple Charm to allow some failed dice on rolls against virulence or morbidity to be rerolled. Having secured their own disease resistance, the Lunar can then mitigate that of others with Rabid Beast Strike, through which a decisive attack with hand, foot, claw or horn hits an enemy with an enhanced form of wound infection, in which the Lunar\u2019s Stamina gets to serve as its morbidity and virulence values if higher than the default, and it\u2019s also effective against the Exalted, supernatural beings or anybody else who heals in that manner. The resulting disease even gets a chance to start right at Major intensity if the attack is made on an enemy that was crashed, and the Charm affords a means of transmitting any other disease the Lunar might be a carrier of, albeit without the enhanced numbers. I\u2019m wondering if there\u2019s too much chicanery to the idea of turning into some well-known animal reservoir like a reptile, assuming that its carrying salmonella, and using that as a basis to infect people with typhoid\u2026<br /> <br /> False Death Technique is\u2026 basically what it sounds like. You get to make a roll using Stamina or Appearance, with double 9s and non-Charm dice equal to wound penalties, to pass off as dead. Magical observation might be capable of seeing through it, but would need to beat the Lunar\u2019s successes on the original roll, and while actually doing anything not dead will typically give the game away, it\u2019s suggested that there are occasions where the Lunar can bluff people into thinking them undead. It even gets to reflexively and mutely invoke Bear Sleep Technique for the Lunar who wants to make use of the immobility, and a spirit shape of an animal to plays possum provides the option of taking it under Awareness.<br /> <br /> Scorpion-and-Toad Absolution is the foundation of one of the old signature tricks, this time rendered as allowing persistent rerolling of 6s on rolls to resist poisons, being able to get the 5s as well if it\u2019s something they\u2019ve survived before, or something derived from an animal that the Lunar can shift into. It\u2019s a Totemic Charm for Wits, which anybody should be able to guess the animal type to allow. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> After imagining the range of Lunars who might congregate in Luthe, I was wondering about the assets that might be afforded to allowing them down there, and it comes in Shifting Breath Inversion, an Indefinite Charm with no cost whatsoever, to allow a Lunar to adapt to be capable of breathing in any environment and be immune to any complications from environmental pressure (or lack thereof). I\u2019d note that, while the focus is on aquatic environments, this would also be really helpful for survival at extremely high elevations. Heh, it took me a moment to understand what the Charm was getting at with all of the talk of aquatic animals needing extra help to function out of water, before I realised that the point was to allow somebody to do something like being a shark that could function on land, which\u2026 has implications\u2026 a Dynast who things they\u2019re safe from an Armoured Terror off the coast, before it comes crawling up onto shore on creepy mutant boar god limbs from Princess Mononoke. That\u2019s niiiice. To top it off, they can get extra successes to rolls against poisons that could only be inhaled, or against any inhaled poison while in an amphibious shape. Lunars with water breathing spirit shapes can learn it with Wits. Normally I might be a bit against more absolute effects, but when it\u2019s something for the sake of expanding the range of environments they can function in (and bring their animal shapes to), and considering that they\u2019re Lunars\u2026 I kind of feel exactly the opposite way about this one. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> And the prior Charm gets a nice little crossover with the eating one in the power of Behemoth\u2019s Inhalation Prana; supernaturally powerful lungs allow a Lunar to breathe in at extreme pressure, drawing in vapours and gases apart from air within medium range, even if they\u2019re magical, to concentrate them in the Lunar\u2019s person (although they still have to deal with any poisons involved). I like some of the described alternative applications of this, such as clearing out an obscuring fog, or clearing a burning building of dangerous smoke. The Lunar at Essence 2 who manages to fully overcome the toxicity or other danger of any such inhalation will get the option to breath it back out as an unblockable decisive attack that uses Stamina at an enemy in medium range, to try and expose them to its danger. And if in the form of an animal of Legendary Size, the increased capacity allows it to be drawn in from out to long range. I think this is Stamina starting to touch off of something more weird, and I am loving it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Adder Fang Method was previewed to us in the introduction of Totemic Charms, a power to allow one to generate their own toxins and venoms, either a default one that can only inflict bashing damage when it\u2019s burned through Initiative, or those of any possessed animal shapes, which can even be applied to a weapon or projectile with a good stunt. I\u2019m not quite sure how to read the Protean effect; it says that one in the right animal shape, one can \u201cinstead increase a dose\u2019s duration by (Essence)\u201d, and I\u2019m wondering instead of <u>what</u>? Is it saying that the poison\u2019s Stamina duration can be increased by that amount? Substituted for it? I think that language needs to be cleaned up a bit.<br /> <br /> Ah, Blood-and-Tear Elixir Cultivation, this is the one I was missing back in Intelligence, that one to manufacture medicines from one\u2019s own body. A nice bit of oddity in how a Lunar might function as a physician and promulgator of cures, even if they\u2019re at their best when the Lunar of their Solar mate is using them (to reroll 1s on a Medicine rolls) and I particularly like the premise of getting a more effective substance to treat a malady the Lunar has personally suffered, being a kind of reservoir for acquired immunities. Perhaps I expected it in Intelligence partially because that\u2019s what the Totemic effect allows for it.<br /> <br /> Oh my, that was rather a lot of Essence 1 Charms, but it\u2019s finally moved up from that, with Mamba-and-Cobra Mastery, to provide a significant enhancement to the Lunar\u2019s own venom, at the cost of two Initiative, getting a default of making the poison last one extra round and makes it corrosive enough to inflict aggravated damage, and then being able to continue extending the duration for every 10 rolled on the hit <i>and</i> damage rolls. If they repurchase at Essence 3, they have the option for those 10s to instead increase the poison\u2019s damage or the penalty it imposes, or mix and match the three parameters up to a maximum of five bonuses, as well as producing a poison that can never have its duration reduced below one. It would seem to me that Lunars don\u2019t need to wait very long before they can develop the power to make somebody die in a particularly unpleasant manner, just Essence 2 and three Charm purchases.<br /> <br /> Plague Rat Prana follows a similar principle in its enhancement of the power to transmit contagion through attacks, as a Lunar becomes capable of retaining illnesses that they\u2019ve managed to fight off within themselves as an asymptomatic carrier, allowing them to be generally contagious or to transmit them deliberately through attacks. Their control is fine enough to allow them to subtract their own Essence from the virulence of the disease to a minimum of 1, for the sake of not being too much of a danger through exposure to allies or bystanders who are simply standing too close. And then Pestilential Venom Fang allows them to pay a mote to improve the disease within them by adding +2 to morbidity and virulence, also making it capable of subtracting 1s from successes for rolls to resist (it much be making a particularly clever and adaptive pathogen, by exposure to the Lunar\u2019s blood and example). And Friend of the Plague then allows them to round it off by creating other delayed carriers, able to utilise Rabid Beast Bite with just a touch when not in combat, ensuring that the carriers will not have to cope with the disease themselves for days even while they remain infections, and being virtually undetectable as infected characters. It looks like a good suite to assault a locale with illness, and I especially like the implications it has for the Full Moon Caste, how being able to exert themselves physically isn\u2019t all showy kung fu action, it can also be more subtle and widespread.<br /> <br /> Undying God-Beast Perfection is an odd one, having minimums in Stamina <i>and</i> Strength and needing Deadly Beastman Transformation as one of its prerequisites. Still, I imagine it\u2019s the only way to give the war form its persistent regeneration, permanently enhancing that shape so that the Lunar restores a health level every time they reset to base Initiative. It makes me wonder if the more finalised version of that Charm might be a bit different from how it\u2019s shown up in quick character write-ups, possibly something that might particularly line up with this to allow a Lunar in the shape to keep firing off decisive attacks to keep knitting themselves back up.<br /> <br /> Hmm, wonder what a name like Incomparable Bezoar Nature might entail\u2026 oh, it\u2019s not about the stomach stone side, just the curative one, as a Lunar gets to be permanently immune to the poison of any animal that they can turn into, as well as letting the Stamina Excellency add two dice per mote for resisting other poisons.<br /> Poison Blood Prana provides an alternative delivery mechanism for toxins, as close range decisive attacks can be responded to with an unblockable counterattack in the form of a poisonous blood spray, hence adding the wound penalty as non-Charm dice to the Stamina based attack roll to expose an enemy to one of the products of their Charms, with increased duration if they\u2019re in an animal form at the time.<br /> <br /> But they start going for something different and a bit odder with Salamander\u2019s Tail Feint; acceptance of crippling injuries are allowed to negate even more damage, even to the point that it would go below the usual threshold for being allowed to make the substitution (referred in the flavour text as enabled by a process of letting the attack take off extraneous flesh). If the fight is to uphold a Defining Intimacy, it doesn\u2019t even use up their once per story limit on taking crippling injuries; that it can only be used once per scene would definitely put the Lunar ahead of the curve, particularly if they use their other Charm to keep fixing their mangling up. It gets even better with Protean, as being in an animal shape that can naturally shed limbs as a defence doesn\u2019t make it contribute to the per-story limit in any event. Mind, the Defining Intimacy part is the one that really intrigues me; come up with a Lunar that is mad for fighting, and let them throw themselves into the fray with wild abandon, coming away maimed and mangled to take a few scenes to fix it up, and then start all over again.<br /> <br /> Into Essence 3, and something rather Hellsing-esque in Form-Shedding Sacrifice, an Instant Charm with no cost. The Lunar attains a new method of using crippling to negate damage, in the form of sacrificing a shape that they\u2019re in, exchanging their Essence in damage levels for permanently losing the shape. On the face of it, it sounds like an extreme loss, but after a few hours you probably have more faces from random guards and bandits and voles and sparrows than you need, so you might as well shed them to save the real you. I especially love how it can be combined with the Charm to fake death to let a shell of the lost form be left behind, while the Lunar reflexively tries going to ground.<br /> <br /> Acid-Spitting Attack allows them to go a bit more directly bombardier than the Dexterity Charms did, as the Lunar becomes capable of paying motes, Initiative and Willpower to spit acid, an unblockable decisive attack that uses their Essence plus threshold attack successes as the dice to inflict aggravated damage, being able to shoot to medium range and all. Alternatively, they can spew it as a nasty spray hitting anybody in short range and rolling Essence as damage. Both attacks get to ignore Hardness, and the corrosion of the acid causes damaged characters to briefly lose some of their own soak and Hardness. And for one more, instead of an attack, they can go and spray it over an object as a demolition feat with automatic successes, getting to waive the Initiative and Willpower costs, and using their Stamina as the basis for determining what feats are permissible (which can still be elevated with the right Strength Charms). The attack form only gets to be used once per scene, but has the accommodating reset of crashing an enemy (which has to be particularly easy when you\u2019ve just made their armour all mushy).<br /> <br /> Oooh, some more extension to the internal capacity with Storm-Swallowing Technique, as the Lunar gets to suck up mundane and supernatural weather effects, possibly having to deal with some physical afflictions if they\u2019re something like magic acid rain or\u2026 electrical storms, probably. It says that it\u2019s only out to medium range (or long when Legendary Size), but I think that probably ought to be read in terms of the radius around the character that gets cleared rather than obligating them to rise miles into the air to get close enough. Ooh, and it\u2019s strong enough to prevent the likes of Stormcaller from being able to reimpose its weather. It can only be used once per day, but it has to be one of the more impressive things the Lunar did that day; just imagine harnessing it to save a town from a tornado or something. And not at a super high Essence, either.<br /> <br /> Ah, finally Flesh-Waxing-Full Regeneration provides that capacity to deal with somebody hacking away limbs or removing eyes. It\u2019s\u2026 incredibly cheap, a scene-long Charm for only 1 mote and 1 Willpower; if the Lunar is in a fight, then their crippling penalties will be reduced by one after each passage of the difference between their Essence and 7 rounds, fully restoring themselves once it hits zero. Not being in combat takes longer, as the penalty reduction comes only at the end of a scene and needs to be invoked repeatedly, but again, cheap; four scenes and as many motes later, and you\u2019ll have your arm back good as new.<br /> <br /> Indestructible Recursive Design is a curious approach; the Lunar becomes permanently receptive to healing magic (presumably that of others as well as themselves) even while in perfect health, allowing it to cause them to grow extra organs or tougher tissues, in the form of giving themselves up to their Essence in temporary -0 health levels (which means they eventually get to make up for the shortcomings of Ox-Body Technique, I think).<br /> <br /> After all of those, Perdurant Beast Perfection seems positively mundane, a two mote Charm to subtract their Essence from the dice rolled for an environmental hazard\u2019s damage. It allows resistance to hazards that would inflict uncountable damage with a Willpower point surcharge, but rather than negating completely, it reduces them down a flat 15 damage, which will then be further reduced by this and any other appropriate effect. That is unless they\u2019ve adapted to the environment by Rugged Beast Endurance, in which case they get to reduce those extreme hazards without needing to pay Willpower. The reference to swimming in the Demon Sea is want sells this Charm for me; language like that was used in a Second Edition Charm that just made you immune outright, which felt to me as though it detracted from the image of making an enduring feat against a legendarily deadly hazard. I prefer this more involved format.<br /> <br /> And Endurance comes to an end with a skip to Essence 5 for\u2026 Soul Beyond Shape. Huh, what does it- ah, this\u2019ll be your kind of Sha\u2019a Oka trick, saving yourself from death by sacrificing a human or animal shape for it (apparently not one you need to be in at the time). It automatically heals your Incapacitated health level, and automatically activates Charms for faking death and going into healing hibernation for free, and being able to keep themselves safe with Wound-Mastering Body Evolution not needing to have an Initiative cost. Only decisive attacks can hurt them again while in that state, and even if they\u2019re mortally wounded again, they can just keep on sacrificing shapes. It can only last until the Lunar heals a health level, and the process is given a nice touch of visual oddity as the lost shapes are expelled from the Lunar, bearing the prevented mortal wounds. After that, can\u2019t be used again in that story. Isn\u2019t even very expensive, just needs ten motes and three Willpower. Certainly lends a lot to why some of the older Lunars might be hard to pin down.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Yes, after the more utilitarian Defence, Endurance does a lot to draw in a lot of the more elaborate and mythic, the feats that could really let a Lunar dedicated to Stamina impose themselves upon the world, in addition to not letting the world impose itself on them. As I said before, I think it\u2019s really valuable for the Full Moon Caste for them to be able to be associated with a bunch of feats like this.<br /> <br /> All right, so, going over all of these has\u2026 left me completely exhausted. And there are still the actual Berserker Charms to go through. But there\u2019s only four pages of them, so those shouldn\u2019t take too long. I\u2019ll have myself some dinner, and then knock those out.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T16:10:43-07:00","id":1296264,"title":"","content":"Okay, finish off Stamina with the Berserker Charms, which I\u2019m going to guess will have a lot more room to breathe now that they\u2019re not treated as one of the two mainstays of Lunar combat power.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Starts off again with the iconic Relentless Lunar Fury. First thing I notice is how it can last longer than it could before, being a scene long Charm now. So what does it do\u2026 okay, so first it centres itself on the berserker concept by being only possible to activate after having received damage from a decisive attack, rather than just pulling that rage up willy nilly. For their trouble, they get halved wound and crippling penalties, and reap Initiative from any further decisive damage, and if they\u2019re in the form of an animal with the complementary Merit, they also get that old benefit of adding a dice to all physical Attribute rolls. The Lunar doesn\u2019t become mindless, or even necessarily have their judgement clouded, just\u2026 hyperfocused, as they\u2019re prevented from making influence actions unrelated to combat, engaging in withdrawal, or even ceasing to fight at all before all enemies are unconscious, dead or running away. It\u2019s possible to end the Charm early, but doing so punishes by instantly crashing the Lunar. I think it\u2019s a good, nuanced take on a berserker rage, and for a starting Charm I\u2019d say that it provides both decent benefits and a good sense of character. Excited to see how the following Charms expand and elaborate.<br /> <br /> First of those is Deathless Fury Unleashed, a Permanent Charm allowing Halting the Scarlet Flow to waive its Willpower cost if activated at the same time as the preceding Charm, with higher Essence allowing it to still be paid to allow the regeneration to continue functioning even if the Lunar is crashed. Then Mother Grizzly Attitude, a more direct Permanent enhancement to the Fury, allowing it to be invoked not when the Lunar is struck, but when they witness such an attack against their mate of somebody for whom they have a positive higher Tie, also allowing the Charm to provide a bonus to Parry used in defending others (not just the person whose injury provoked the rage, it would seem). That idea of expanding the scope a bit, and being able to play out the scenario of being pushed by outrages inflicted upon others is very compelling. I especially like how that lines up with Fury not interfering with their mind very much, that the ward\u2019s Lunar defender doesn\u2019t necessarily have to be terrifying to them.<br /> <br /> Into Essence 2 for Enraged Ratel Persistence, which is not Permanent; instead, its activation allows the Lunar to draw strength from adversity in turning their wound penalty into a bonus for Resolve. That\u2019s something it gets to do all on its own, but in combination with the Fury it ensures that fear or influence that would cause a Lunar to give up on the fight are completely ineffectual. That\u2019s probably pretty powerful as social defences go, but counterbalanced by the specificity of when it can be used, and it helps preserve the integrity of that method of fighting.<br /> <br /> Undaunted Berserker Ire allows a Lunar in combat to substitute Initiative for Willpower for resisting social influence and similar magics, which might be helpful even in the face of the prior Charm if there are troublesome forms of influence that aren\u2019t explicitly trying to end the fight. The exchange rate is rather high, 5 to 1 against the Willpower, but I guess that the higher turnover rate for Initiative can help make it back up easier than spending Willpower would do. When Fury is active, it gets a once-per-scene option to waive one point of the Willpower cost for resisting an influence.<br /> <br /> Another alternative activation condition in Sleeping Tiger Wakes, as the Lunar gets to activate their Fury just in response to somebody threatening or insulting one\u2019s mate or the subject of a higher Intimacy, allowing a Join Battle to be made with half the Essence in bonus successes if this is the way that the fight starts. It\u2019s interesting to me that a Lunar can\u2019t be as angry on behalf of themselves as they can be for somebody else\u2026 maybe with the exception of somebody insulting an appropriate Major or Defining Principle.<br /> <br /> Essence 3 now, and Knife-Biting Attitude, which has been mentioned in at least one place before, going to have to look back at what\u2019ll make more sense in context later, but for now\u2026 huh, neat, get to activate before decisive damage is rolled, and get Initiative equal to inflicted damage, pushing the Lunar even further as they\u2019re damaged. It can\u2019t normally be used in crash, but can when Fury is active, which seems like it might makes this a rather good standby for pulling oneself out of that state (especially for a Lunar who buys enough Ox-Bodies to get another 12 or 20 health levels, and has the body to spare\u2026 hmm, already forgotten if regeneration interacts with all of this).<br /> <br /> Ah, Bloodthirsty Siaka Frenzy is finally giving Lunars some of the power in situations to activate Fury, as well as getting some of the apex predator stuff back in, as they get to use the Charm when <i>they</i> have inflicted damage on the health of somebody else, namely down to at least an opponent\u2019s -2 health levels. Furthermore, they get the option to spend three motes whenever Initiative resets to base in order to add the struck foe\u2019s wound penalty after that latest attack to the value of their base Initiative.<br /> <br /> Wounded Bear Rampage permanently endows a Lunar with a sense of empowerment to the will to survive as their injuries grow, allowing the non-crashed Lunar to get Willpower every time they take damage to their -2 or 4 health levels (further synergizing with Ox-Body), which can rise even beyond their personal limit. Oh, wait, spoke to soon, it doesn\u2019t work <i>every</i> time they take such an injury, as it\u2019s the odd permanent Charm that can only be used once per scene; like one of the earlier Charms, even then it won\u2019t reset if the Lunar still has at least -2 health levels damaged when the next scene rolls around.<br /> <br /> Gone up to Essence 4 now with Frenzied Desperation Strike, a little something for the Lunar that has been crashed, allowing them to launch a decisive attack despite having no Initiative. It took me a couple of reads (and going back to make sure I wasn\u2019t misremembering the crash rules) to figure out what the base damage of that attack would be, but noting how it had a <i>minimum</i> damage of 1 put it together for me; most likely it\u2019s a matter of Lunar\u2019s Essence + 5, then take the negative value of their current Initiative, half it, and subtract it from the other value. Been a while since I did much beyond basic maths, but\u2026 yeah, dividing negatives is fairly straightforward, so if you\u2019re Essence 4 and at -6 Initiative, you\u2019ll roll six dice in decisive damage. And hey, if you hit with that, it actually resets you to higher than you were at before, ending up at 0 Initiative. Like, the damage value\u2019s still going to be small, so probably only really ever useful to somebody with no Hardness, but it can probably still be a good turnaround. Only gets to be used once per scene, but if at least 20 Initiative is gathered in a single tick, then it can be reset.<br /> <br /> Terrible Wolverine Onslaught is, I think, a bit unusual as a Simple Charm that can only be activated while under the effects of a prior Charm, being limited to synergy with Fury. It also costs an aggravated health level in addition to the motes and Willpower, all for the sake of the Lunar stirring themselves into an even more severe frenzy, ensuring that all of their physical Attribute actions get one success, wound penalties are ignored completely, they\u2019re actually applied to decisive damage rolled against the Lunar (I think\u2026 it couldn\u2019t be that they\u2019re offset a bit by weakening their own attacks), they can use up their attack action to reflexively clash any direct targeted on their person with a withering or decisive response, and any decisive attack made on a battle group will force them to check for rout, while making routs in response to attacks that reduce size are made at a higher difficulty. The Charm can only be reset by healing aggravated damage (I wonder if having it downgraded to lethal counts), and a berserker animal shape adds one other success when the rolls are for attacks. The effect on battle groups is especially interesting here as an indication of how horrifying the Lunar is becoming.<br /> <br /> And that probably comes to a head in Essence 5, with Unchained Monster Death-Rage. Ah, I was wondering if there would be something like this, a power to keep a Lunar relentless past the point of mortal injury. It\u2019s actually fairly straightforward mechanically; if a Lunar with Fury active has the Incapacitated health level filled, they can ignore that for the remaining duration of the Charm, all further decisive damage is worthless, base Initiative is increased by two, an Initiative point is gained whenever the Lunar starts their turn, and they add their damn Initiative to the value of their natural soak. So it\u2019s basically, if they\u2019re really powerful, trying to kill a raging Lunar only makes them more deadly, up until the point when the fight ends and they drop into unconsciousness or the verge of death (barring the occasion of something fixing their Incapacitation before then).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, good job done with the Berserker Charms. I appreciate how not all of them are strictly dependent on Relentless Lunar Fury, and that Charm itself really shines when it\u2019s not dependent on figuring out combinations from Charms scattered all over the place. I\u2019d say you could build some strong character concepts or highly memorable scenes just out of what can be found in this subcategory.<br /> <br /> <br /> And there, I did it! Covered all of the Stamina Charms in one day, with a bit of time to spare. On the whole, I would say that Stamina doesn\u2019t quite have the same depth of theme that Dexterity did, but I think it makes up for that in a bit more variety, as well as carrying one of the iconic Lunar fighting styles. It\u2019s kind of the Appearance of the physical Attributes, I think. Really, I\u2019m just glad that an otherwise somewhat bland Attribute managed to still accumulate a lot of pretty cool things.<br /> <br /> <br /> I\u2019ll take a slight break, and then try to get in some kind of start with the Strength Charms before I head off to sleep. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T16:34:15-07:00","id":1296269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296228#post1296228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, I was only referring to the additional Initiative required by the Protean effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>In that case the effect is either 9 at Essence 4 or 10 at Essence 5, it can't be 8.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">https://swordofcreation.net/</a> : A secondary forum focusing on archiving homebrew, both mechanical and fluff, and receiving critique on it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/108-flare","name":"Flare","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar108_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T18:26:34-07:00","id":1296287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flare</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296269#post1296269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In that case the effect is either 9 at Essence 4 or 10 at Essence 5, it can't be 8.</div> </div> </div> </div>Errrrrrr\u2026.{raised eyebrow}<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296131#post1296131\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">if in the shape of an animal with Impenetrable Armour, an enemy need to have even more Initiative (generally going to be nine or ten) to successfully crash them.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T18:33:19-07:00","id":1296289,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296287#post1296287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Errrrrrr\u2026.{raised eyebrow}<br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>hmm. Strange. I guess I misread your post, apologies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">https://swordofcreation.net/</a> : A secondary forum focusing on archiving homebrew, both mechanical and fluff, and receiving critique on it.","edited":{"userid":108,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/108-flare","user":"Flare","date":". 03-12-2019, 09:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/108-flare","name":"Flare","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar108_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-12T21:49:02-07:00","id":1296338,"title":"","content":"Okay, gonna get a start on the Strength Charms. I\u2019ll admit, I\u2019m being drawn to these from the promise of the base gratification of being able to turn into something huge, as well as other displays of raw power, although I\u2019ll also be interested in seeing variations of that.<br /> <br /> The whole time leading up to this, I\u2019ve been wondering if my assumption that the previewed Charm to haunt somebody\u2019s nightmares wasn\u2019t maybe the Heart\u2019s Blood one and I had jumped the gun on assuming that there were further variations on acquiring shapes, so I\u2019m glad to see it\u2019s not the form that Terrifying Predator Consumption takes (and, come to think of it, that thing might have been Essence 2 anyway). I find the concept of the Charm, of being able to get the shape of somebody who fears you by defeating them in combat in some manner that doesn\u2019t kill to be effective, and I have a liking for the irony that it might entail a subversion of the expectation that allows it to function in the first place, but I do wonder if it being effective regardless of the strength of the Tie might make it too easy compared to most of the others. Maybe that\u2019s mitigated a bit by the presumed effort of subduing a target? I\u2019m a bit wary.<br /> <br /> All right, getting a start on the good stuff with Towering Beast Form, allowing one to assume the shape of an animal with Legendary Size. When reports first came out about this having a cost persisting across its use there was a bit of concern, so I\u2019m glad to see that the Charm has a bit more leniency with that by way of only requiring that cost per turn if the Lunar wasn\u2019t pressing their attack aggressively enough. That and how, like Emerald Grasshopper, the Charm allows for moving between such large forms without paying further costs. And it\u2019s good to let it have another Attribute option since it\u2019s a necessity for at least a few characters to even assume their spirit shape.<br /> <br /> Yes, there\u2019s that nightmare Charm again, Stalking Nightmare Hunter. After having seen the likes of Intelligence provide additional Heart\u2019s Blood Charms, I half expected it to be here (the other half was taking cues from things like Manipulation). This kind of placement does emphasize it more of a Charm for acquiring shapes than assaulting people psychically, although it\u2019s still adequate in that manner. I continue to be bemused by the idea that Strength, of all Attributes, allows for acquiring shapes from afar.<br /> <br /> And then Mountainous Spirit Expression, but which the shape of an alternative animal or a human can be granted Legendary Size. At times like this, I am given a bit to wondering about the parameters of that; between animals such as elephants and tyrant lizards and references to warstriders, that\u2019s somewhere between four and about ten metres tall, and that can be a significant variation. It doesn\u2019t amount to much mechanical difference, but it does still influence how I might try and see the thing play out. As for the Charm, it simply reiterates the benefits of the Legendary Size Merit, and otherwise it has the same rules as Towering Beast Form.<br /> <br /> Hmm, an Essence 4 Charm up here, Undaunted Behemoth Avatar, wonder what tha- ah, relieves some transformation into a large creature by way of removing the recurring cost unless one if crashed. I can\u2019t recall if Hungry All-Consuming Cloud was one of the swarm Charms, but it gives that benefit to it as well. Does require one to have bought deeply into Strength (or Stamina, if appropriate) though.<br /> <br /> All right, getting a start on the Offence Charms, and my eye first goes to a sidebar informing us that, by and large, the Charms can\u2019t enhance weapons that don\u2019t actually use Strength in the damage roll, which makes sense. With that clear, begins with Boulders-as-Pebbles Attitude, allowing the Lunar\u2019s powerful arms to apply ranged attacks without Accuracy penalty and with increased range, as well as not needing to pay Initiative for improvisation of the likes of, well, throwing a boulder at somebody. Essence 3 and Strength 5 allows one to extend range even further if a Willpower surcharge if paid, possibly even to extreme range. I think it\u2019s a nice one to start with, being more tactically oriented while still having a good image of terror from the possibility that you\u2019re not quite far enough to be safe. Makes up for the slightly narrower application.<br /> <br /> Deadly Claw Blow is an Instant Charm without cost, and\u2026 this is the one to substitute Strength as an attack Attribute. I hadn\u2019t considered it before with the Stamina one, but both have received some modifications from how they were originally suggested, not being Permanent and having certain limits on their application; in this case, it\u2019s only for close range attacks, and only if they go unarmed, using natural weapons or heavy ones. I presume that in the end there were certain balance concerns, although like with Stamina, my only personal interest is in it giving a good sense of image consistency. Speaking of balance, it also lays out clear rules for how the following Strength enhancing Charms aren\u2019t going to afford one a means of making ridiculous attacks.<br /> Okay, starting to get into the more interesting stuff with Ferocious Biting Tooth to provide additional Overwhelming damage to withering attacks or the lower of Strength or Essence as bonus successes for decisive damage, as well as letting unarmed attacks inflict lethal damage. Divine Predator Strike provides post-soak or decisive damage based on how many 10s came up on the original attack roll (counting 9s as well if the enemy is showing their belly by being crashed), up to a maximum of Strength for withering attacks and that or Essence if it\u2019s lower for decisive. It\u2019s getting a bit of conceptual momentum going.<br /> <br /> Snarling Hound Strike allows a clash to be treated as a contest display between opposing predators, with the Lunar being able to assert themselves against an enemy whose Initiative is not higher by making that character\u2019s rolled 1s subtract from their successes, to a limit of the Lunar\u2019s Essence. While it\u2019s normally a Supplemental Charm to the Lunar\u2019s own clash, it can also be employed reflexively if the enemy is the one initiating the clash by magic, so a Lunar\u2019s fury could overwhelm somebody like another Lunar trying to entangle an opponent in a net of flesh. If the clash is decisive, it can also add the value subtracted from the enemy\u2019s damage to the value of base Initiative.<br /> <br /> Waiting for something to leap out at me, and unfortunately it\u2019s not Tiger Claw Swat, even if there\u2019s nothing wrong with the Lunar\u2019s parries and successful clashes knocking the enemy that tried it back and down. Does at least seem like a good way to make a point.<br /> <br /> Unstoppable Beast-Force Blow starts incorporating the denial of advantages side, as it allows decisive attacks to be enhanced by subtracting threshold attack roll successes from a target\u2019s Hardness, in addition to a damage roll that benefits from double 10s. Ah, here\u2019s something a bit more interesting, Last Warrior\u2019s Unity, the means by which a Lunar can make it impossible to disarm a weapon by way of directly fusing it to the Lunar\u2019s body, albeit at the cost of rendering that hand non-functional for everyday business. There\u2019s also a helpful description of how such merged weapons that change around when the Luna changes shape.<br /> <br /> Okay, and I\u2019m going to call it a night with Prey-Hobbling Bite; where Stamina provided a few methods against crippling damage, this is the start of Lunars\u2019 inflicting it. It targets a lower Initiative enemy again, allowing a decisive attack that creates an injury with a -1 penalty to start, on top of which are any added by the 10s rolled on damage, up to a limit of the Lunar\u2019s Strength. The crippling attacks can also get an extra die if the Lunar should be in a form that can naturally inflict crippling attacks.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T08:22:59-07:00","id":1296421,"title":"","content":"Just gonna get stuck right back in.<br /> <br /> At some point I feel as though I\u2019ve got to go back over all of these Charms and make a stronger note of their Type, since it\u2019s an element that I might often overlook even though it can have a significant impact on the dynamic of Charms, especially concerning how the Lunar ones might combine with Martial Arts. In any case, Mighty Ram Smash is a Simple Charm to create a decisive attack that starts off with one success on the damage roll, and is able to cause knockback if it rolls at least two more successes, with rules for adding Hardness ignoring damage if the target should impact something sturdy. It provides a limit to how far somebody can be knocked back by a strike made at range, but they\u2019ll still take impact damage according to how high the initial damage roll was, and if they\u2019re making the attack while in the form of a charging animal, they get to treat their successes as twice their number for the sake of determining the knockback range. The potential to do this with thrown or shot weapons in particular interests me, makes we want to go back and check on comparable Solar powers sometime.<br /> <br /> World-Shaking Slam seems like a rather braggadocio name for an Essence 1 Charm. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Hmm, when knocking a foe prone or back, the damage roll made against them gets to pull double duty as a feat of demolition roll against whatever they impacted, which adds further Hardness-ignoring decisive bashing damage to the enemy. I like the idea of that, but I can\u2019t help but wonder if it runs up against the corebook\u2019s suggestion that feats of strength rules not be strictly regarded when dramatically smashing somebody through scenery in the middle of a fight. Maybe if this is regarded as something to cause more meaningful damage that might undermine the structure itself\u2026 anyway, also has an Essence 2 repurchase to allow one to pay a surcharge to ensure the enemy is trapped following a successful feat, which is experienced as a grapple requiring another feat of strength to be released from; it suggests being trapped by rubble, but I\u2019m liking the idea of comically planting somebody in the ground. Being in an animal shape that can trample ups the damage that a successful demolition inflicts on the enemy.<br /> <br /> Grapples themselves start getting enhancement through Grizzly Bear Embrace, letting threshold successes on the attack roll be added as dice to the Initiative one, as well as to the control roll if the enemy is at lower Initiative. The Protean benefit for this one is particularly big, as the Lunar gets to add the value of Essence and Strength to Initiative for the sake of placing them above a target to get to apply the bonus dice for the control roll, if they\u2019re in a shape with Legendary Size or some grapple ability. I\u2019d really like to go back and see what Dexterity Charms this one might match up well with.<br /> <br /> Then Mangling Grasp Might can Supplement grapple-based attacks by doubling 9s for their withering damage or adding Essence as bonus dice to decisive, as well as doubling how many rounds of control can be given up to make throws or slams, even being able to throw an enemy out to short range. That\u2019s followed by Kraken-Arm Lash, a Simple Charm with an Initiative cost that allows a Lunar to make their grapples out to medium range by means of extending arms, with a successful catch pulling them into close range, and also affords the option of immediately ending the hold by throwing two range bands away. This sounds like another versatile Charm, being useful for either getting ahold of an enemy that might otherwise be good at keeping their distance to crush them with other Charms, or providing a number of options for relocating an enemy on the battlefield; I think that it would synergize particularly well with several Dexterity Charms concerned with enemy placement. If it\u2019s made in the shape of an animal with an ability called Infinite Grasp, that I don\u2019t believe I\u2019m familiar with, the Initiative cost is waived if the enemy is at lower Initiative. Hmm, I\u2019m beginning to think that I\u2019m going to need to start buying up more of Hundred Devils Night Parade.<br /> <br /> Foe-Hammer Technique, for when you just need to use one enemy as a club against another; a grappled enemy can be slammed into another at close range, making the attack apply to both characters, although the second one only takes half the damage rolled against the first, to which soak and Hardness are still applied, and it won\u2019t give up any Initiative if it was withering. Only gets to be once per scene, but is reset by crashing a grappled enemy with savaging attacks, which I\u2019m sure many of the rest of these can oblige. Oh, but being in a grappling animal form allows the damage rolled against the second target to be made in full, making this sound like an especially inviting Charm to wield in animal form. Finally, Throat-Bearing Hold allows a Lunar to set their grappled enemy up for allies by only needing to spend one round of control to restrain or drag them, which also inflicts an Onslaught penalty, and if done in a pack hunter shape has that penalty also apply to soak and Hardness for a turn.<br /> <br /> And now it\u2019s one of the Lunar main events, the true Third Edition rendering of Deadly Beastman Transformation. This one has been such a big deal that I actually went and pulled up a couple of the versions attached to Quick Characters, just to see how they might compare. It\u2019s a Simple Charm that requires the Lunar to already be in their hybrid form (although it becomes reflexive if invoked in a combo with that Charm), enhancing its attitude and strength to that of a war form for just one mote (although that will be in addition to the commitment for the hybrid body of course). From that, the Lunar gains a persistent bonus of their Strength to rolls for withering damage (meaning they\u2019ll also be able to add it again from the Excellency, since dice caps don\u2019t apply), feats of strength and movement actions, the value of the Attribute is added to their soak, and their Strength is considered to be increased by the value of their Essence for determining possible feats of strength. In exchange for that, they gain a penalty to Defence which can\u2019t be negated (I\u2019m going to guess this is meant to convey a certain shift in attitude rather than anything to do with their size or reflexes), and connection to Artifacts becomes downgraded a bit. It\u2019s actually pretty similar to the one given to the Lunar in the corebook (with some of the other bonuses having been distributed out to other Charms that have been made to synergize with it), and identical to that in the write-up for Eska of the Seven Blades. Ultimately, I find this to be a more impressive expression than Second Edition got; the Lunar actually gets some potentially significant bonuses to hardiness and activity in combat or exertions of raw strength, and I think those help it to feel more like a dedicated battle transformation than just a flat dot for the physical Attributes and trying to string together the most efficient collection of mutations one can think of (plus, like the old Relentless Fury, needing to look over Charms scattered all around for what other bonuses can be pulled in). Really, it would be good enough for being something that doesn\u2019t tell the reader to do most of the work of making it actually perform the function of being a terrible battle shape. And being only Essence 1, I am a bit interested to see if it has any expansions later on. I think it came out well.<br /> <br /> Ah, didn\u2019t have to wait long for that; getting to maximum Strength allows purchase of Terrifying Ogre Alteration, letting the war form have another four dots worth of mutations to go on top of the hybrid body. In this case, I\u2019m cool with that; the base Charm is already doing a lot apart from mutations so this just amounts to a nice treat on top, and I do like the idea that the war form can constitute an additional layer of physical transformation to the hybrid body. And hey, we\u2019ve got some very nice Supernatural Merits to pick from now.<br /> <br /> Essence 2 starts with another iconic power that I\u2019ve been waiting to see again, Claws of the Silver Moon. I\u2019m looking at the differences in activation cost, and I\u2019m pretty sure I can guess what the discounted one is for. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> And\u2026 I was quite incorrect, heh. So, this time around the Charm is rendered as allowing one\u2019s human form to grant unarmed attacks or a natural weapon the traits of an Artifact (I\u2019m presuming a light weapon one, although I think that should be a bit clearer), and in an animal form one of its attack types gets slightly enhanced Accuracy, damage and Overwhelming for withering attacks, with different shapes allowing different natural weapons to be enhanced. The altered cost is not about Deadly Beastman Transformation, they\u2019re additional motes to let the active Charm add one\u2019s Brawl as non-Charm dice to feats of demolition. It\u2019s stated that, by and large, an unarmed attack being given Artifact traits in this way isn\u2019t compatible with Martial Arts, although the Storyteller is encouraged to use discretion in allowing the odd compatibility when the imagery of the clawed attack is fitting to the style in particular, such as something using tiger claws as a form weapon. And what I think is a very big deal is how this one is also made a subject for repurchase to acquire Evocations, along the same parameters as the Moonsilver Carapace. That one is a bit of a big deal to me, as I must admit a certain soft spot for the image of a Lunar, say, shooting off certain kinds of energy attack while also thinking it\u2019s not quite fitting to their own Charms, so I like the idea of this as a means of compromise (in addition to anything else that Evocations might explore). It does still have a bit of compatibility with the war form, but only in that it can be reflexively activated alongside that Charm.<br /> <br /> Right, after those more signature ones it seems to be settling back down to more utilitarian ones, although I think they\u2019re starting to get more impressive; Armor-Rending Claw Fist can supplement a withering attack to let it ignore half of the soak granted by armour, and if damage is inflicted the onslaught penalty gets to apply to any Hardness for the turn (once per scene, reset by hitting a crashed enemy with a decisive attack). Foe-Defying Frenzy allows one to reflexively clash with a decisive attack (being able to reflexively ready a weapon if necessary), which doesn\u2019t need to count as the attack for the round, but is also purely defensive and thus rolling no damage; at least not without an Essence 3 repurchase that allows a Willpower surcharge to be paid to do so (which gets refunded if the resulting damage incapacitates the attacker).<br /> <br /> Huh, Ox-Dragon Smash looks pretty cheap at one mote and Willpower; Simple Charm to make a withering attack with Essence post-soak damage, that if successful stuns the enemy with the force of the blow; their onslaught penalty applies to any rolls they make until they finish their next turn, even if their actual Defence penalty refreshes in the meantime. If the attack crashed the enemy, then it ensures that the onslaught penalty doesn\u2019t refresh, and the actions are going to be penalized until recovery from crash. The Charm actually includes an example of use, although for the life of me I can\u2019t figure out why this, of all of them, should warrant such (I can honestly think of a few other Charms that would have been much better served by such clarification).<br /> Anyway, Winding Constrictor Coils helps out the Lunar who is grappling somebody, ensuring that their Defence penalty for being engaged in a hold is lessened and not lowering their control rounds if lower Initiative enemies attack or damage them, or from any enemies at all if the Lunar is doing so while in the shape of an animal with Crushing Coils. As a kind of stunt, I like to imagine the odd Lunar in the shape of an anaconda, who remained extremely difficult to untangle from some unfortunate Wyld Hunt member even after being decapitated.<br /> <br /> Horrifying Beast-God Might is another enhancement to the warform, increasing any Initiative Breaks it gets by a value of the wound penalty of a crashed enemy plus one. And Limb-Maiming Flourish is an\u2026 interesting take on enhancements to Lunars dealing out crippling injuries, in that it basically makes the crippling injury into a kind of deal; if the Lunar has at least Initiative 10, they can supplement a decisive attack to ensure that every success shown on the damage roll gets rolled again for further damage, unless the victim elects to take the highest form of crippling injury that attack is capable of creating. I presume that still actually mitigates the damage, but I\u2019d say for the Lunar doing so it\u2019s a decent trade off to get a limb or something as a trophy out of it. And it doesn\u2019t technically override the voluntary nature of accepting crippling injuries, it just\u2026 smooths the wheels a bit. Hehe, it strikes a certain chord for me personally; there\u2019s a bit in one of the Team Four Star specials where a movie villain begs Piccolo not to break his arm that has always felt very Lunar to me, and now I\u2019m seeing it realised as a sort of inversion, in which an enemy hoping to avoid the greater damage begs to please have their arm broken\u2026 clean off. In any case, accepting that injury doesn\u2019t count as their valid crippling injury per story, and they\u2019re immune to the Charm being used again\u2026 but just for the scene; a Lunar could probably spend the rest of a story chasing down some Wyld Hunt that took liberties, repeatedly targeting one unlucky person until they\u2019re left in\u2026 a state I shall not describe. And if they\u2019re in an animal shape good for crippling attacks, incapacitating an enemy with the enhanced attack or coming away with their body part feels so edifying that they get a point of Willpower back.<br /> <br /> Nightmare Scar Memento is a permanent enhancement to the Lunar\u2019s general presence, ensuring that any characters who receive some form of crippling injury feel a Tie of fear towards the Lunar that persists until the injury heals (including getting some kind of magical prosthetic); Minor in most cases, Major if it took away a limb or similar major body part. The Exalt will also get their Essence in bonus dice to threaten rolls made against that character, and they can take it with Charisma with a spirit shape that can threaten people. This is putting me in mind of a Lunar with the demeanour of Captain Hook\u2019s crocodile, or Moby Dick, haunting some Dynast with the memory of their lost limb. Hmm, it would probably make for an effective and very nasty stunt if the Lunar keeps the removed part as some kind of trophy that they show off while making the intimidation. I don\u2019t know why I\u2019m fixating so much on the implications of Lunars violently removing body parts\u2026<br /> <br /> Moving on, Foe-Pinning Strike, which has a bit of a cost at six motes, five Initiative and a point of Willpower. Oh, also the first Essence 3 Charm. In exchange for all that, they get to make a decisive ranged attack using solid projectiles against somebody within close range of a large object or solid surface, that will pin them against it if at least three levels of damage are inflicted. It functions as a grapple, for which the Lunar rolls Strength plus whichever combat Ability was used for control, which they can enhance with other appropriate Charms for grabbing. Getting free requires the trapped character or an ally to dedicate turns to dislodging the projectile, each one given removing two rounds of control, or else succeeding at a feat of strength requiring the Attribute at 5 to attempt, although whatever happens pulling loose can be done immediately and reflexively once the control rounds run out. A lot of Strength has been more severe up until this point, but I find this to bring in some more of the Lunars\u2019 playful quality.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m guessing we\u2019re going to roll that back again with Jaws of the River Dragon. Oh, that\u2019s actually a very straightforward thing, doubling 9s on rolls to make and control grapples. And Grasping Pseudopod Method allows a Lunar at Initiative 15 or more to supplement a grapple to ensure that, if it\u2019s successful, they can make the control roll twice and keep the better result (only needing to pay for Charms enhancing the rolls once), with being in a shape good for grappling allowing a 2 Initiative surcharge to be paid to make the Charm reflexive upon successfully landing the attack, but before the Initiative is rolled. I especially like the imagery ascribed to this, of limbs dissolving and reforming in place to ensure a tight grip.<br /> <br /> Hmm, Sundering Fang Bite reapplies some of the force that Lunars can apply to shearing through armour, allowing them to shatter mundane weapons that failed to Parry or clash a Lunar\u2019s decisive attack, or automatically disarm them if Artifacts. It has a significant cost of 4 Initiative, which specifically has to be paid before the Lunar is allowed to roll for the damage of their attack, so it\u2019s basically like the breaking weapon absorbing some of the assault\u2019s force. Still, disarming a person one way or another is probably worth it. And then Burrowing Devil Strike applies that sundering force more directly, as the Lunar makes a special decisive attack with its own damage pool of Essence plus the 10s rolled on the attack, that ignores Hardness and with any damage rolled at all halving Hardness for the duration of the scene, unless the targeted foe crashes the Lunar that did it. It also has its uses against an enemy with no Hardness, as the attack\u2019s threshold successes will also be added to the damage pool (which is for an attack that does not reset to base Initiative). It\u2019s only available once per scene, but I think the reset condition of gaining 12 Initiative in one tick is more accommodating than some similar resets.<br /> <br /> The description of Fatal Strix Fury got a chuckle out of me; it gets to add a surcharge to Mighty Ram Smash to direct a launched opponent straight up, causing them to experience falling damage. And then Swooping Hybroc Death-Strike lets that be followed up by the Lunar jumping up (using the turn\u2019s movement action) after them while still in the air to put a bit of extra force behind their fall, as a Hardness-ignoring reflexive decisive attack using the amount of damage that horizontal knockback would have inflicted as the base pool, with the Lunar themselves not risking damage from coming back down to earth. If it\u2019s done in an animal form capable of flight, the attack roll gets non-Charm dice, and the Lunar has the option of remaining in the air. I was wondering if they might get something equivalent to those Solar flash-step attacks, and am glad to see the incorporation of shapeshifting give it a distinctive twist.<br /> <br /> Savage Moonsilver Talons looks like it\u2019s getting back into more of the murderous mood, as the Lunar gets to put a bit of their anima behind an attack on an enemy who has an especial bearing of prey, being at least 15 lower on the Initiative order; for that, withering attacks get to double threshold successes in determining raw damage, and decisive attacks get to add those successes to the damage pool at all. It\u2019s only once a scene, but gets reset when the user resets their Initiative with a successful decisive attack and then crashes some enemy, so it feels like it encourages a particularly aggressive approach.<br /> <br /> That momentum gets picked up on for the following Charm, Stampede-Force Strike, as the Lunar who incapacitates an enemy and resets Initiative gets to reflexively launch another decisive attack on somebody that the Exalt hasn\u2019t attacked already in that tick; they apparently have to use the Initiative they reset to (which I\u2019ll note can be higher than usual by now, if they\u2019re using it alongside the warform), but if it\u2019s not stopped by Hardness, then they get to add half of their Initiative prior to the reset as dice for the damage pool. They can also use the Charm to repeat a decisive attack on a battle group when another decisive attack lowered their Size. Trampling animals that made the Charm available with a decisive attack in which they tramped an enemy get to apply the benefits of that ability to any attacks made using the Charm; that it refers to it in the plural makes me think that it means the Charm can be reactivated repeatedly in the same tick, so long as each attack incapacitates an enemy. It seems to me as though this might make for a good finishing move when dealing with a number of foes who have taken a few licks across the fight.<br /> With Shell-Crushing Atemi, a Lunar can use decisive throw or slam attacks to divest an opponent of their armour, removing three rounds of control from the attack\u2019s damage to tear it away from their body and six rounds to do so in a manner that fully destroy it. Artifact armour instead costs the rating plus 3 in rounds of control to remove, breaking attunement in the process, although it\u2019s ineffectual against armour created by the user\u2019s magic, such as Glorious Solar Plate. If it\u2019s done while in the form of an animal with Strength 5 or more, the control rounds paid to remove the armour still get to apply to the throw or slam\u2019s damage roll.<br /> <br /> And it seems like things are getting particularly unpleasant with Spine-Breaking Technique\u2026 although the effect is fairly straightforward, simply inflicting a savaging attack on a grappled enemy without needing to reset to base. It\u2019s only once per scene, but has a reset condition of a regular attack resetting to base and then getting Initiative back up to 12 through a withering savaging attack, which should both work for somebody so invested in grappling and again puts my mind back towards the warform\u2019s increased base.<br /> <br /> And now it\u2019s into Essence 4, starting with All-Devouring Tyrant Maw, which\u2026 allows a Lunar with at least five rounds of control in a grapple to simply swallow up an enemy. It does require a certain logic with concerns to size in doing so, mind; the Lunar isn\u2019t getting some hyperspace gullet, there has to sensibly be room in their current shape for the being that they wish to snatch up. It seems to basically function as allowing the grapple to be maintained while the Lunar is freed up to act otherwise and the caught character can\u2019t attack anybody besides their holder, although the Storyteller is probably free to declare dangers from things like stomach acid or suffocation if they make sense for the specifics. The Lunar doesn\u2019t get to apply the typical grapple actions besides releasing\u2026 ah, I misunderstood the earlier point, the Storyteller fiat is just on ways that the grappled character might be further constrained; the danger of digestive fluids is the actual point of this, as the target has to deal with such as an environmental hazard or high difficulty and using the Lunar\u2019s Essence as the damage value, with a comment about how the Lunar has the option to deal with an incapacitated enemy by either spitting them out unconscious, or keeping them into to be fully digested (I\u2019m guessing with a preternatural rapidity) before expelling any indigestible equipment. Rounds of control from the grapple can only be lost from taking damage, and losing control completely that way is represented by getting enough wounds to open a hole that the victim can struggle free from, inflicting another damage level that is unavoidable. Changing shape to something too small to hold the enemy forces the Lunar to spit them free in the process of doing so.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m going to assume that a few Legendary Size shapes like the tyrant lizard can still apply their special attacks to try eating people, but for the most part I think that it\u2019s sensible to cover the notion of a Lunar doing so while in a gigantic form (or when they\u2019ve magically converted an enemy into a smaller one) with a Charm. It keeps the thing from having a lot of messy uncertainties, which I think has a higher value than whether or not it strictly makes sense.<br /> <br /> Even when at a regular size, a Lunar can invoke the spirit of being a gigantic form mercilessly crushing underfoot with Behemoth\u2019s Footprint Tread, as they pour fifteen motes and three levels of anima into creating a crushing shockwave of force, that rolls a decisive attack on any character in medium range, without discrimination, that applies one third of the Lunar\u2019s Initiative plus threshold successes on the attack roll as a damage pool against each of them, while also knocking any who take damage another range band away and prone. If it\u2019s in an animal shape that can Trample, the damage pool is based on <i>half</i> of the Lunar\u2019s Initiative, which I think might make this qualify as one of the most dangerous attacks to apply against multiple enemies in the game. That\u2019s something like the kind of power a warstrider could put behind hitting multiple people (albeit at a more limited range and with less collateral damage). And for my comments about energy attacks before, I particularly love the idea that a Lunar might manifest this with something like bringing their own foot down corresponding to the image of their anima coming along as a huge spectral foot. It sounds like a good show all round. Also particularly like the idea of doing it while in the shape of a horse or something; rearing up and bringing your forelegs down accompanied by a thunderclap.<br /> <br /> Divine Predator Supremacy makes a Lunar particularly stirred by their opponents\u2019 vulnerability, getting to make a withering attack that includes non-Charm dice equal to their wound penalty <i>and</i> doubles dice depending on the depth of that penalty; 9s for -1, 8s for -2, <i>7s</i> for -4. It\u2019s only once per scene, but incapacitating a crashed enemy (i.e. probably the person struck by this Charm) resets it. Come to think of it, the very existence of this Charm puts a lot of those reset conditions for reaping large amounts of Initiative all at once into perspective; I doubt that they necessarily strictly <i>require</i> this, but the idea that you can ever get a Charm to really elevate the successes added to your raw damage pool (plus the benefits of Deadly Beastman Transformation, plus the Strength Excellency) make it feel a bit less ephemeral.<br /> <br /> Rampaging God-Monster Empowerment invigorates the predatory spirit of a Lunar by letting them reroll their Essence in failed decisive damage dice against lower-Initiative enemies and get motes for all of the rolled 10s up to any spent on enhancing the attack (which surely starts with the 8 spent for this Charm), and allows 1s to be rerolled as well if the attack was on a crashed enemy\u2026 oh wait, it\u2019s a scene long Charm, the Lunar can reap these benefits persistently across a fight, that\u2019s neat (although it ends with the Lunar\u2019s own crash).<br /> <br /> And now there\u2019s (Style) Beastman Avatar, a name with a particularly exciting promise. Ah, it\u2019s an enhancement to Deadly Beastman Transformation by allowing it to be kind of melded with a Martial Art that the Lunar has mastered, letting the warform and Style\u2019s Form Charm by activated simultaneously when assuming the former, with the added cost of a Willpower surcharge.<br /> Across the board reflexive Form Charm activation sounds like a particularly good benefit (even if each additional Style added costs more experience), and I do find something compelling both in the idea that there can be something characteristic to Lunars assuming the warform for the sake of their martial arts, and that the first thing they do upon donning their horrific monster form is settle into a stance or kata.<br /> <br /> And to finish off the Offence Charms at Essence 5, there\u2019s Titan-Rending Fury. Simply put, a crashed enemy that attacks the Lunar can have their attack swatted aside with a reflexive clash that doesn\u2019t use up attack for the round, and just wins outright (although I\u2019d warrant that rolling successes is useful for pushing such an enemy deeper into crash). Only gets to be used against the same person once in a scene, unless they recover and are then crashed again. After a lot of more extreme Charms, I find something elegant in the simplicity of saying that a Lunar at the pinnacle of Strength and power can merely brush aside the attacks of people so disadvantaged in a fight that they\u2019re barely worth acknowledging. This is a Charm for demoralising a Solar swordsman who comes to challenge an ancient Lunar in their lair and keeps going even while being severely outclassed. I\u2019ve been hoping that some of Lunars would be casual displays of huge power, so I\u2019m really happy to see some of that going here.<br /> <br /> <br /> And that\u2019s the Offence Charms. Starts off with a slow burn, but has some very nice realisations of some of the most iconic Lunar powers and then builds up to some wonderfully brutal feeling measures of attack and counterattack.<br /> <br /> Took freaking ages to get through, though. Going to have to see if I can double time it for the rest of the Charms after lunch.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T12:34:49-07:00","id":1296475,"title":"","content":"As I suspected, there are more Mobility Charms in Strength, although at this point I wonder if they\u2019ll be very concerned with raw manoeuvring at all or just be about combat mobility.<br /> <br /> <br /> Angry Rhino Charge starts it off, with\u2026 darn it, I need to check back on the rules, just a sec\u2026 right, just lets Strength be substituted for Dexterity in rolls to ruse or oppose disengagement. It\u2019s only when using the Protean benefit of an animal with Trample that it costs motes, being able to pay two to add a success to the roll, and getting a point of Initiative if their actions succeed. Bounding Bharal Technique can spend a mote to add a success to any jumping related roll (which can also include rushes and disengages), which doesn\u2019t qualify towards the dice cap if the Lunar is at Initiative 12 (or 8 if in the shape of an animal that jumps good).<br /> <br /> Okay, starting to get more interesting with Impala Leaping Approach, which allows one to jump two range bands straight into the air, at the top of which they can comfortably land on an available horizontal surface or cling to a vertical one (which might call for an Athletics roll if appropriate); otherwise, they fall without any other protections or benefits. The Charm can also be used in the shape of a flying animal to ascend two range bands while in flight, although only once per scene, with a reset simply requiring the Exalt to attack a character at close range, or defending against the same.<br /> <br /> Spider Catches Sparrow is another form of ascending flash-step, albeit towards an enemy that is in the air under their own power, allowing one to leap at an enemy no further than at short range above them for an attack, being able to rise to close range of an enemy at the further band. If the resulting attack causes crash or at least three levels of damage, the enemy is knocked to the ground and prone, suffering falling damage even if their fall wasn\u2019t that long. The Lunar\u2019s own return to earth is cushioned a bit, only treating it as a close range fall. I\u2019m glad that Lunar power affords them a means of dealing with a flying enemy that doesn\u2019t obligate them to become a flyer themselves.<br /> <br /> Ground-Denying Defense extends a bit of Strength\u2019s element of controlling enemy movement by allowing them to roll Strength + Athletics, using up the move action, forcing enemies who want to advance to close range with the Lunar to make their own Athletics rolls against the Lunar\u2019s initial successes, with only those who overcome it being able to get close, the rest being keep at bay by blockading manoeuvres. It\u2019s a simple Charm, but it can be flurried with other actions. Considering the things they can do with some ranged attacks, it\u2019s interesting to imagine a Lunar dedicated to Strength who, through these Charms and maybe a bit of tossing people around with their grapping, are able to prevent enemy approach while peppering them with darts and stones that hit like a lead weight.<br /> <br /> Relentless Monster Pursuit sees a return of one of those Instant Charms with an effect of extended duration, as the Lunar gains two Initiative for rushing some foe, and thereafter for the rest of the scene gets a point of Initiative every time they move into close range with that enemy, rush them, or prevent them from disengaging. It can only last while the Lunar keeps pursuing that person, breaking if they either move away or rush a different character. Chasing somebody in the form of a pack hunter allows the Lunar to transfer the Initiative to an ally within short range, and it can only be used once a scene, so one probably ought to select their object of pursuit carefully. This probably goes very well with the Dexterity pursuit Charms, and I really like the image of a Lunar building up Initiative primarily or even solely from preventing a foe\u2019s escape, and then unleashing a devastating decisive attack upon them as one of their only strikes in the fight.<br /> <br /> For Essence 2 there is Foot-Trapping Counter, to double 9s on the roll opposing a lower-Initiative enemy\u2019s attempted disengage, and getting the foe\u2019s spent Initiative if they fail. There\u2019s a point resolving how the Charm would function in the event of multiple Lunars being valid recipients, which seems very curious to add here of all places. I guess that somewhere in playtesting there was seen to be some crazy exploit to this that was significant enough to warrant the addition of this. Chasing an enemy in the shape of an animal with a Speed bonus is particularly helpful, as the Lunar gets to convert some of their opposition roll\u2019s dice equal to that bonus directly into successes.<br /> <br /> Pouncing Beast Impact has a basic effect of adding an automatic success to a rush (which is non-Charm if the Lunar is in a shape that has a pouncing attack), and\u2026 something else that I think I\u2019d need to reacquaint myself more than I care to right now with the rush rules. The point is that the Lunar gets to make a Hobbes jump at an enemy that hits with enough force to not only knock them to the ground, but also generate a shockwave that might knock down everyone else within close range. Seems like a quirky little method of disrupting an enemy formation.<br /> <br /> And Mobility ends on Essence 3\u2019s Mighty Behemoth Leap, to make jumps of extreme force. The Lunar needs to have a clear launching point, with no enemies in close range that aren\u2019t crashed or trivial, and then gets to leap across up to half their Essence in range bands (no less than two) either horizontally or vertically, with no risk of falling damage for coming down from the latter. When the Lunar goes up, the weakened enemies in close range are knocked prone, and the same goes for any in close range of the landing spot who fail to oppose an Athletics roll with their own Dodge. As if having a high manoeuvrability Charm that can render those where the Lunar appears vulnerable wasn\u2019t good enough, they get to capitalise on that advantage by reflexively making a decisive attack on somebody at close range, with a target knocked prone affording extra damage dice equal to how much they failed to stay upright by. There\u2019s a Protean benefit for pouncing animals to make the Lunar\u2019s attack unblockable, but I\u2019m not sure if that refers to the shockwave or the follow-up decisive. Still, looks like a nice burst of power in addition to being able to get across long distances quickly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, like I thought, Mobility is also more focused on the combat side of it than generally getting around. I guess if a Lunar wants to match the general overland travel of an Athletics focused Solar, they do have to fall back a bit on turning into something like a high-speed bird or cheetah. Which isn\u2019t really a bad lookout, when you\u2019ve got those as options, I guess that devoting attention to Charms doing it would feel redundant in a way that even Protean effects would struggle to make up for. Or maybe it\u2019s just a matter of limited word count and low priority. As for what is here, I think it\u2019s got a good showing of force and relentless pursuit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It\u2019s short enough that I\u2019m just about good for finishing off these Feats of Strength Charms as well.<br /> <br /> <br /> Surging Thew Technique looks like a good start to enhancements to Lunar raw power, as it grants in an Instant effect an extra point to Strength for determining categories of feats that can be attempted, as well as allowing actions that normally take a long time to be either instantaneous, or at least vastly accelerated if instant would be too implausible. It has a Protean benefit of instead adding a non-Charm die to the feat action for assuming the shape of an animal already capable of those feats. The minimum is Strength 2, so I like this premise that a character with just basic strength has a means of getting to perform feats with minimal Charm investment, and getting to do them fairly well; probably not hard to find a Lunar that can\u2019t at least dramatically bash down a sturdy door.<br /> <br /> At actual Strength 3, the Charm Fortress-Smashing Charge lets a Lunar assume the aspect of an unstoppable object, being able to reflexively attempt feats of demolition against obstacles as they cross range bands, with some bonus successes (that get to be non-Charm if in a shape good at charging), but is limited to only one use per range band. From there to Essence 2 it\u2019s Yeddim\u2019s-Back Method, letting the Lunar\u2019s Strength in a feat roll be converted directly into non-Charm successes. And Many-Beasts Might provides a unique means of extending the range of a Lunar\u2019s potential feats; if the Lunar stunts to add a second Attribute to the Excellency for the action, and pays up for the full bonus, then the Attribute\u2019s value is added to the Lunar\u2019s Strength to determine if the feat can be attempted. In that spirit, it also helps out a Lunar in the shape of an animal too weak to attempt a feat, allowing the Lunar\u2019s own Strength to be considered for that purpose instead.<br /> <br /> Rampaging God-Beast Puissance is an Essence 3 Charm, and\u2026 it\u2019s a little odd. It says the Lunar can add a full free Strength Excellency to a feat of strength, but it costs 5 motes and is a Strength 5 Charm. Oh sure I know it means that the dice provided don\u2019t count to the dice cap, and so another five motes can be spent to add five dice, and I know that \u201cfree Excellency\u201d means something particular in the system, but there\u2019s still a sense of dissonance with the familiar meaning of the term \u201cfree\u201d. Still, in addition to the expanded dice cap, once per scene the Lunar who uses a feat to demolish enhanced by the Charm that upholds a higher Intimacy gets a point of Willpower for it.<br /> <br /> Terrible Steel-Rending Talons allows their shattering strength to be applied to weapons, creating a disarm gambit with extra successes on the Initiative roll equal to Athletics, that destroys mundane weapons outright if successful. For Artifacts, it allows for an additional reflexive feat of demolition, with a single success causing enough damage to break attunement, and successes equal to the Artifact\u2019s rating + 5 (or a flat 15 for N/A ratings) harm enough to cause it to require repairs to be functional. It\u2019s a Simple Charm, but if Shell-Crushing Atemi is used to break attunement to armour, it can be reflexively activated without having to pay its Willpower cost, to go straight to the attempted demolition. In any case, it can only be tried once a day. I always like powers to break Artifacts, so I\u2019m glad for this in principle, and think the specific details here look good.<br /> <br /> Unyielding Brute Will empowers a Lunar to perform a feat of strength to hold up a falling structure or hold back a moving object, disregarding the minimum Strength required (which seems to basically amount to a straightforward means of making a Strength 7 feat under particular circumstances). It does also allow one to prevent uncountable damage from such sources, though; get to catch a falling boulder or the finger of a Primordial, and drop it to the side without harm. That it\u2019s based on planting oneself firmly does oblige them to have not made a movement action in the round, though, and also requires its mote cost to be paid persistently across rounds if it needs to be held up persistently, unless the Lunar is in the shape of an animal that is built for lifting, such as a Beast of Burden (the spirit shape of which lets it be taken as a Stamina Charm) or something of Legendary Size. Can only be used once per day. <br /> <br /> Iron Tyrant Impact is the Essence 4 Charm and gets a cost of just Willpower and an anima level, helping out a bit for achieving some of those higher level feat difficulties. The roll gets to reroll 5s and 6s until they\u2019re gone, allows a feat to be attempted that would normally not function with the Lunar\u2019s size and leverage (such as holding up a large structure without it collapsing on itself or just tearing away sections of it), in the form of feats to destroy or damage large structures extend their effect out to one range band or feats of strength that act as though the Lunar\u2019s grip or leverage had the same distance. At Essence 5, every five extra successes (three in a Legendary Size animal shape) extend those effects by another range band, up until they\u2019re applying force to long range. Either case is given a few fun examples of ways in which they might be used. Also limited to once per day, but can be reset if a feat of difficulty 15 or higher is accomplished.<br /> <br /> And last of all is the Essence 5 Charm, Hundred-Handed Titan Thew. Which\u2026 oh that\u2019s right, there are Strength 10+ feats, forgot those existed. Anyway, this Charm costs one mote and two anima levels, for which the Lunar gets to attempt a feat as though they had the best Strength of any shape in their library, if the action was benefiting from a full Excellency. So you don\u2019t need to turn into a tyrant lizard to be able to try and lift another one. And the Protean effect means that an animal capable of Strength 10+ feats using the Charm have no limit on the feats they can attempt\u2026 I presume that the new chapter is going to include something capable of such.<br /> <br /> <br /> Feats of strength aren\u2019t exactly things that lend themselves to much variation in the first place, so I feel that what these ones go for is nice and direct in conveying a sense of enormous power, and a few nice images for casually lifting or smashing very large or sturdy objects. I think it\u2019s a good look for Lunars on the whole, and in particular for realising their Strength Charms.<br /> <br /> <br /> So in the end, Strength didn\u2019t end up having anything else as abstract as the nightmare Charm, which in retrospect I honest should have expected the moment I saw it was under Heart\u2019s Blood considering the precedent set by everything else. But I\u2019m actually fine with that, since as I was making my way towards Strength the memory of the other Attributes started making me think that such a variation would end up being out of place. No, instead we get a nice Attribute focused on sheer power, while still finding a few spots to apply it in more clever, tricky or tactical manners, and it\u2019s nice to see that in addition to all of the Attributes that can be a bit more indirect.<br /> <br /> Okay, only one Attribute left, and\u2026 not sure how to handle it, because I am friggin\u2019 exhausted with the charge of the last couple of days. It\u2019s not <i>very</i> late yet, and it\u2019s possible that the last chapter won\u2019t take very long\u2026 I\u2019ll give myself a break for a few hours, try and get a start on Wits, and see if I can get up early enough tomorrow to push through to the end and clear the way for the sample Lunars and animals. My denouement on the book can come when the Kickstarter has finished. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T13:22:24-07:00","id":1296491,"title":"","content":"I think free excellencies still count for the dice cap. What I think Rampaging God-Beast Puissance does is just let you add the second attribute for free.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T13:33:18-07:00","id":1296493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296491#post1296491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think free excellencies still count for the dice cap.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, so they do. You can tell that I'm a little sleepy.<br /> <br /> Not as much as when I was looking at the first few Strength Charms, and had to stop when I started drifting into waking dreams of helpful forest critters bringing me jewellery.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I think Rampaging God-Beast Puissance does is just let you add the second attribute for free.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't <i>say</i> that thooough...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T14:10:25-07:00","id":1296508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296493#post1296493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not as much as when I was looking at the first few Strength Charms, and had to stop when I started drifting into waking dreams of helpful forest critters bringing me jewellery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds like a good Lunar charm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T16:43:02-07:00","id":1296540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296508#post1296508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sounds like a good Lunar charm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, not to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-13T20:11:30-07:00","id":1296579,"title":"","content":"Now Wits. I\u2019m going to be honest, I have no particular expectations for Wits one way or another, which <i>does</i> make it rather fitting that it\u2019s saved until last after all other feelings and expectations have been effectively run dry. I don\u2019t know, it\u2019s just that something about Wits doesn\u2019t quite jump out to me as much as the other Attributes\u2026 which really should be really weird, considering how the name should imply something very resonant with Lunars. It might be because it was possibly among the worst realised Attributes of Second Edition; Appearance had basically nothing, but at least the nothing it had remained on message. But really, what it means is that I am as a blank canvas on which the Attribute is free to impose meaning.<br /> <br /> \u2026Vaguely expect something to do with Join Battle. And Resolve.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So, what about the Heart\u2019s Blood? Moonlit Path Guide is a lovely name, at least. Hmm, interesting, sacred hunt by way of journeying alongside a person through some revelatory peril, in which the \u201ctarget\u201d experiences some significant personal growth, such as an Intimacy rising to Defining and then being upheld, breaking away from a Defining Intimacy, or accomplishing a legendary social goal. The insight the Lunar gets into the person\u2019s character conveys the shape. I know I should have learned by now not to read too much into the Heart\u2019s Blood Charms, but I can\u2019t help but wonder at implications for Wits having a certain element of companionship (beyond the general Lunar character).<br /> <br /> Well, with that in mind, it does indeed start with Resolve Charms (which gives me an amusing flashback to times when people have wished there was a Resolve Attribute, vetoed largely because of its denial for Lunars). Ah yes, this was in the previews (..I think?), Moon-Follows-Sun Assurance, a permanent enhancement to the Lunar to ensure that the Ties to the Solar mate (when positive) become virtually sacrosanct, unaffected by any social influence alien to the Solar themselves (although the Lunar can also change their own attitude). It also enhances the nature of the connection by allowing the Lunar to reap Willpower once a story for forming or strengthening a positive Tie to their mate, which can bring them above their usual limit. Interestingly, they can also get that sense of affirmation from the Solar forming or strengthening such a Tie back to the Lunar. If I can court controversy for a moment, something like this is part of why I like the Bond as it currently is, where handling most of it is in the Lunar\u2019s hands, and they\u2019re the ones given the incentive to actively draw the Solar\u2019s attention back for them. I know there can be arguments against all of these, but as it is it appeals to me.<br /> <br /> Now, Grudge-Nursing Resentment allows the Lunar to sustain their rage in the face of duplicity or reasonable counterarguments, being able to increase their Resolve by 2 when faced with an influence that would oppose a negative Tie, which gets to be non-Charm when set against an influence intended to weaken or alter the Intimacy. Resisting the Lure of Madness is a boon to the No Moon who\u2019s going to be plumbing horrific depths, as they get to add their Essence to Resolve as a non-Charm bonus against influence that would inflict Derangements or cause Psyche effects, or else grant the same bonus to any dice roll with any Attribute if it would help to prevent gaining a Derangement. Like before, the self-affirmation means that the successful resistance provides Willpower.<br /> <br /> So I\u2019m starting to get at least the beginnings of a picture, of powers from the Attribute in terms of \u201ckeeping your\u201d, although I suspect it will have a bit of versatility.<br /> <br /> True-to-the-Pack Dedication is an alternative form of identity protection, allowing the Lunar to dissuade influence that would cause them to harm the subject of a positive higher Tie or one\u2019s mate, by making a roll of whichever is lower between Essence and the Intimacy\u2019s level, and adding the successes to Resolve as non-Charm bonuses, and providing a particularly powerful benefit of being allowed to spend Willpower to resist even a successful influence, <b>without</b> the need for a Decision Point. It\u2019s a Charm with only Wits 2 minimum and no prerequisite, so it\u2019s not very hard to make it so that if a Lunar deeply cares about somebody, that can be enough to make them almost intractable against being convinced to act against their interests. Shepherd Wolf Vigil is a permanent Charm that enhances the previous one, and any other Charms based on positive Ties to individuals, and allows the protection of those Charms to apply for Ties to cultures as well (in addition to a flat +1 Resolve in opposing influence against a positive Tie to a culture ruled by the Solar mate).<br /> <br /> So another thought that I\u2019m getting is that, like Perception, Wits will be another one that feels like a good secondary pick for Changing Moons (at least at the base). And then there\u2019s the comparison to Solar Integrity\u2026 I wonder if it\u2019s a matter of evolution in the Charm technology or a deliberate intent for different character path, but I\u2019m noticing that thus far Lunars are getting some more varied enhancements to their Resolve, although they\u2019re currently largely focused on dedication to something human (and a few of the Lunar\u2019s own feelings). As I recall of the Solar Charms, they got somewhat fewer to oppose influence, and more focused on the protection of Principles, even if a lot of what they got was more absolute. My recall may simply be at fault again, but these are ones I would most want to compare side by side.<br /> <br /> Huh; there\u2019s Silver Heart Faith, a Simple Charm that can be activated following Resolve being beaten by an influence against a positive higher Tie to someone, lowering the cost for resisting by one Willpower, which can bring it down to zero. Possessing Grudge-Nursing Resentment allows that benefit to also be extended to negative Ties. It\u2019s only once per day, but can be reset by upholding the Tie that was being protected by it (something that I would be insistent requires something more active and deliberate). So when it comes to holding off influences against personal Ties\u2026 you\u2019ve got this Charm in which the only thing you need against persuasion is a contrary Intimacy of the same strength as what you were being appealed to through (does the Charm you\u2019re safeguarding always count), or if used in combination with True-to-the-Pack, nothing at all (besides ten motes).<br /> <br /> Now up to Essence 2, and Chain-Breaking Fury, for the Lunar who has fallen under an influence or Psyche effect (it says they can\u2019t have resisted it with Willpower, which\u2026 I\u2019m guessing this must be referring to cases when you can have progressively paid towards resisting a Psyche effect). When acting in accordance with that influence brings them into conflict with another higher Intimacy (this might be at least one occasion where Principles can be safeguarded) or the Tie to one\u2019s Solar, they get to activate the Charm to be allowed a do-over on their Decision Point (which is carefully noted to still need to meet all the conditions and costs of the first one). If doing so allows the Lunar to shake off the influence or effect, they\u2019ll immediately form a Major Tie of rage towards the one who tried to lead them into such outrageous action. It only gets to be used once per story, but can be reset with action to uphold an Intimacy used for the Decision Point when it is Defining (or any level of the Tie to the Solar). That reset condition gives me a sense of characterization whereby a Lunar who manages to see reason against some direction gets really pissed, but then also feels encouraged to immediately go off and do something affirmative for one of their values, helpful to their friends, or harmful to their enemies. The Solar aspect conjures especially amusing ideas when running with the idea that the Tie is negative; I\u2019m picturing a Lunar who has been convinced or mind controlled into doing something, up until the point when it becomes apparent that it\u2019s going to be beneficial for their mate to whom they\u2019ve formed a mild commitment to act as a rival, which compels them to shake it off, before they go off and do some seemingly random act of harm or sabotage to the Solar or their interests.<br /> <br /> Eternal Guardian\u2019s Vow is another permanent enhancement, this one drawing more on the legacy of one\u2019s past incarnations, as the Lunar becomes capable of treating influence that would lead to harming one\u2019s Solar mate or act in some manner contrary to the Tie if it\u2019s positive as unacceptable. Interestingly, the influence of the Solar themselves is not subject to that, although who knows why they might want to tell the Lunar to act in a harmful manner. It does, however, introduce a slight weak point, just to prevent stories concerning a Lunar dedicated to Solar from becoming really one-note, I\u2019m sure; characters can make an instil roll against the Lunar, obliged to roll twice and use the weaker result. But if it works, the Lunar\u2019s protection from Eternal Guardian\u2019s Vow is rescinded against the character who made the influence until such time as the Lunar performs and action that upholds the Tie to the Solar, but <i>only</i> if it\u2019s Defining. I\u2019m glad for giving something in there to allow more engagement with the stories of such dedicated characters, and I think the exchanges that the instil roll will be made to represent could be particularly intriguing play experiences; the introduction of some niggling doubt, say.<br /> <br /> Going in another direction with that is Silver-and-Gold Union; the Lunar is able to activate the Charm to allow a positive Tie to Solar mate that is valid for a Decision Point to function as though it\u2019s Defining (while the actual Defining Tie waives a Willpower cost for resisting influence). It\u2019s only usable once per story, but can be reset if the <i>Solar</i> acts in a manner upholding a positive Defining Tie to the <i>Lunar</i>, that causes them a significant hardship in the performance, as soon as the Lunar becomes aware of that action. This alone would be something to convince me to not give the Solar an immediate Tie; when a Lunar can reap a benefit like that from it, it makes me really feel as though there should be circumstances in which getting the Solar to feel so strongly, starting with getting them to feel anything whatsoever, should be all them.<br /> <br /> Laughing Into the Teeth of Madness, there\u2019s a Charm name that\u2019s speaking to me right now. Hmm, variable mote cost, will be interesting to see what that\u2019s about. It permanently enhances Resisting the Lure of Madness to allow the Resolve bonus to be applied to actions that will help to resist shaping effects to mind, soul, destiny, or body (for those Lunars who haven\u2019t taken their damn tattoos yet). Ah, here\u2019s where the cost is starting to come in; in the event of an effect with no such avenue of resistance, 2 motes can be paid to force one, allowing the Lunar\u2019s Essence + current Willpower to be rolled against the current Willpower of the originator of the effect (or a difficulty set by Storyteller in the event of being from something not a character, such as Wyld exposure), and either character needs to pay Willpower to make up for all of their dice that show failures. The Lunar winning this one (which I\u2019m guessing includes if the other character can\u2019t pay all the Willpower they owe) doesn\u2019t stop the effect altogether, just diminishes the impact, removing any capacity it would have to prevent shapeshifting or the use of Charms, and allowing the introduction of a condition that allows the shaping to be broken if it would be permanent or deadly (although the Lunar still needs to take actions with the right Abilities to figure out what that condition might be). So I\u2019m guessing something like\u2026 the Lunar encounters a magic that would turn them to stone, and rolls against it; if they fail, turn to stone outright. If they succeed, the shape they\u2019re in at the time turns to stone, but they can shapeshift into others, and can either hold out until the effect wears off the cursed form, or figures out a means of dispelling it if it would naturally last forever. Gonna have to figure out other stuff for the tattooed Lunar\u2026 still, I\u2019m happy to see this premise put in, with slightly altered form (after all, mitigating being turned into a cat by becoming a catperson means something a bit different for folks who could do that already), for Lunars, maintaining the principle of letting the idea of such curses by engaged with in a more interesting way than shutting down altogether.<br /> <br /> It also introduces a workaround for Eyes of the Unconquered Sun, or any other magic that would deny their shapeshifting, to make the Essence + Willpower roll (with double 9s) against the other, to both prevent the effect from taking hold and conferring immunity for a scene. I\u2019m glad for that, and how it\u2019s much lower Essence than the other side of it; I\u2019m not <i>against</i> it being part of that Solar Charm, but on its own something about it didn\u2019t quite sit right with me.<br /> <br /> (Incidentally, I\u2019m having British Bake-Off play in the background at the moment, and it\u2019s helping me feel more confident about the manner in which I\u2019m reviewing everything {blush})<br /> <br /> Chaos-Defying Embrace is a permanent enhancement to the prior Charm, allowing a ten mote surcharge to be paid on the last two Charms in the tree so that their effects can be applied to protect somebody within medium range to whom the Lunar has a positive higher Tie, while we\u2019re given a note that this can\u2019t be applied to an effect retroactively. It can be applied to a Solar mate at any level of Tie (although seemingly still limited to the positive), which also grants Willpower if the protection is successful. I wonder how the mitigated curses might work for somebody who couldn\u2019t shapeshift anyway; I guess the Lunar will need to carry around their statue friend until they can fulfil the conditions to save them.<br /> <br /> Sacred Guardian Renewal is another personal source of affirmation, as the successful affirmation of Resolve against an influence opposing a personal Intimacy reaps that Intimacy\u2019s value back in motes (although again, it requires motes to have been spent on enhancing it). Preventing opposition to a Tie to the Solar treats it as though it\u2019s Defining, or gives one more mote if it actually is. Even with still having the capacity as a mote refunder, I think this one may be a bit more flexible than some of the prior Charms of that kind, and it does do a lot to solidify the sense of a Lunar\u2019s own feeling and values being something very deep and hard within them. I like the sense of contrast in that; the flesh is malleable, but the mind and soul beneath can be adamant, and gives the malleability such purpose.<br /> <br /> Hohoho, the idea in Nightmare Beast Soul that the Lunar\u2019s spirit shape can serve as a kind of internal guardian to ward off interlopers to their dreams is a delightful image. The Lunar is permanently enhanced to have +2 Resolve and Guile as a non-Charm bonus against effects that target while the Lunar is asleep, and if their dreams are entered outright by other forms of magic, a point of Willpower can be spent to manifest their spirit shape as a protective nightmare, whose action is mechanically represented by an opposed Willpower roll in which the Lunar has the advantage from the benefit of double 9s, with success allowing the nightmare to drive the intruder out, the psychic shock of the experience causing Willpower loss equal to 1s rolled on the attempted resistance, and forced to take levels of lethal damage for any Willpower they can\u2019t afford; extending from the image of the protective spirit shape, I love the idea of such an intruder being unexpectedly driven out by a nightmare beast, and being shocked to return to the waking world and realise that they\u2019re developing wounds reminiscent of what the animal would do. Scratches, bites, very very deep hoof shaped gouges\u2026<br /> <br /> Going into Essence 3 with Argent-Etched Taboo, to help draw on the cultures the Lunar values; if forced into a Decision Point from an influence overcoming a Resolve boosted by a positive Tie to a culture, activate the Charm (with a cost of just one mote) to allow one of the culture\u2019s customs that would plausibly oppose the influence to function as an Intimacy of the same level as that towards the culture. Can only be used for any given cultural Tie once per story, but gets reset for them by participation in one of its customs. This one has some really fun sense of personality; that a Lunar can care for a society while not really valuing all of its customs, but if doing so would help the Lunar to stay true to that society, then they can adapt their mindset to do so momentarily. Then feeling a need to take part in some of the culture\u2019s practices, somewhat perfunctorily, but in a manner that at least affirms to the Lunar that it\u2019s a practice that they people they care for love.<br /> <br /> Hmm, Shadow-Mind Meditation. Oh dear, what was I saying about the Lunar\u2019s mind being adamant? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> The Lunar who learns the Charm can pull together everything negative in their personality to form an underlying shadow-mind to exist independently within them, which will share the Lunar\u2019s Defining Intimacies and whatever the Tie is to a Solar mate, but the Major and Minor ones are its own, needing to either be negatively inclined Ties or Principles of pessimism or scepticism (which must make for a very fractious personality if they\u2019re directly contradicted by any Defining Principles). While the Charm is Instant, its activation requires a process of four hours of meditation to wake up or push down the shadow-mind, and once awake, its Intimacies will function in place of the Lunar\u2019s own... and I suppose from some of the surrounding language that the idea is supposed to be that the shadow-mind (which is still the Lunar, just on their worst day) is in the driver\u2019s seat. So, its Intimacies are in place, not sacrosanct as such, but more resistant to attempts to create or improve positive Ties or make optimistic Principles, and unable to voluntarily form or strengthen such attitudes while in that state.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve some thoughts, but I\u2019ll reserve them until I think I\u2019ve seen the full cascade.<br /> <br /> Right, Dark-Premise Duality enhances the prior Charm, allowing the shadow-mind to evolve into a true separate personality, a kind of picture of the Lunar on a very different life path. It gets its own set of Mental and Social Attributes, based on slight reallocations of the Lunar\u2019s own, which can change available Charms while the shadow-mind is at the forefront (both to suppress ones that don\u2019t meet the minimum, and buy ones that the true self won\u2019t have the stats to use), including Excellencies. Aaaand that\u2019s it, that\u2019s the extent of this Charm and the shadow-mind in general.<br /> <br /> So, in the end, it did turn out that Lunars got a bit more like the Solar persona Charms than what Manipulation afforded, but it still plays out very distinctly. I don\u2019t actually think it contradicts my point about Lunar self-control; unlike Solars, the altered Intimacies can\u2019t infringe upon the original self, and the shadow-mind can\u2019t be given an entirely separate set of memories or overall personality. It is technically the concept with much narrower scope, but I think that gives it a certain resonating focus; even when a Lunar is mystically altering their own mind, they\u2019re still firmly themselves, just kind of\u2026 leaning into some of the more menacing aspects of the Lunar Essence to evoke themselves in a worse light (albeit possibly one that might have greater mental acumen or social grace). I wouldn\u2019t even call it an alternate personality (something I\u2019m kind of glad the existence of this path is curtailing), more of a really dark mood that can be empowering, and switched off on a whim (within reason of the time for meditation). As an alternative distinct to these Exalted, I like it quite a bit.<br /> <br /> Okay, moving on into the last few. After all of that, glad to see something simpler in Intransigent Silver Soul, with which half the Essence can be added as non-Charm bonus to Resolve in opposing influence that would weaken a Defining intimacy or produce a result opposed by Tie to a Solar mate. That level of defence for any given Intimacy can only apply once per story, and has the severe reset condition of upholding the Intimacy with a legendary social goal. Nothing to say about this one in particular, it\u2019s just another solid addition to the ongoing identified thesis of preserving Lunar personality.<br /> <br /> And to finish off the Resolve Charms at Essence 4, there is Beast-Mind Metanoia. And here, at the end, they actually do get a callback to the old notions of social defence through adopting the mind of an animal; used in response to influence that successfully overcomes Resolve, the Lunar can shift themselves into a mindset equivalent to any animal that they could shift into. They\u2019ll still be capable of understanding their known languages, but the range of their own vocabulary and capacity to make sense of more complex statements might be compromised. In cases where an influence would be tripped up by such limitations, it gets to be treated as unacceptable (or occasionally produces an understanding significantly divergent from the speaker\u2019s intent, and the Lunar will pursue that instead; there\u2019s an example given, and I presume it means that the Lunar on this course retains that mindset until the influence is resolved). Being in an animal shape helps the Charm a lot, as it makes it significantly cheaper to assume the mind of the animal that one is in the form of. A defence of this kind can only be used once per story, but can be reset by the ecstatic experience of Limit Break.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Yes, I think Resolve came off very nicely, giving a good starting place to characterize Wits and encourage keeping them about you. More than that, the way it characterizes Lunar personality and priorities seems like a valuable level of insight across the board. And I like the social influence system enough that ways to interact with it are something I\u2019m more discerning about, and I\u2019m satisfied with the manner in which it was realised here; strong, but with channels to enable interaction, and occasionally provoking interesting responses.<br /> <br /> <br /> Yes, I think I\u2019ve made a good start into Wits. If I start early enough tomorrow and maintain my focus, I might just be able to pull this off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T05:46:00-07:00","id":1296664,"title":"","content":"Running through to the finale. Some more stuff for interacting with animals is next, something that I did start noting the absence of a while back. It\u2019ll be interesting to see the extent of Animal Ken.<br /> <br /> <br /> It begins with the Permanent Charm Pack Instinct Affirmation. That a Lunar walks the line between human and animal gives them an inherent affinity, causing animals to feel an instinctive loyalty that functions as equivalent to a Minor Tie, and ensures that Exalt doesn\u2019t get penalty to influence rolls made for targeting several at once, nor is their Resolve improved for lacking common language, although animals with the pre-existing loyalty of the familiar bond won\u2019t feel such an impulse, and magically enhanced creatures are apparently too dissimilar for it. It\u2019s a Wits 1 Charm, but with Wits 3, it also allows a Lunar to spend Willpower in an animal shape to ensure that animals of the same kind feel the loyalty as a Major Tie for the scene, with the exception of narratively significant animals that have some reason to be distracted or have overriding impulses, such as hunger, mood-influencing disease or the draw of a spirit. It\u2019s an interesting thing to start out with and make so basic that every Lunar could purchase it; the image that no matter where they are, the local animals feel a positive inclination, as well as being open to being convinced to help out the Lunar to the extent they plausibly can and doesn\u2019t risk them much. I imagine a big thing it\u2019s helpful for is wilderness survival without having to be bothered by predators or pest insects that might be a distraction or inconvenience.<br /> <br /> I had been wondering if animal riding would be an element that Lunars would get power for, and Quicksilver Steen Unity answers that question handily, conveying an idea of a shared instinct between rider and steed. It\u2019s an Instant Charm that provides a choice of three effects to apply at each activation: reflexively making a defend other action to protect a steed, reflexively making a distract gambit that the steed will benefit from (which doesn\u2019t use up attack action but is only permitted once per turn), and allows the value of a positive Tie for the mount to be added to the difficulty of gambits to knock the rider off. As an alternative, the Lunar in the shape of a riding animal who is being mounted can use the Charm to make defend other or distract actions for the benefit for the benefit of that person, and make it more difficult for that rider to be knocked off. It\u2019s a fun idea, that a Lunar can have equal facility for the role of rider and mount, applying the experience of one to benefiting action of the other. It certainly makes the flavour text of shared instincts feel very literal; I have a picture of a Lunar assuring their horse that they know first hand what it\u2019s in for.<br /> <br /> Start getting something to enhance their own familiars, as Guardian Fang Rebuke allows that animal to make a decisive counterattack in response to an enemy attacking a Lunar, which either uses up the familiar\u2019s action for the round, or uses up the next round\u2019s action early if they\u2019ve already done something. It has an Initiative cost, but if the Lunar has assumed the shape of their familiar\u2019s type of animal, that cost can be taken from the familiar\u2019s rating (although before they get to roll for damage), which gives an interesting image of how the Lunar might fight in tandem with a closely bonded animal (especially if it\u2019s something that already functions in a pack).<br /> <br /> Gets into Essence 2 already, with Blessed Moon-Beast Empowerment. It\u2019s an Instant Charm with a cost in motes, Willpower and one experience point to imbue a familiar with additional powers, one each time the Charm is applied: it can extend the Lunar\u2019s tattoos to the animal, conferring the same protections and being able to hide them when the Lunar hides their own, the familiar becomes capable of undergoing the sacred hunt on the Lunar\u2019s behalf, granting the Exalt the shape for the familiar\u2019s kills (which has to be immensely useful for gathering the shapes of Tiny and Miniscule animals without having to go out of one\u2019s way to hunt such insignificant creatures that a bird or cat will happily take care of), allows the animal to also be a subject of reverence for other animals in a similar manner to the first Charm (with the virtual Major Tie coming from one\u2019s own species), or immediately forming a Defining Tie of loyalty for their master which makes any influence from any source besides the Exalt to act against it unacceptable. It says that the experience cost is waived the first time the Charm is used, but* I\u2019m not sure if it means it\u2019s only the very first time or the first use on any given familiar. In any case, familiars with experience invested in their development that die lead to those points being refunded. These are some very neat ways to represent an affinity between an animalistic shapeshifter and an animal companion, particularly for those parts that make the animal feel as though it can be something of an extension of the Lunar.<br /> <br /> Beast-Power Awakening is a Reflexive Charm with no cost that allows a Lunar to use the familiar as a source for Essence to be fed back into empowering it; once a day when in short range of the animal, Wits + Presence, Ride, Survival or War can be rolled, with Essence in auto successes, which reaps a pool of motes for each success that persist for the scene that can be spent on Charms targeting the familiar or its own magical abilities. I wonder\u2026 yes, Solars have a similar ability, but their case is actually much more limited, as it requires one of their own Charms to imbue the capacity to draw motes into a familiar, and can only take up to five motes (albeit without need of a dice roll, and the use of the motes isn\u2019t limited). Interesting to see a case in which a Lunar\u2019s animalistic affinity allows a more ready and deeper tap into the energy of a familiar.<br /> <br /> Chimeric Beast Companion is another Attribute Charm with a universal prerequisite, as this follows on and permanently enhances Perfected Hybrid Interaction, allowing it to apply mutations to a blessed familiar within medium range, not even needing to pay for them if the Lunar is themselves currently wearing those mutations through the Charm. Moon\u2019s Hidden Face can also be used to hide those mutations on the familiar. It\u2019s both tactically beneficial, and feels like a cute reference to the idea of pets looking like their owners. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Nature-Reinforcing Allocation allows a Lunar to greatly improve training an animal\u2019s latent abilities or Merits, with invocation allowing the training to be made with specialty and in just a week, with double 8s on the training roll for teaching any latent ability that the Lunar knows themselves (a really fun idea of how turning into animals can allow Lunars to relate). At Essence 3, the Charm allows training of magical abilities at a cost of two xp a pop (also refunded if the beneficiary dies) for those that have received a Moon-Beast enhancement. The Charm has a Protean benefit in which spending the whole training period in the same shape as the familiar waives its Willpower cost (further adding fun to the idea of a special affinity between them, and the idea of teaching through personal demonstration; feels very Sword in the Stone to me, which was one of my favourite movies in childhood).<br /> <br /> Erudite Beast-Mind Expansion is an enhancement to the prior Charm, allowing it to confer an alternative benefit on a Moon-Beast enhanced animal for a four xp surcharge; granting it a human-level intelligence and capacity for higher level complex and abstract thought, and understanding of any languages the Lunar knows (and even speaking them, if its anatomy would allow for such a thing; got to have some very talkative parrots), and even some decent dice pools for diagnosing ailment, investigation, reading intentions and strategic activity, as well as improved Willpower, Resolve and Guile. It can also be applied to familiars that might already be so intelligent, providing an extra die for pools for Intelligence-based actions and anything they could do beyond the animal\u2019s natural faculties, up to a limit of 14 dice for any given pool, and still get the Will, Resolve and Guile bonuses. Like before, the first time it\u2019s used the xp cost is waived (making me think the prior Charm is indeed only for the very first time it\u2019s used), and the costs are refunded if the familiar is lost. It\u2019s nice to think about the improved levels of companionship a Lunar might get from a familiar (as well as some special tactical uses, such as sending as a spy or scout), although the thing that intrigues me the most is granting a Strategy pool; my mind goes straight to the image of a vanguard army being led by a Lunar\u2019s associated tiger or something (I\u2019m assuming it could communicate orders through writing with claws).<br /> <br /> Eternal Companion Endurance then helps them out by giving a familiar two -2 and one -4 health levels, which can be purchased up to Essence times for each familiar.<br /> <br /> Now at Essence 3, with Instinctive Unity Approach, allowing a form of instantaneous spiritual communication between Lunar and familiar, in the form of letting the Lunar enhance the familiars dice actions at a cost of 2 motes to start, and one Initiative per added die, up to a limit of the Lunar\u2019s Essence (which are non-Charm dice, for whatever that matters), as well as being able to improve static values up to half the Lunar\u2019s Essence at a cost of 2 Initiative per added point. The concept of enhancement for a familiar from the Lunar is interesting, but I do think it\u2019s a pity that it\u2019s limited to combat situations. Still, at least it gets a Protean benefit of the Lunar who grants the enhancement while in a kin shape adding a free success or +1 to pool or value respectively.<br /> Watchful Hound Vigilance helps a Lunar and their familiar to be in synch when in dangerous situations, as when they both make a Join Battle or roll to detect concealed threats, they both get the benefit of whichever one rolled higher, and if both of them win Join Battle, the familiar gets to make a decisive attack on their first turn that won\u2019t reset to base. I like the sense this conveys of being on the same wavelength in battle, or the image of the Lunar following the cue of their familiar\u2019s alert response. Hmm, the Charm has the Protean keyword, but that\u2019s either in error or a benefit has been inadvertently omitted.<br /> <br /> Heh, and a familiar apparently gets to act as an even more fierce battle companion, with Throat-Ripping Guardian to allow a familiar to reflexively clash an attack directed at the Lunar with a decisive attack of their own, so long as they\u2019re within range of the attacker (with that same condition from earlier on as taking either this rounds or next round\u2019s action). If the familiar loses, the attacker gets the option to target the attack at the animal instead of the Lunar. If the Lunar is in the same shape at the time, they get to apply a bonus from Instinctive Unity Approach <i>without</i> needing to pay Initiative for it, which seems like a pretty good way to ensure the animal wins, and makes bringing a familiar to combat feel like a good choice all around; I\u2019m picturing a Lunar in battle with a Wyld Hunt, each of whose members keeps being shadowed by a bonded animal (especially something small and stealthy, like a rabbit). Hmm, I need to check back on how quickly some of those Universal Charms allow a Lunar to shift shape\u2026<br /> <br /> Unleashed Primal Fury serves as a Permanent enhancement to the prior Charm and Guardian Fang Rebuke, allowing a surcharge to be paid to let the Lunar add their own Initiative to that of the familiar when it makes the decisive attacks enabled by those Charms. That comes at the cost of success resetting the Lunar to base as well (although if both are the same shape, their base Initiatives go up by one), and losing the same amount of Initiative if the attack fails, although it also means that the attacks <i>don\u2019t</i> count as the familiar\u2019s action for the term. I will note a contradiction in some of what is written here; the Charm cost lists the surcharge as being for three motes, while the Charm\u2019s text says it\u2019s only one; considering the potential for letting a familiar roll huge amounts of damage, I feel as though the larger cost is more fitting.<br /> <br /> Twin Beasts Dancing provides an additional upgrade to the Charms: for Guardian Fang Rebuke, allowing a Willpower surcharge to be paid for the Charm to allow the Lunar to employ a Charm with the Clash keyword in tandem with the familiar\u2019s counterattack, or for Throat-Ripping Guardians to allow a Counterattack Charm to match the familiar\u2019s clash. The latter one also allows the Lunar to enhance the familiar\u2019s attack with Unleashed Primal Fury without needing to reset their own Initiative. When in the familiar\u2019s shape, the Lunar gets a once per scene bonus to waive the Willpower surcharge for the power. This is a really nice-looking idea for the familiar bond allowing a unique form of coordination, and I quite like the image of an enemy being kept off balance by a barrage of attacks from the Lunar and their mirror image familiar. A lot of these doing a lot to further that image of the familiar being able to function as an extension of the Lunar, a bit different from the Solar in role of beastmaster I think.<br /> <br /> And last at Essence 3 is Locust-and-Tyrant Menagerie, allowing the Lunar who knows Mountainous Spirit Expression and/or Tyrant Mouse Dominion to reflexively apply those Charms on a familiar blessed with Moon-Beast while within medium range of them. The applications of this one are probably pretty multifarious, although the image that comes straight to my mind from the flavour text is the Lunar keeping a tyrant lizard concealed in a pocket, that then gets thrown at an unsuspecting group of enemies before the commitment is released, unleashing hell on them.<br /> <br /> Essence 4 then provides Supreme God-Beast Unleashed, costing ten motes, one Willpower and three levels of anima, to allow the Lunar to pour their own transformative Essence into the Moon-Beast familiar and greatly enhancing its power: the animal is granted Lunar\u2019s Essence in auto-successes to withering attacks and an Overwhelming value, the same value in dice for decisive attacks that double 10s on their damage, add it to their base Initiative and soak, get +1 to Defence and a Hardness of 10, gives a <i>further</i> +1 Defence and +5 soak to a familiar with Tiny or lower size and enable them to make withering and grapple attacks unimpeded, creates immunity to fear, and reduces the cost of Guardian Fang Rebuke, Instinctive Unity Approach and Throat-Ripping Guardian, as well as magical animal abilities by two motes. It ends on a familiar that is clashed, and it\u2019s per scene limit requires a familiar (apparently not necessarily the enhanced one) to incapacitate an enemy. Seems like a good all-around improvement to a creature\u2019s ferocity and coordination with the Lunar, but the one I like the most is the increase deadliness given to a Tiny or Miniscule familiar; heh, I\u2019m remembering some of my reactions to Solar Charms, picturing a hummingbird that might flit around slashing and stabbing an enemy... although know it\u2019s being supplemented by the picture of a Lunar who opens their hand to reveal a tarantula or smaller spider, giving it a kiss and whispering as they lay it on the ground, to go harass an opponent\u2019s ankles.<br /> <br /> Last at Essence 5 is Witch-Beast Ascendancy, which unusually has Intelligence 4 as one of its minimums and Celestial Circle Sorcery as a prerequisite. Oh shit, a Lunar can initiate a familiar uplifted to human intelligence into Terrestrial Circle Sorcery! Don\u2019t think Solars are ever going to figure out that trick. The animal gets an 8-die pool for sorcery, which can be further raised with Erudite Beast-Mind Expansion, and can teach it spells with Nature-Reinforcing Allocation like they were magical abilities. The Lunar is on the hook for the xp costs of any workings the animal performs, so might be good not to give it to one too ambitious. The Charm can be purchased repeatedly for initiating any number of familiars, with its own cost refunded should the animal die. The implication of this one are just massive; never mind the degree to which such a being can provide assistance in close quarters or at a distance for the Lunar (I\u2019m particularly imagining that one of Raksi\u2019s secrets is a menagerie of animals as sorcerous assistants), the big one that stands out to me is the degree of self-determination this could give a familiar. I\u2019m picturing things like a horse that gives itself shining wings and the ability to speak, going around shooting iridescent flames and fighting for equine freedom, or the aforementioned tarantula who summons demons or elementals to luxuriantly carry it around on a comfy pillow. Yeah, somewhere out there, I want there to be a Dominion in which the Lunar has their familiars as their lieutenants and governors, several of which take great pleasure in having visiting officials meet them in highly ceremonial manner in which they\u2019re obligated to show great respect; the tarantula in particular likes letting visitors assume that its spirit attendant (to whom it has formed a telepathic link to allow the spirit to speak on its behalf) is the lieutenant, so it can make a big show of angrily demanding that they address it directly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> All right, so the Animal Ken Charms had a lot of good stuff in them. I\u2019m personally glad that stuff to enhance riding didn\u2019t turn out to be very much of a focus, and instead the attention was given to development of familiars in a lot of interesting ways, creating a compelling image of what an Exalt able to turn into any of those animal forms might get out of or be able to bring to having such a creature as companion.<br /> <br /> Nearly there, probably not much more left\u2026<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T06:24:49-07:00","id":1296667,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of a Witch-Beast Ascended familiar out living it's Lunar, then tracking and killing the Wyld Hunt who did the deed in revenge. Possibly joining the Silver Pact afterward.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T06:41:49-07:00","id":1296677,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296664#post1296664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019m picturing things like a horse that gives itself shining wings and the ability to speak, going around shooting iridescent flames and fighting for equine freedom</div> </div> </div> </div><img src=\"http://images6.fanpop.com/image/photos/41500000/She-Ra-and-Swift-Wind-she-ra-princess-of-power-41596175-1013-541.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My Storyteller Vault releases!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/269728/Sorcerous-Ways-Mystic-Martial-Arts\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerous Ways: Mystic Martial Arts</a> - Punch people for magic power!<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/252892/These-Mean-Streets\" target=\"_blank\">These Mean Streets</a> - Advise and systems for street-level punk-noir Vampire larp","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/169-wastevens","name":"wastevens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar169_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"939"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T06:55:55-07:00","id":1296681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FallenEco</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296667#post1296667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the idea of a Witch-Beast Ascended familiar out living it's Lunar, then tracking and killing the Wyld Hunt who did the deed in revenge. Possibly joining the Silver Pact afterward.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;What happened to all you guys? You're such in a terrible shape!&quot;<br /> &quot;It... so much fire... it burns... it...&quot;<br /> &quot;It can't be the Anathema, we decapitated them and flayed their skin, we all made sure of that. They couldn't have survived, right?&quot;<br /> &quot;... not the Lunar...&quot;<br /> &quot;Then what the hell did this to you?&quot;<br /> Before the poor soul in the infirmary can articulate a reply, she doubles over and vomits white maggots.<br /> At least they are safe while they are being nursed back to health, and they can even admire the view from the court, with nothing but perfectly peaceful pigeons and butterflies fluttering around.<br /> <br /> *dun-dun-DUN*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Part-time table flipper.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/519-chairkicker","name":"ChairKicker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar519_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T08:03:25-07:00","id":1296696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296664#post1296664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, somewhere out there, I want there to be a Dominion in which the Lunar has their familiars as their lieutenants and governors, several of which take great pleasure in having visiting officials meet them in highly ceremonial manner in which they\u2019re obligated to show great respect; the tarantula in particular likes letting visitors assume that its spirit attendant (to whom it has formed a telepathic link to allow the spirit to speak on its behalf) is the lieutenant, so it can make a big show of angrily demanding that they address it directly.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a really interesting idea. I thought about much of the charmset in terms of both individuals, and the effects on creating societies, but for some reason I didn't consider how a Lunar's familiar(s) could, due to human-level intelligence, play a political role.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T09:01:21-07:00","id":1296708,"title":"","content":"And I believe this is now the last category of Charms\u2026 Navigation. Damn, so close\u2026 still, I expect that this might include not just basically getting around, but general awareness of one\u2019s presence and bearing, of the kind that might help overcome attempts to confuse or misdirect. We\u2019ll see.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It begins with the return of Lodestone Reckoning Manner, with which a Lunar gets to spend a scene plotting out a journey and then rolling Wits + Survival with Essence or 3 bonus dice <i>and</i> double 9s, to then bank the resulting successes, committing a mote for the duration of them being held. Those can then be added up to a limit of the Lunar\u2019s Essence as non-Charm successes to Survival rolls for foraging or finding shelter, or Sail rolls to navigate maritime hazards. It can only be activated to benefit once per journey (I take it that the planning stage needs to designate a destination, lest Lunars arbitrarily declare journeys completed), as well as ending once all the banked successes are spent. When at Wits 5, it can be repurchased to double 8s on the plotting roll, and allow the journey to be planned instantly. I\u2019m not quite sure what the line about banked successes being available whenever the Lunar travels means, unless it\u2019s saying that they can be applied to any kind of roll; on the face of it, it sounds like the original version has a limit that is not apparent. The Wits 5 version can only be used once per day. Lunars who have spirit shapes that migrate can learn it as a Perception Charm.<br /> <br /> The next few Charms are Permanent enhancements to the Lodestone: Dauntless Yeddim Caravan allow the banked successes to be applied to rolls against environmental hazards, traps, poisons or diseases encountered along the journey, with an Essence 2 upgrade to allow three motes to be spent while doing so while also letting twice the Lunar\u2019s Essence in companions enjoy the successes (although they get it as a Charm bonus). Moon-Cloaked Wanderer is the same, but lets the successes enhance covering tracks, Stealth rolls to evade guards or enemies, or Larceny rolls against obstacles along the journey that are staffed. Finally, the Essence 2 Charm Monarch-Wing Migration allows a four mote surcharge to be paid when activating Lodestone, providing a sense of special shortcuts along the journey that can allow the Lunar and companions to double their speed travelling along their charted route (ohhhh, so <i>this</i> is where some faster overland travel might go; clever), with a little note on expected daily travel times. Riding animals don\u2019t count towards the limit of beneficiaries, and it can only enhance travel by ship if the Lunar is at the helm and there aren\u2019t more than the limit of beneficiaries aboard.<br /> <br /> All right, what can they do now with Wyld Migration Formation? It plays off of Lodestone without directly enhancing it, instead requiring six motes to be committed for only up to an hour, during which time a Lunar and companions can travel the Wyld or similar warping environment without danger of exposure so long as they stick to the charted course, with the same exception for mounts. It\u2019s stated that when travelling in this manner through the Wyld, at least one of the paths along the way will follow Creation\u2019s physical laws. It can be reactivated at each interval without having to pay its Willpower cost again, and at Essence 3 a surcharge of three motes lets it function for the length of a day. Huh, this Charm used to belong to Perception\u2026<br /> <br /> I like the literary connotations of the Albatross-and-Hurricane Odyssey name (first for Essence 3). When the Lunar encounters a hazard, trap or naval obstacle while going about with Lodestone, they get to roll Wits + Awareness, Sail or Survival against the difficulty, able to add successes from Lodestone, to figure out a safe passage for themselves and companions (and mounts) without needing to roll to prevent damage from the danger, even in cases where doing so should seem impossible. If the Lunar is in a shape native to the environment, the roll to figure out a path gets double 9s.<br /> <br /> Seeing these Charms together is helping to develop my picture of No Moon Lunars being very well-travelled in their pursuit of assets and secrets, a distinct purpose to the undertaking from Eclipse Caste Solars.<br /> <br /> Treading Midnight\u2019s Road is a Permanent Charm to allow the Lunar\u2019s party guided under Monarch-Wing Migration to increase their speed by a factor of Essence, so long as the group is not being tracked, or has anyone know their current location or where they\u2019re going, consisting of finding all kinds of weird shortcuts rather than becoming physically faster (which is used as a basis for being unaware that somebody has started following them by noticeably becoming much slower). And Ranging Wolf Deception lets them turn the tables a bit on pursuers: the Lunar gets to conceal tracks for themselves and companions with Essence rerolls for failed dice, and removes the limit on successes that can be added with Lodestone. Doing so allows the tracks to create false leads, either to a location familiar to the Lunar or a designated direction and distance. Even superhuman and magical senses can be hoodwinked by this misdirection, following the false track in belief that they\u2019re after the Lunar. If used within a Lunar\u2019s territory, it gets to waive Willpower cost. The Totemic benefit for this one is actually to Manipulation (which pairs well with some of its powers for affecting evidence), for spirit shapes with camouflage. I like this one, it\u2019s very Bugs Bunny.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oh, and that\u2019s the end of Navigation already. I\u2019ll be honest, the main thing I\u2019m satisfied about here is the limitations on Charms for sailing, as I\u2019ve had in mind for the last three or so years that sailing in particular was a thing in which little Lunar power would be found, as part of comparing them to Solars. I\u2019m good with it being primarily focused on getting that ship from point A to point B, but not exactly more sophisticated manoeuvres that can be applied in, say, naval combat; you wanna fight ships, turn into a freaking kraken. That aside, I think there\u2019s a decent bevy of effects here for the more wayfaring Lunar.<br /> <br /> <br /> I\u2019m just gonna keep going until the end, the prior wasn\u2019t that long. Huh, Cache Charms; didn\u2019t really expect to see these ever again.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oh, but it actually manages to start off with something more unexpected and interesting, as Desert Basilisc Diadem permanently enhances a Lunar to allow them to mount hearthstones in the surface of their own bodies, benefitting from them as though they were slotted in an Artifact (with weapon and armour enhancing powers applying to natural attacks and soak, and also compatible with any magic that might turn a body part into a weapon or armour). They can slot Essence or 3 hearthstones at a time, and concealing them within clothing or similar means they can only be noticed by characters that have spotted the Tell. Setting aside the connotations for power, I like to imagine the ways in which this might give Lunars some even more distinct looks.<br /> <br /> And Radiant Basilisc Jewel allows them to actually crystallize a greater hearthstone of their very own, that is expressive of something personal like Caste, spirit shape or Defining Intimacy, which is socketed as though by the prior Charm without counting towards the limit, and can\u2019t be removed from the Lunar\u2019s body, as well as having the best mote recovery a hearthstone can bring. Damn, even with the Storyteller veto on stones whose effects would be too powerful, this looks like a pretty significant transformation to provide to oneself, especially at Essence 1. I am surprised that it isn\u2019t Totemic for Stamina, though; when I went through that Attribute with no such Charms, I was sure I must have misunderstood inferences elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Squirrel\u2019s Cunning Technique creates a special action to conceal a small, light object that gets Essence auto-successes and can\u2019t be opposed at all without heightened or magical senses, although the Lunar can exempt specific people from the effect; one could slip plans or blueprints into the crack between bricks in a wall, and only let their agents be capable of finding them again. Ah, and Magpie\u2019s Nest Resourcefulness was a previewed Charm, allowing a roll for retroactive declaration that some small, useful object was actually on their person all along, so long as there\u2019s a place to plausibly hide it. Cuckoo\u2019s Nest Cunning at Essence 2 enhances that Charm, allowing one to not only retroactively declare an object\u2019s presence, but that they\u2019ve already switched it for something on the person of an ally within medium range, contriving an explanation of when they made the switch. Huh, it says there\u2019s an extra possibility at Essence 2, and looking at the later ones\u2026 the Minimum for this is in error. Anyway, the higher level allows it to be also used to switch for enemies as a special kind of disarm gambit (or regular opposed roll, out of combat).<br /> <br /> I\u2019ve got to say, these are proving a lot more interesting than just concealing a bunch of things Elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Huh, wouldn\u2019t have expected Wits as a method for awakening Evocations, but Rousing Hidden Power permits a point of Willpower to be spent to do so for no experience cost, for a resonant Artifact or a hearthstone. The power can be lost, however, if the resonance with the Artifact is lost (such as with the warform), and it can only be used once a day.<br /> <br /> Now it\u2019s <i>actually</i> at Essence 2, and okay, with all of this good will generated, they can start doing stuff with Elsewhere for Many-Pockets Meditation; a mote commitment allows an object light enough to carry or wear (only applying to Artifacts when attuned) to be sent Elsewhere, recalling them by cancellation, although not in a manner that instantly readies them. At Essence 3 with repurchase, committing four motes allows it to banish an object large enough to require no more than a feat of strength of difficulty 10 (so up to about the size and weight of an elephant), taking five minutes of concentration or figuring out an ingenious hiding spot (although the large object can\u2019t belong to another person). So it\u2019s an Elsewhere Charm that requires commitment where a Solar one would not, but on top of not being very expensive is vastly more versatile, being able to keep hold of a large variety of things, some of which might have particularly impressive applications once drawn out. A Lunar might pull aside their cloak to reveal an implosion bow yet.<br /> <br /> Hidden Wonders Cloak enhances that Charm, allowing the objects to be concealed in a space normally too small for them instead of Elsewhere, which can allow others to draw them out of the space at the Lunar\u2019s designation. The carrier\u2019s nature can\u2019t be perceived by others, barring with the use of powerful magic to perceive magic (which also allows them to draw out concealed objects). If the carrier is destroyed, its contents go straight to Elsewhere. I must admit, apart from being able to designate an alternate to get the things out, this feels like a stylistic choice more than anything, although I don\u2019t necessarily have a problem with that. Oh, missed it over the next page\u2026 a contained can only hold twice Wits in smaller objects, or one per carrier for something massive from the Essence 3 repurchase.<br /> <br /> On to Essence 3 itself, there\u2019s Quicksilver Legend Evolution, with which it\u2019s possible to permanently keep the Evocations awakened through Rousing Hidden Power if they\u2019re used to uphold a higher Intimacy or accomplish a significant goal, although it can only be used to fix another Evocation when one has been awakened naturally, and once per story. Still, I like that image of, having awoken some of an Artifact\u2019s power, the Lunar can adhere it fully to the instrument with some act that affirms the depth of its importance.<br /> <br /> And from that comes Moon-and-Sun Panoply, a previewed Charm that I found immensely compelling for its imagery of a Lunar making an intimate and powerful gift for a mate, and the idea that they have shared custody over an Artifact, either to go forth with possession of it as an intermediary, or switching possession back and forth while in battle (images that I\u2019m really enamoured of from Xenoblade Chronicles 2). I will note, however, that I observe the language about attunement to be <i>slightly</i> misleading; the idea that the cost to attune a mate\u2019s Artifact is reduced to zero seems a bit of a cold comfort when the Charm\u2019s own cost requires a commitment of bloody eight motes!<br /> <br /> Anyway, moving on from that, and\u2026 oh Christ, a long Charm. Okay, going to break down what The Spider\u2019s Trapdoor can do (wait, Decisive-only keyword?)\u2026<br /> <br /> Oh, so the Lunar gets to retroactively declare that they lured an enemy into a cunningly concealed and devised trap (possibly with a stunt, if that stretches furthest plausibility), allowing a special for of grapple gambit that is undodgeable and unblockable to be made against an enemy at up to medium range. One roll is made to serve as both attack and control, using Wits and Survival or appropriate Craft, opposed by an Awareness or Dodge roll, and with a penalty to the opposed roll equal to Lunar\u2019s threshold successes on Initiative roll if the attack was unexpected. Usual Charms for enhancing grapples can\u2019t improve the attack, but if it\u2019s successful the enemy is clinched. The Lunar can\u2019t make the usual actions or attacks on a grappled enemy, but remains themselves unimpeded, and it takes expending whole turns of action for the caught person or a helper to remove control rounds (while attacking the Lunar is no help). If being used with a Craft, a successful catch yields three silver craft points, and an enemy being incapacitated or surrendering within the trap brings Essence gold ones. Only usable once per scene, but reset by landing a decisive attack rolling at least 15 Initiative and resetting to base, or successfully concealing from all enemies; gives me a fun imagine of giving a person a really nasty smack across the face, before revealing that you hadn\u2019t laid only the one trap, and directing their attention to the noose they\u2019ve just stumbled into before it yanks them up.<br /> <br /> The Charm Insidious Shadow Ruse upgrades Spider\u2019s Trapdoor, letting one increase its gambit to 6 in exchange for making it also function as a disarm, distract or unhorse gambit. And then at Essence 4, there\u2019s Implausible Lunar Panoply, through which a Lunar gets to retroactively declare that they were in possession of a two-dot Artifact, so long as they succeed on a roll with an appropriate Ability at difficulty 7 (otherwise the Artifact proves to be defective in some fashion). If the Lunar is supposed to have crafted it, the reveal can even yield gold points for them.<br /> <br /> <br /> All right, the Cache Charms acquitted themselves well. Lots of fun and whimsical stuff present there for the Lunar who wants to be sneaky, foresighted and have a bit of extra magic. Nice that it was kept more interesting and on message than just having a private pocket dimension.<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, five pages of Territory Charms (should have seen these coming from Charisma), let\u2019s go.<br /> <br /> Secure Den Prana: Lunar can spend a day working to make for themselves a space large enough to contain the sum of Essence and Wits people, resulting in a secure location difficult to discover or penetrate. Casual observation won\u2019t find it, and even a seeker has to overcome the successes on the Lunar\u2019s roll to make it. Trying to track people that have taken refuge within or other indirect methods of discovery are penalised by Essence, though the Lunar and those deliberately invited can find it without difficulty. Crafting the den can even make it reap craft points. Having Magpie\u2019s Nest Resourcefulness allows the existence of the den to be declared retroactively, although at a penalty to that Charm\u2019s roll (but not a heavy one). And the Charm\u2019s activation costs no Willpower if the den is within a claimed territory.<br /> <br /> At Essence 2, Rats-in-the-Basement Style allows Magpie\u2019s Nest to be enhanced, enabling its use within Essence miles of a den to retroactively declare the existence of a secret means of getting to it, at a difficulty of 5. The time between route and den will be no more than an hour and it has the same difficulty to discover as the den, although the number the Lunar can maintain is limited by Essence. If used in the form of a small creature, the secret passage can be made usable only for beings of that size.<br /> <br /> Essence 3 begins with Labyrinthine Lair\u2019s Depth, an enhancement to Secure Den Prana that allows an additional commitment of five motes to be made for the den if it\u2019s within a territory, to increase its size in terms of people by a factor of ten, although it takes a week to put it together, and increases the penalty for declaring that it existed all along. Between this and a few other Wits Charms, I\u2019m getting some means of equipping a concept I imagined for a character who convinced the inhabitants of a town on the Blessed Isle to depart and go live a free nomadic lifestyle.<br /> Forbidding Wilderness Perils is the first not concerned with dens, instead allowing a territory to be made more forbidding, as the Lunar does things like create a persistent environmental hazard (remove natural barriers against them or bargain with spirits), much more difficult to forage for food and shelter within (sabotaging the natural resources), making a traveller experience a virtual Tie of fear for the land and a penalty to rout checks for visiting battle groups, who are also harder to command in Strategic Maneuvers and easier to Demoralize (setting up fearsome monuments or cultivating gods of night and shadow), or (with a repurchase at Essence 4) making presence a possible point of repeated exposure for nasty diseases (contaminating water or animals or bargaining with disease gods), although that one can be safeguarded against by characters who are smart or observant. It\u2019s an Indefinite Charm, so maintaining the dangers can be costly, although each one after the first only costs half as many motes. Sounds good for the Lunar who wants to discourage company or punish pursuers.<br /> <br /> Harmony-With-Reality Technique allows an extended action to stabilise Wyld zones encompassed by a territory, with significant difficulty depending on how far into the Wyld it\u2019s gone. The Wyld zone doesn\u2019t actually solidify, it just becomes more habitable; its internal laws will be weird, but not of a form that makes human life impossible, and mutations either stop being inflicted (for bordermarches) or have a bonus equal to the Lunar\u2019s Essence to resist them. A Solar mate who shapes the Wyld stabilised in this way gains dice equal to the level of a positive Intimacy to the Lunar +1 on the rolls to do so; heh, so in the end, at the point where they\u2019re at their least associated with the Wyld, Lunars actually did end up getting a power to help out with Wyld-Shaping Technique (albeit of a less extreme form than people tended to propose in the past).<br /> <br /> Let\u2019s see, Hungry Mouse Mandate can be used to curse a territory to ensure that unwelcome visitors are bedevilled by the local fauna, represented as requiring an individual or a designated member of a group to make a Survival roll every week within against one used by the Lunar when the Charm was activated, with failure ensuring that some member of the group will suffer a botch on a roll to traverse or act (but not fight) within the territory, bringing potential disaster down on the group as animals menace or interfere with them. Also penalizes attempts to influence or tame the animals, unless made by somebody using appropriate magic. Within a story, can only inflict as many botches on a group equal to the Lunar\u2019s Essence.<br /> <br /> Midnight Guardian Territory lets a Lunar expend a full anima to suffuse a territory with it, creating a visible aura and causing those who sleep within to have dreams of being protected by a shining guardian beast, getting the benefits to Resolve and Guile of the Nightmare Beast Soul Charm (which can be withheld by the Exalt from specific characters). The Lunar will also become aware whenever a character sleeping within the territory has their dream\u2019s invaded, and then spend a Willpower point to fight it off in the manner of the Charm, being able to protect multiple people targeted by the same effect with just one point and roll.<br /> <br /> Fleeting Silver Mirage can be activated within a territory, and so long as the activating Lunar does not move or get knocked prone before the start of their next turn, at that point they vanish and reappear somewhere else in the territory. Essence 4 allows a two-Willpower surcharge to be paid on the Charm once per story to use it to travel <i>between</i> different territories, or to or from a secret den.<br /> <br /> And the very last Charm, at Essence 5, is Tapestry Spun of Dreams. The effect is actually quite simple; if the Lunar is within a middlemarch level Wyld zone contained by Harmony-With-Reality Technique, it can\u2019t inflict mutation on people, and if in the deep Wyld, the bonus dice added to the resistance roll become successes. This gives me a picture in which the people live a nomadic lifestyle, only entering the Wyld zone to harvest its fruits and live off its general bounty when the Lunar can return to safeguard them from it.<br /> <br /> <br /> Not much else to say on the Territory Charms, other than how these strike me as a lot more tactical girding and application for them than the more compelling Charisma Charms, but that\u2019s only fitting.<br /> <br /> <br /> And that\u2019s Wits done with. In the end, still a bit of a diffuse Attribute, but in a manner that feels more versatile than all over the place, and it expresses a lot of powers that are fun in their trickiness. I can actually see a coherent through line between them, mind; Resolve and Navigation are awareness of the self and keeping one\u2019s head clear, Animal Ken is aligning one\u2019s perspective with that of something alien, and territory and cache are about outthinking others, all falling under Wits varied umbrella.<br /> <br /> I\u2019ll save final thoughts for the Charms as a whole for my denouement. For now, I\u2019m going to dive straight into the final chapter. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T11:02:21-07:00","id":1296748,"title":"","content":"And here it is, the final chapter: Heroes &amp; Monsters. I\u2019m going to approach this one a bit more casually; I\u2019m here primarily for updated perspectives on familiar characters and sample Charms above Essence 5, so I hope to remain focused on those aspects rather than the more comprehensive breakdowns elsewhere.<br /> <br /> <br /> Huh, a little introduction for the premise, that\u2019s different; probably helpful to clarify that the write-ups are not comprehensive. And a sidebar preparing one for the approach to Essence 6+ powers; having come to mechanically define them, apparently the intention is that such Charms don\u2019t represent grand new ceilings to power, but a way of articulating something distinct to the elder or signifying the most noteworthy parts of their legend. Even I had been imagining some formats for higher Essence Charms to represent a more transcendent form of power (even if specific and unique to character), but as somebody who likes the setting to maintain a certain integrity, this definition for the extent of Exalted power that isn\u2019t using things like N/A Artifacts is quite satisfactory to me.<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, let\u2019s have a look at some of you fellows\u2026 begins with an independent and Castless Lunar, Hata Mor, depicting somebody whose extremely harsh personal experiences have created an aversion to authority that even mentors of the Silver Pact are repellent, which might leave this former gladiator as one of the single most isolated Lunars in the setting. I really like how this guy Exalted as a result of gladiatorial injuries, but his method for trying to take down the objects of his ire is by fighting them in their native arenas, of politics and commerce. The choice of stonefish as the spirit shape for someone of that methodology is very clever (I\u2019ve got a mild background that has given me an enduring respect for that animal). I came in thinking that the backstory sounded very typical, but what the character made of himself as a Lunar gave it a very nice twist, characteristic of them as Exalted, as well as reinforcing some of my newfound respect for the Casteless. Heh, and he\u2019s also got a claw strider familiar, so you know his taste is excellent.<br /> <br /> All right, let\u2019s see how they came through on promises for my girl Lilith. Nice to see backstory focusing a bit on the slight memories for her ancient experience of Exaltation (as a huntress, naturally). I knew that Desus was detached from her completely, but I was not expecting her mate to be focused on so directly, with a name and everything. I like the certain poetic sense in recounting the experience of the Solar Bond. Desus may be gone, but I find some of the elements that were compelling about that relationship, the notion that it was distinctly passionate (vague as it often was) to be effectively recaptured in some of the ascribed First Age feats; I love the idea that there can be something memorable and legendary in a demigod visiting special gardens or enjoying a sumptuous ball.<br /> <br /> Hmm, the nature of the cooling has changed\u2026 I have no love for the particulars of how Desus\u2019 abuse and Lilith\u2019s experience of it was described, but I do retain an attachment to the description in her introductory fiction in the First Edition core of <i>some</i> kind of secret hurt in her past having driven her self-imposed exile. Still, I can let it go; the needs of the Lunars in the game are different now, and such ambiguity is probably harder to maintain when you\u2019re focusing a lens on the character. No, it\u2019s better to maintain the idea that her pain and sorrow was from even war against the Usurpers being unable to relieve her loss. And I do find that it makes for very strong language leading up to the point of her eventually forsaking humanity for living as an owl, which is really all I want out of this.<br /> I like the descriptions of her placing herself into a new context, not yet ready to fully commit to the methods and duties of the Silver Pact while she\u2019s still searching for her reborn mate, but maintaining her own grudge against the Realm and Bronze Faction, and wanting to try and reach out to her surviving friends and peers. One could probably make an interesting scene of being present for her meeting Raksi again for the first time in centuries, and trying to make sense of her current persona.<br /> <br /> Like how the description of her incorporates that helm she had in her first art piece, which I\u2019ve always found to be a damn shame to have not been more prominent. Interesting that even with her current quest, she\u2019s spending most of her time in animal shapes. I like the point of characterisation in the idea that she\u2019s taciturn, but becomes markedly less so as she spends more time with people. Oh, and she\u2019s Casteless now, that\u2019s <i>very</i> interesting; I guess her time with the Silver Pact must have been quite short. Mind, Changing Moon\u2026 never really suited her, even within the versatility of Lunar Castes.<br /> Yes, I like those new weapon names, they\u2019re much more interesting. Oof, that Defining Principle of life being pain is rough, and I like how her most positive Intimacy is a Defining Tie of nostalgia for the First Age, contrasted against a Minor Principle of wishing she could let the past go. I think one could reap some interesting social scenes out of these Intimacies.<br /> <br /> Hmm, hmm, yes\u2026 I can\u2019t stop picturing her as resembling Lapis Lazuli. It\u2019ssss distracting, but useful!<br /> <br /> All right, show me her power! Okay, first, yeah, (Style) Beastman Avatar feels as though it was <i>made</i> to align with Lilith\u2019s use of White Reaper Style. Okay, good, elder Charm off on its own, by name of Waking Dreamer Fugue.<br /> <br /> \u2026. What did Silver Mask Submersion do? Hang on, it\u2019s in this Word document <i>somewhere</i>\u2026 no it isn\u2019t, I have it down as Sub<b>v</b>ersion, so one of us has made a mistake. Anyway, it\u2019s the Charm to convince yourself you\u2019re the shape you are. So Lilith\u2019s iconic power is that she can retain the illusion and delusion of being an animal even while being free to move between her shapes, with a human form coming across as a weirdo, and making the Charm free when aligned with spirit shape. That\u2026 explains a lot of the preceding elements. I like it, it\u2019s understated while still being a significant enhancement over a prior power.<br /> So Lilith ultimately came out very well to me. What about any new insights into Ma-Ha-Suchi?<br /> <br /> I like the comments on how many of his peers view him as an idealist, and having fallen so much into intimidation wouldn\u2019t be his first choice. Plus the idea that he\u2019s gotten extremely comfortable in his hybrid body, in contrast to prior Editions. I\u2019m glad that his characteristic voice remains, and now has its proper place as his Tell. The rest are things already inferred from his prior references in the book\u2026 but the Intimacies present a fascinating picture, with the hint of ego behind his desire to build the Realm anew, a Defining Principle of doubting if the struggle was worth it, and even that he has a Tie of loyalty to his allied goddesses.<br /> <br /> Ahahahaha, I quite like the clever hint of revealing that goat was his original spirit shape through which Merits come from his hybrid body and which from the chimera Charm. And his iconic power is in the role or warlord (I would have sworn something for his hybrid body). The guy gets to make a gigantic display of sheer terror for free when against a battle group being affected by a Demoralize Stratagem, with a few other benefits. Seems all right.<br /> <br /> I think I\u2019ve been most interested to see Raksi after Lilith, just to get the more complete picture. Rounds her out a little with the idea that her current state is partially owed to a point of the Usurpation having removed a sense of security, but I like how it maintains her as having enthusiastically leaned into this insight. Hah, I love the idea of Raksi as a Renaissance woman who feels herself deprived of interesting discourse because people who could be true intellectual peers are on the other side of the war. Somebody needs to start a correspondence between her and Mnemon. Oh and I get a chuckle out of them leaning into the point that Raksi has never been portrayed consistently in art to just say \u201cRaksi looks different almost any time you see her and nobody knows what she really looks like; recognise her by her Tell and the fact that she\u2019s acting like the queen\u201d. Also think a chimpanzee is a more fitting totem for her.<br /> <br /> Hmm, no Artifact weapons by default\u2026 okay, her Intimacies, this is the big thing I\u2019ve been waiting on. The Defining Principles put a lot of her into context, as like the first line it emphasizes how her sensibility of an aura of terror and immense power is rooted in a need for security. I also like how she has a Defining Tie of really liking how she\u2019s turned out, without reservation or remorse; the impression I get is that the security isn\u2019t borne from fear, just pragmatic acknowledgement that she lives in a world defined by the enemy. Hence also having a Principle that the war can\u2019t be won cleanly. It lends an interesting edge to the idea that she respects those who don\u2019t fear her, even while being driven very powerfully to try and make them. A Minor Principle of being unconcerned with the details of government is expected, but I particularly like a Tie of wonder for Luna.<br /> <br /> Huh, isn\u2019t written as using the hybrid body; makes sense, description of her using the warform always felt a bit out of place with her sorcery. The Book of Three Circles is given some Evocations to represent empowered sorcery from knowledge and being able to counter or distort basically everything\u2026and her iconic power is armouring herself while shaping her sorceries, which carries a neat image of how she used to fight.<br /> <br /> Okay, should be younger Lunars now, think I\u2019ll just skim them and make short observations on key features\u2026<br /> <br /> Shadow-Rending Razor, she was mentioned in a mini Dominion with that moon worshipping assassin cult. Oh, former Immaculate monk who tried creating a utopian commune on Exalting, only for the Realm to drive her to vengeance when they murdered it. I like how she\u2019s presented as trying to maintain a private serenity and occasionally taking personal sabbaticals from her vendetta to wander as a healer.<br /> <br /> Swift Wayward Whisper, another new person. Interesting picture of a Lunar whose own home and concerns were far from the Realm, but was drawn to the Silver Pact\u2019s cause by her sense of justice. I like how she tries to keep ties with her mortal village, and how when she impulsively takes some anti-authoritarian action that ends up causing more harm than good, she tries sticking around to fix it. Probably going to get her in trouble one day.<br /> <br /> Bit more insight into Vanamaithri Mirror-Soul\u2026 interesting backstory of having been born to a designated role as a serene social mediator that was ruined by the Lunar Essence fever, and fighting the Realm was initially more of an outlet for that than true devotion (I find that their shahan-ya took advantage). So the experience at Skandhar-Bhal was rediscovering their true self, more than anything. I like that as a point for the story to pick up at. I also like the description of how they use clothes to display their moonsilver tattoos.<br /> <br /> Aaaand that\u2019s all of the Lunars. Overall, I\u2019m happy with the approach to the elders, and find distinct Charms in each of the newer and younger people (although it could have used a sample No Moon besides Raksi).<br /> <br /> I am going to bomb through these animals so quickly.<br /> <br /> Aaaagggghhh of course it would start out with badgers, something I really like. Wait, that\u2019s all you\u2019re going to say about them? Come on, badgers can be downright vicious when they clamp down their jaws (as far as I know). Huh, mechanical rendering of burrowing is interesting.<br /> <br /> Ah, finally an insect write-up. I find something cute in the idea of beetles being given vicious little attack stats that can only properly apply to other animals of that size. Huh, butterflies can be trained to shed magical sleeping powder; that appeals to the child in me that once loved a certain Pokemon.<br /> <br /> I like that there are crabs here, and in particular how Creation includes such a range that some of them can be large enough to engage in proper combat without needing mutations or anything.<br /> <br /> Nothing to say about deer\u2026 I like the range of Merits that frogs get, although I wonder how to resolve Sticky Tongue if a Lunar increases it to a larger size.<br /> Ah, good, the mighty hippopotamus. Oh sure, you give <i>them</i> a bone-crunching bite. Overall, traits look reasonably formidable.<br /> <br /> Huh, a Great Stoat. Looks like a versatile little predator. I\u2019m glad it gives suggestion for modifying into a smaller mustelid, but I would want to adapt it to the North as a wolverine for a Lunar I have in mind.<br /> <br /> Ooohhh I don\u2019t remember the name of the dinosaurs that these Iron-Skull Devil\u2019s probably are\u2026 still, something that charges around with a solid bony head seems like a good addition to the Lunar repertoire. Wouldn\u2019t have expected something with the simplicity of jellyfish, but apart from benefiting the totem of a Lunar from some art that I really like, probably has some fine-tuned tactical applications. Hmm, and maybe commanding them as a battle group\u2026<br /> <br /> Proper respect given to the athletic prowess and fighting spirit of the mountain goat\u2026 would have expected a bit more to represent the gnawing power of rodents (real cute on that sidebar name, persons). Octopus looks good, glad to see some giant stats\u2026 good stealth for your owls\u2026<br /> <br /> Were dinosaurs corresponding to shieldback lizards not already in the core?<br /> <br /> Bah, you get out of here, swans, nobody wants you around. Although at least you get to batter with wings.<br /> <br /> Yesss, the Thunder Lizard really feels like something to introduce your park with\u2026 like the communal qualities and thick blubber of the walrus\u2026 and I\u2019ll never say no to some nice wasps (though I\u2019m surprised there\u2019s no suggestion for laying eggs.<br /> <br /> <br /> \u2026 I done? I done! Just in time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":158,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","user":"Isator Levi","date":". 03-14-2019, 02:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T11:34:18-07:00","id":1296756,"title":"","content":"The pestletail stats could always be used to represent an ankylosaur if you want, but it\u2019s really glyptodon. The shieldback lizard is an actual ankylosaur.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/229-stephen-lea-sheppard","name":"Stephen Lea Sheppard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar229_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T11:34:22-07:00","id":1296757,"title":"","content":"Well done. /10char","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T12:08:07-07:00","id":1296765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stephen Lea Sheppard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296756#post1296756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The pestletail stats could always be used to represent an ankylosaur if you want</div> </div> </div> </div>{checks back}<br /> <br /> No I can't. The dinosaur enthusiast child within me won't allow it. I'm sure I have to have read before that pestletails have shells they can retreat into, but I guess it never made as much of an impression as them smashing things with their tail, and the ankylosaur was a pre-existing image for that to latch to.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T20:12:52-07:00","id":1296875,"title":"","content":"These last few days were rather intense, so I'm going to give myself a break for a couple more before putting together my denouement assessing the book as a whole.<br /> <br /> There is, however, one thing that I want to do in the meantime:<br /> <br /> Actually purchase and read Exalted: the Lunars. <br /> <br /> I... would definitely not want to do another thread concerned with reading it in detail, after this experience. Maybe convey the odd observation or reaction to it. It just has such an infamy to it, and remains distinct as an element of First Edition that actually wasn't heavily mined for the following one.<br /> <br /> I also don't really want to pay money for it, so I'm trying to figure out an arrangement with some gift cards I still have from Christmas (gift cards kind of being one of the worst things to give to me, as I tend to feel as though buying anything for myself with them is wasteful and indulgent).<br /> <br /> Initially had a thought on using my Amazon card to buy a physical copy, as there are actually some available for fairly cheap, buuuut it didn't quite feel right for that to be my one physical First Edition book. That and the general inconvenience of waiting for it to ship. Also, I think I might kind of want to use that card to get the third volume of that cool Elric comic.<br /> <br /> The gift card that I think might possibly work on Drivethru has the problem of being worth a decent amount, which is grating against my innate thriftiness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T00:22:43-07:00","id":1296929,"title":"","content":"I have one question relating to your last post, about the NPCs.<br /> <br /> What did you think about Raksi and Ma-Ha-Suchi's intimacies? We noticed something was distinctly missing; my friends were annoyed, I found it amusingly realistic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T10:22:35-07:00","id":1297017,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296929#post1296929\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have one question relating to your last post, about the NPCs.<br /> <br /> What did you think about Raksi and Ma-Ha-Suchi's intimacies? We noticed something was distinctly missing; my friends were annoyed, I found it amusingly realistic.</div> </div> </div> </div>{raises eyebrow}<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296748#post1296748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">but the Intimacies present a fascinating picture, with the hint of ego behind his desire to build the Realm anew, a Defining Principle of doubting if the struggle was worth it, and even that he has a Tie of loyalty to his allied goddesses.</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">\u200b[\u2026]</span></span></span></div><br /> <div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">okay, her Intimacies, this is the big thing I\u2019ve been waiting on. The Defining Principles put a lot of her into context, as like the first line it emphasizes how her sensibility of an aura of terror and immense power is rooted in a need for security. I also like how she has a Defining Tie of really liking how she\u2019s turned out, without reservation or remorse; the impression I get is that the security isn\u2019t borne from fear, just pragmatic acknowledgement that she lives in a world defined by the enemy. Hence also having a Principle that the war can\u2019t be won cleanly. It lends an interesting edge to the idea that she respects those who don\u2019t fear her, even while being driven very powerfully to try and make them. A Minor Principle of being unconcerned with the details of government is expected, but I particularly like a Tie of wonder for Luna.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> What did you think was missing? Ties to specific students? I don't think those are absent, just that there ought to be focus on more core characterisation with limited word count, and that the descriptions in the Silver Pact chapter are good enough for them to be guessed at (though I would imagine they're all Minor).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T10:29:36-07:00","id":1297020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297017#post1297017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What did you think was missing? Ties to specific students? I don't think those are absent, just that there ought to be focus on more core characterisation with limited word count, and that the descriptions in the Silver Pact chapter are good enough for them to be guessed at (though I would imagine they're all Minor).</div> </div> </div> </div>I suspect it was Betrayed Feelings, Bitter Nostalgia, or something else like &quot;Had a bad breakup.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm glad it wasn't, since I'm sick of former allies turning mortal enemies having always been lovers; I especially like how it seems Raksi would happily move on if MHS would leave her alone, because she is not the one who cares about previous entanglements.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T10:53:07-07:00","id":1297027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297020#post1297020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I suspect it was Betrayed Feelings, Bitter Nostalgia, or something else like &quot;Had a bad breakup.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Why do you suspect that? Ma-Ha-Suchi has a Major Tie to her of &quot;Bitter Loathing&quot;, Raksi has a Major Tie to him of &quot;Disdain&quot;. Their feelings about one another are clear and prominent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I especially like how it seems Raksi would happily move on if MHS would leave her alone, because she is not the one who cares about previous entanglements.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ma-Ha-Suchi is presented as the one who basically became a hermit over it, where Raksi was presented in the Silver Pact chapter as having a particular liking for needling her former friend any time they happen to be present in council together.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T10:54:06-07:00","id":1297028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297020#post1297020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I suspect it was Betrayed Feelings, Bitter Nostalgia, or something else like &quot;Had a bad breakup.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm glad it wasn't, since I'm sick of former allies turning mortal enemies having always been lovers; I especially like how it seems Raksi would happily move on if MHS would leave her alone, because she is not the one who cares about previous entanglements.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, they weren't lovers. Not every friendship, alliance, or other pairing is going to be tangled up with a romantic or sexual relationship.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T11:12:11-07:00","id":1297034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297017#post1297017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> {raises eyebrow}<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> What did you think was missing? Ties to specific students? I don't think those are absent, just that there ought to be focus on more core characterisation with limited word count, and that the descriptions in the Silver Pact chapter are good enough for them to be guessed at (though I would imagine they're all Minor).</div> </div> </div> </div>Ideology.<br /> Raksi's intimacies don't include any devotion to the ideology she apparently fell out with Ma-Ha-Suchi over. No intimacies towards &quot;a Lunar Realm is impossible,&quot; or &quot;every Lunar should rule themselves&quot; or &quot;localism is best&quot;, or whatever.<br /> <br /> What she actually has is &quot;only being the most powerful can keep me safe,&quot; &quot;Her dominion (Ownership),&quot; &quot;Ma-Ha-Suchi and his adherents (Disdain),&quot; &quot;I don\u2019t care for the petty details of governance.&quot;<br /> <br /> So her motives in opposing Ma-Ha-Suchi seem to be entirely about personal issues, and not an ideological dislike of a world-spanning Realm. She hates the idea of a Lunar Realm ruling her... it's not that she's upset by the principle of other Lunars being part of a world-spanning Realm.<br /> <br /> Ma-Ha-Suchi has &quot;I will raise up a new Lunar Realm where I\u2019m admired and respected by all,&quot; and &quot;Raksi and her adherents (Loathing).&quot; He has no principle of &quot;Lunar unity is the best way to defeat the Realm,&quot; or &quot;We must raise a new Lunar Realm to rule the world with justice.&quot; Or something like that.<br /> <br /> Some of my friends thought this was something missing.<br /> Personally, I liked it, actually. It reminded me of when I was involved in politics, and the biographies of politicians I've read, and how much a lot of stuff does actually just derive from personal issues, likes, stakes, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T12:04:09-07:00","id":1297053,"title":"","content":"I just want to say from the outset lest it get overlooked: I know that I'm not disagreeing with you specifically. The following is concerned with why I found no sense of contradiction or unfulfilled expectations in the write-ups.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297034#post1297034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ideology.<br /> Raksi's intimacies don't include any devotion to the ideology she apparently fell out with Ma-Ha-Suchi over. No intimacies towards &quot;a Lunar Realm is impossible,&quot; or &quot;every Lunar should rule themselves&quot; or &quot;localism is best&quot;, or whatever.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, but you may recall from our conversation in the thread about Raksi that I believe was either several hours or a couple of decades ago that I never really presumed her investment in that idea that be particularly ideological in the first place. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> To me, Raksi's Intimacies have more or less confirmed my expectation that she's a rather pragmatic creature. Not in the crude sense of &quot;pragmatism equals having no sentimentality or moral constraints whatsoever&quot;, but just that her motivation are driven by straightforward practical considerations for her own benefits.<br /> <br /> So, while Raksi may hate the Usurpers, the read I get on her motives is simply that the Shogunate and its heirs are a threat to her life, and she likes being alive, so those enemies need to be destroyed. The main thing that it's contradicted is my tentative idea that there might be circumstances in which she's willing to die for the sake of others, which even then I said was provisional depending on what her Intimacies were like.<br /> <br /> I just want to reiterate that I really like these ideas that Raksi is presented as being ruthlessly opposed to something that is unambiguously intent on killing her, without that being presented in terms of her constantly living in some kind of sympathetic fear. She's not Cersei Lannister, there's not a vulnerable person underneath all of that who is living under a perceived Sword of Damocles. I mean hell, between her Intimacy towards herself and the description of her general outlook, Raksi is supposed to like herself more as somebody who's been fighting for her life for a thousand years.<br /> <br /> With all of that in mind, I see her perspective on how Lunar governance of the world should be distributed largely in terms of something that would thoroughly demolish institutions that could be threatening to her, while also leaving her with boundaries in which she can be supreme, thus fulfilling the other Defining Principle about the importance of that amount of power.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Ma-Ha-Suchi and his adherents (Disdain),&quot; &quot;I don\u2019t care for the petty details of governance.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>These are the parts that I think effectively encompass why she would oppose Ma-Ha-Suchi's ideals; she doesn't invest in a model that she's certain is superior, she just holds his sentimental model for the future (which is described in his own write-up as something that many other Lunars think of as too romantic) in contempt, with a hint of thinking that it would demand activity from her that she regards as tedious.<br /> <br /> Overall, my perspective on Raksi from the beginning was to wait until the write-up gave us the full picture of how the writers intended to portray her, and I was careful to not form a full picture of her until then. It helps that I find what they wound up doing with her fairly interesting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ma-Ha-Suchi has &quot;I will raise up a new Lunar Realm where I\u2019m admired and respected by all,&quot; and &quot;Raksi and her adherents (Loathing).&quot; He has no principle of &quot;Lunar unity is the best way to defeat the Realm,&quot; or &quot;We must raise a new Lunar Realm to rule the world with justice.&quot; Or something like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>That one was a bit more surprising, but in a way that I like, and a bit of a reinforcement of what sets the First Age elders apart; that they're often fighting from a perspective of having lost something, compared to the second and later generations, who are trying to build something better than the only world they've ever known. I still think he's got some positive attitude towards the actual people he might govern in his current Tie to the inhabitants of the Tangle.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T16:06:08-07:00","id":1297104,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Minton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297028#post1297028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, they weren't lovers. Not every friendship, alliance, or other pairing is going to be tangled up with a romantic or sexual relationship.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I thank you for doing that, and Wizard's friends for thinking something completely different (and owe them an apology) as once you dwell too long on the crap sections of AO3, you have goggles for people with shipper goggles.<br /> <br /> (I remember the great Zutara Wars and Harrymort Apologia. The horror...the horror...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 03-15-2019, 07:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-16T03:14:18-07:00","id":1297192,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297053#post1297053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just want to say from the outset lest it get overlooked: I know that I'm not disagreeing with you specifically. The following is concerned with why I found no sense of contradiction or unfulfilled expectations in the write-ups.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. Basically I just thought it was an interesting point that no-one on this forum seems to have noted, and so might be interesting to discuss.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-16T03:56:30-07:00","id":1297200,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297192#post1297192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure. Basically I just thought it was an interesting point that no-one on this forum seems to have noted, and so might be interesting to discuss.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Looking back over my comments on the book, it seems as though Ma-Ha-Suchi's motive should have been apparent from an early portion of his shahan-ya write-up.<br /> <br /> That's what comes of it taking a month to read both ends of a book. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Incidentally, my talk before was a bit misplaced: in the end, dedication to a goal of using most of my day for getting through large sections of the book in double time was <i>helping</i> me sleep, or at least wake earlier.<br /> <br /> In the absence of that, I'm back to just... persistently finding distractions.<br /> <br /> Need to puzzle out a way to trick myself into shutting down.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-16T05:01:04-07:00","id":1297212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1296748#post1296748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ooohhh I don\u2019t remember the name of the dinosaurs that these Iron-Skull Devil\u2019s probably are\u2026 still, something that charges around with a solid bony head seems like a good addition to the Lunar repertoire.</div> </div> </div> </div>They are <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachycephalosaurus\" target=\"_blank\">Pachycephalosaurus</a>, if you haven't already found them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T05:41:22-07:00","id":1297698,"title":"","content":"With the full compiled text released, I'm going to write out my thoughts on the fiction pieces once I read them, and then my final thoughts on the book overall before the end of the day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T06:06:06-07:00","id":1297700,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297017#post1297017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What did you think was missing? Ties to specific students? I don't think those are absent, just that there ought to be focus on more core characterisation with limited word count, and that the descriptions in the Silver Pact chapter are good enough for them to be guessed at (though I would imagine they're all Minor).</div> </div> </div> </div>As one of the people that wasn't impressed with the intimacies, and that's not due to them in and of themselves but rather they were the last thing we saw of the characters so the last chance to salvage things, what I mostly found to be missing was opportunity. Honestly I didn't care where it came but some details about what happened, why and the events stemming from it would have really interested me and it's just not really touched upon, almost every little detail has to be inferred from snippets of text. We have these two old, powerful Lunars with their own realms and we are told they've had some kind of major ideological falling out, going from once allies to now despising each other and there was such a great chance to make something of it, grand sweeping plans never realised, major ideologies in a shadow war with one another, Lunar Trotskyites and Stalinists who should be teaming up to fight the vile elemental bourgeoisie but can't get past each others differences. Something cool and expanding the setting beyond two old folks hunkered down in their palaces.<br /> Instead we got a vacuous, self-centered Raksi that, honestly for me, is by far the least interesting character in Exalted now, or rather would be if Lilith's utterly lackluster write-up hadn't bailed her out.<br /> And an actually reasonably interesting Ma-Ha-Suchi, the 'is it worth all the suffering' angle really made him come to life for me, but without a sufficiently interest rival it feels like only one piece of the puzzle.<br /> <br /> And no, I had no interest in them being former lovers or such. Frankly I find even the canon pairings a bit too much sometimes without adding ships of my own. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25050-fodor","name":"FODOR","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T08:51:42-07:00","id":1297735,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FODOR</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1287173-isator-levi-reads-lunars-fangs-at-the-gate?p=1297700#post1297700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly I didn't care where it came but some details about what happened, why and the events stemming from it would have really interested me and it's just not really touched upon, almost every little detail has to be inferred from snippets of text.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we have enough detail to matter for something that happened in the distant past of the game, without turning it into a text book or detracting from the limited word count of present day things that matter a lot more.<br /> <br /> The Silver Pact was more on the ropes and less ideologically solid during the Shogunate. The Contagion happens, and a less powerful successor state pops up; between that and having had more time to think about it, Lunars start talking about what they're going to do with their shiny new Australia. Ma-Ha-Suchi, already a bit shaky from the traumas of the Great Contagion, comes out with a minority view, and the fact that his closest ally doesn't share it at all finally needs to come to a head. A bunch of Lunars fight about it, and in the end Ma-Ha-Suchi finds that he's had enough of everything, and largely withdraws to nurse his bitterness and regrets, while Raksi decides to buckle down and start making something of herself, as front-line fighting is a younger Lunar's game.<br /> <br /> Sure some of it has to be inferred, but it's not exactly challenging to do so.<br /> <br /> Mind, I think that in cases like this, the presence of the Great Curse ought to be considered (without the writing ever doing anything as crass and inelegant as directly stating where and when the Limit Breaks were located). I can definitely imagine some Limit trigger's being invoked and some key events in this circumstance hinging on somebody's Monstrous Urge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FODOR</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">there was such a great chance to make something of it, grand sweeping plans never realised, major ideologies in a shadow war with one another, Lunar Trotskyites and Stalinists who should be teaming up to fight the vile elemental bourgeoisie but can't get past each others differences.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are factions like that, all over the Silver Pact. See also what's going on with Sha'a Oka and Leviathan.<br /> <br /> What is the issue, that they don't have elaborate manifestos? Most revolutionary splinter groups do not. I spent years having to study the IRA, the delineations in the Silver Pact along lines of regional variations, ambiguously phrased differences in fighting priorities and straightforward personality fallings out feel supremely natural and familiar to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FODOR</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Instead we got a vacuous, self-centered Raksi that, honestly for me, is by far the least interesting character in Exalted now</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure would be weird if the world turned on the whims and shifts and inexplicable popularity of vacuous, self-centered people, wouldn't it?<br /> <br /> That's another thing I like about the Exalted, how much it resonates with historical dynastic politics. What is basically random chance assigning enormous amounts of power to people who, more often than not, prove to have distinctive personality quirks and flaws, and fraught, often familial, personal relationships with other major power players is often ruinous as a basis of government, but it can make for some very compelling drama.<br /> <br /> To me, part of the fun of Exalted is the assignment of large amounts of power to people whose personalities are a train-wreck. That being said, I'm extremely of the idea of an extremely prominent and authoritative Exalt whose goals are motivated not by lofty ideology, but practical considerations in response to a world that other people have made. If they were all ideologues, they wouldn't feel very true to life to me; it's nice to have some people whose world views are as straightforward as &quot;you win or you die&quot;, especially when they're in situations in which they're not wrong about that.<br /> <br /> Nobody was watching The Sopranos to get the protagonist's philosophical outlook on being a crime lord.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FODOR</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">rather would be if Lilith's utterly lackluster write-up hadn't bailed her out.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a whole thread for that, maybe take your concerns there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FODOR</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And an actually reasonably interesting Ma-Ha-Suchi, the 'is it worth all the suffering' angle really made him come to life for me, but without a sufficiently interest rival it feels like only one piece of the puzzle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ma-Ha-Suchi's rival is the Realm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T09:00:38-07:00","id":1297736,"title":"","content":"That's a lot of text to fundamentally misunderstand my point and attempt to explain to me why my opinion is wrong.<br /> Cheers, but I disagree.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25050-fodor","name":"FODOR","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-18T12:59:59-07:00","id":1297792,"title":"","content":"Make that tomorrow; I found myself strangely inclined to naps today, and now I've got too many other things to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-26T16:13:58-07:00","id":1300132,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:9px\">People still looking at this thread when it's this far down?</span><br /> <br /> All right, I\u2019ve finally worked up the energy to start looking at these chapter fictions. Doing them all in one go.<br /> <br /> {I'm posting them all in one go, but between lack of focus and conflicting obligations, actually took the better part of a week to bother going through them all}<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, introductory piece. Right off the bat, the first paragraph gets a few things right; there\u2019s an actual group, continuing my point from What Fire Has Wrought about the benefits of focusing attention on something like a game group rather than all solitary characters, the group itself starts off with some cute dynamics between members reflecting the diversity of their origins, and it introduces a sense of some basic experiences distinct to being a Lunar (I like the idea of just casually lounging in the form of an animal in particular).<br /> <br /> Nice follow up to that with the idea of conversing about the relative value and utility of shapes (which also looks like a good organic way to tell us more about the characters and where they came from), and I enjoy the presentation of one shifting between forms as though slipping clothes on or off. The ensuing discussion is rather endearing, with the idea that a character (perhaps a bit facetiously) would hold the greatest issue with being killed by the Dragon Blooded be letting down the teachers, and the two sharing a laugh over a fun boast. The actual subject of the fiction strikes me as an effective way to introduce the Silver Pact and a signature circle; a pair being in search of a newly Chosen Lunar is an activity that would be less abstract or brutal, and is a ready framework to bring in some exposition about what they\u2019re about.<br /> <br /> Heh, okay, talked a bit too soon about it being less brutal. Touching on that element actually does have an appropriate place here, considering the significance that edge has for them, and it\u2019s good to present some of the duality inherent to them. Yes, maintains that theme with the interrogation scene. Lays some of it on a little too thick for me, but I like the added element of horror in the assumption of the war form.<br /> <br /> Nice bit of more solid backstory for that Tegama character that ties things to the general associations of the Silver Pact, and then an enjoyable twist in the Lunar they\u2019re searching for coming out at them swinging. I like the image of realisation in the form of seeing an opponent change shape as well. Oh, and is that Mirror Soul here as well? Wonder what they\u2019re doing so far afield. Oh, wait, no, that\u2019s what\u2019s their name\u2026 Shining Pearl? I\u2019ll know in a moment. So, I\u2019m guessing that means the signature circle consists of a round four; Sazay as Changing Moon, Pearl as No Moon, Tegama as Full Moon, and Tula as Casteless. I would not have expected that last one before, but with how it\u2019s been developed now, it\u2019s perfectly fitting.<br /> <br /> Always like the image of a Lunar conveying threats through smiles. Tula does a lot to endear me to her with the idea of her being protective of a fellow whom she\u2019s not yet sure if it\u2019s appropriate to designate as friend. Ah, Silent Pearl, was close. If this was my introduction to the Lunar Exalted, it would help to set up the nature of the Silver Pact to have a list of luminaries, and it\u2019s an interesting choice for a signature circle to include one of them. Heh, yes, and Silent Pearl\u2019s enthusiasm helps to round out endearment towards the circlemembers.<br /> <br /> Switching to Tula\u2019s point of view is a little abrupt, but it\u2019s nice to end on this note of the youngest Lunar making observations about the rest, both to give a sense of her freshness and a perspective to help further characterise them. I find something distinctly endearing in the idea of the four of them going up together in the forms of birds to scout things out. The characters\u2019 contributions to the planning session are a bit on the nose, but that\u2019s again something that you probably want for an introduction such as this; I do like the subtlety in the implication of Silent Pearl trying to revive some spirit contacts, though.<br /> <br /> And ends on a nice hint of the revolution that these characters are in for.<br /> <br /> <br /> It\u2019s a good introductory fiction for Lunars; gets across an evocative sense of who and what they are, and a nice sense for each of the Castes. I find the dynamic between the characters charming, and I like how the premise of one from the Shogunate allows them to cross a wider gamut, provides an in-road into the circle for the fact of their stretching back further. It\u2019s more low key than I would have expected, although I find that helps to make it a bit distinct. The Dragon Blooded intro fiction is a tough act to follow, but I find this one to acquit itself well, and think it would provide some useful foundation to people not carrying a decade or more worth of ideas about the Lunar Exalted.<br /> <br /> <br /> All right, next is something that actually does feature Vanamaithri Mirror-Soul. Yes, after having already become enamoured with Skandhar-Bhal, I\u2019m liking the idea of depicting one utilising an asset of it to engage in a mystical and spiritual<br /> <br /> experience. It\u2019s not necessarily a thing distinct to Lunars, but I like the bit at the beginning where a bit of Exalted hardiness allowing one to endure a snow storm without shielding eyes can allow one to have a distinct visual experience.<br /> <br /> It looks like this is giving me my answer about what Mirror-Soul would be seeking in finally leading warriors out of the valley (even if its order in the actual book is paradoxically placed before the question would be asked), what with those visions of people in the outside world who continue to suffer. It\u2019s interesting to imagine a Lunar that has forsworn violence seeing their war form for the first time in centuries by way of a vision, rather than simply shifting into it.<br /> <br /> Oh dang, a conversation between the two. The very premise of that feels rather intense, let alone the specific content of it. Somehow, I feel sorry for the metaphorical representation of the war form, between its query about being abandoned and Vanamaithri\u2019s statement about hoping that it might die out there.<br /> <br /> Hmm, interesting bit of interplay there, and ambiguity lent to the idea that Mirror-Soul wants to leave to redress actual injustices (and new dangers from the Solars). But the resolution is great, with Vanamaithri asserting that they can harness the rage and violence for greater ends without their serenity being compromised by it.<br /> <br /> <br /> This is another good fiction piece, effectively pulling double duty of developing (or setting up) this one character\u2019s compelling personal conflict, and demonstrating a tension that can exist within any given Lunar.<br /> <br /> <br /> Next is a story set in the court of queen Watevra Wa\u2019nabi, specifically following some of the travails of Weyna Who-Sees-Much. Oh, I see, this is covering her being tattooed (and also explains one of those art pieces, which I\u2019m going to look back on and see if we finally get a depiction of Raksi to be proud of); that\u2019s a good, evocative scenario to open the Silver Pact chapter on. I like the idea that, in Mahalanka, such an intimate event is made a matter of public spectacle. I like the description of the priest administering the tattoos being in the shape of a silver-haired older man, since it gives me the impression that it\u2019s not necessarily what he really looks like, but an adoption viewed as fitting to the moment. That kind of makes me think about many Lunars who use Many-Faced Moon Transformations or the acquired forms of elderly people when they want to make a point commensurate with being centuries or millenia old, even if they\u2019re still barely halfway through their Exalted lifespan and naturally retain whatever youthful appearance they might have had when Chosen. The idea that sometimes you\u2019ve got to look your age to be taken seriously.<br /> <br /> Nice to see Raksi herself make an appearance, with a bit of dignified bearing (and at this moment, I thank God that she never showed up in a comic). I like the idea of her making a point to stand out and be uncanny by appearing in the form of an out-of-place white person. I\u2019m also liking this Names-the-Wind character, and how he\u2019s serving to reduce some of the intensity of the situation with pleasant reassurances and colloquialisms. There\u2019s a nice image to the idea that Raksi would come down and offer some mild participation, even if somebody else is handling most of it; there\u2019s got to be a certain prestige to being tattooed in the presence of one of the key originators of the process.<br /> <br /> I enjoy the sense given there for the mindset one would be in when selecting one\u2019s Caste; looking back on the circumstances of Exaltation works very nicely here, although now I\u2019m thinking of interesting things that could be done with a character who goes for a Caste very different from that.<br /> <br /> Nice to see some interchange between Raksi and a prospective student. I think it\u2019s a bit cute for her to be asking after her reputation, and like the idea that Names-the-Wind is comfortable enough to find that funny in her presence, even if she reprimands him for it (albeit in a very indirect and not particularly harsh way). For anybody who wanted to focus more time in Mahalanka, I think one could draw a lot of good cues from this story to make the character dynamics more interesting. Yes, that sense is furthered by his choice of favourite story of what Raksi has done; like, he comes across as more than just being comfortable laughing, he\u2019s lightly mocking her to her face, and her response is to criticise and correct his tattooing. It\u2019s adorable. I also like how Weyna tries to dodge out of giving an answer to the question about reputation, and when held to it attempts something more flattering.<br /> <br /> Ohoho lord, Raksi asking a prospective new student straight up what they think of cannibalism, and then implying that she might try to get the newbie into it.<br /> <br /> <br /> I like this one a lot. It covers thoughts I had when reading through the Silver Pact chapter of shahan-yas and their adherents often having the dynamics of dysfunctional families, and I find a voice actually being given to Raksi\u2019s attitude to make her highly endearing (if no less terrible as a person).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Now, the one before the Dominions chapter. Yes, I would say that of them, the situation for the Bronze Tide would lend itself well to a little shapshot, with its more dynamic situation. I think the description of the falling city is very evocative, and there\u2019s something ironic in the idea of a modern Lunar conqueror facing somebody else\u2019s scorched earth tactics. I very much like how that transitions on into the subject of the Bronze Tide\u2019s own flight, and the horrors inflicted by Fair Folk at their back, it reinforces the chain of tragedy in the region that I\u2019m finding so compelling. Not as much to say about this one, but it does well with what it\u2019s handling.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Oh good, I\u2019m quite glad to see Seven Devil Clever show up again in one of these, and back on her home turf. Heh, it\u2019s a fun place to start out, being hunted by a Water Aspect in the filthy river while she hides in the shape of a catfish; seems appropriate for somebody so firmly rooted in that city.<br /> <br /> Oh, I like what that hunter did to discern her from the other fish; laying out bait that\u2019ll make the others swarm away and reveal the one with a different agenda is very clever (although I imagine it\u2019s the kind of tactic that will only work on a Lunar once). Conversely, I like the way that she opts to go on the offensive when she\u2019s found out, and a dolphin\u2019s torpedo-like body seems like a good thing to open up with.<br /> <br /> Nicely written action scene going on there; the Immaculate attackers seem credibly dangerous. I\u2019m particularly fond of the idea that the Water Aspect would give her a bow before pressing the attack, especially after getting his dang nose broken (that\u2019s the worst). I like the description of her running and navigating back through the city to her bolt hole, although it suddenly makes me wonder if Changing Moon was ever exactly the best Caste for her. Ends on a nice note of her commiserating with her troops. The note on how she\u2019s been building pacts with the local spirits reinforces my sense that No Moon is more fitting to her.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think this one makes good use of the more interesting elements of Seven Devil Clever\u2019s story, being reminiscent of the parts I liked from her comic in the Second Edition Manual (everything to do with it besides the chimera). I also think it\u2019s good to present a Wyld Hunt with a bit of victory, or at least genuine threat, in one of those. It helps to reinforce the nature of the danger that the Silver Pact has been dealing with for centuries.<br /> <br /> <br /> All right then, back with the signature characters with a story concerned with Tula. That\u2019s a nice image of one assessing their army\u2019s drills in bird form, and having trained them to recognise particular songs and tones as simple commands. I like the idea of her being so direct and serious that she has to cut through any sense of awe and wonderment that her shapeshifting might instil.<br /> <br /> I think it does a good job presenting the situation of this little guerrilla force as a bit desperate, and this fresh new Lunar struggling a bit with the weight of the responsibility she\u2019s taken upon herself. I can readily sympathise with them here.<br /> <br /> Oof, that\u2019s before getting into her flashing back to the scene when her bandit gang was wiped out. The imagery of her positive memories of old friends being wiped out by the grim recollection of their deaths are particularly heavy; the one with the strongest impact has to be the contrast between a companion who was a brave leader reduced to begging to be put out of her pain (and Tula needing to be the one who did it). This girl needs to find herself a centuries old nautilus Lunar immediately.<br /> <br /> <br /> Interesting\u2026 the Dominions chapter did a lot to add depth to the concept as seen in the corebook, but in the person of a signature character, I think it\u2019s good to have this idea that there\u2019s a cause and a vendetta that she\u2019s viewing as bigger than the lives of those fighting under her. She\u2019s not callous or indifferent to them or their families, but still ready for them to lay down their lives to do harm to the Realm. It\u2019s a nice reminder of the edges persisting with the Silver Pact.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now we\u2019ve got Sazay again to open up the Charms chapter. It\u2019s a bit obvious, but I do get a kick out of the \u201cwasn\u2019t herself\u201d line. I like the way that it characterizes her choice of human target to sacred hunt for this infiltration, her liking of ironic juxtaposition and harsh sense of justice. Considering the advancement of the Changing Moon Caste in my esteem, I like how the sig seems to be the most dangerous member of this circle; the Full Moon will probably just straight-up kill you. I\u2019m also interested in her intention to recount a story of the events she\u2019s involved in.<br /> <br /> Nice breakdown of the social scene that Sazay has inserted herself into, and I really like the way it\u2019s describing her methodology for shit-stirring. The social Attribute Charms have been very evocative, and I like how this fiction really feels like it\u2019s pulling those out, and it makes for a very strong sense of how assuming a stolen shape can function in an environment and with a goal like this. She\u2019s really living up to the Immaculate epithet, there. I especially like how her method for polarizing her shape\u2019s opponents are playing both sides of local politics against the middle.<br /> <br /> Ohh, I especially like the description of what she\u2019s doing with that one honest aristocrat that is loyal to the local prince compared against the Cathak satrap; that playbook of using innuendo to plant a suspicion, disappearing from the target\u2019s memory so the suspicion feels as though it originated within oneself, and then coming right back to further capitalise upon it. That\u2019s a very clever procedure. And I\u2019m always a real sucker for narratives based around manipulated memories, particularly with that idea of somebody opening the proceedings in the same way with each go around.<br /> <br /> And the whole thing is tied together by the point of the entire proceeding being to reveal her own murder (in several senses of the term).<br /> <br /> <br /> This fiction is great; it\u2019s a compelling narrative beat in its own right, and another feather in the cap of the Changing Moon Caste. It conveys the sense of an Exalt wielding their more subtle powers without having to explicitly break down the particular steps, which is a very good introduction for this chapter. And I\u2019m really enjoying the character of Sazay, this delightfully nasty and dangerous and clever trickster figure, cheerfully revelling in the subversive use of her power to achieve what she would reasonably view as a noble end. She\u2019s going to be the one for the other two circle members to beat.<br /> <br /> <br /> It looks like Shining Pearl is about to get their chance. I like how it starts with a description of how they approach gods, set as a contrast to Immaculate priorities. Their nautilus shell also sounds very pretty.<br /> <br /> Another nice description of the use of a Charm baked right into normal text, good. And that\u2019s a particularly good description for both the entry into a sanctum, and what the place actually looks like. \u201cSaturn had claimed you\u201d sounds like a neat kind of idiom or euphemism to employ, especially for a being that is not directly and personally accustomed to death. I like the bit of back and forth in Pearl using some of their own idioms to refer to evading death. Really, the whole exchange has a fun tension to it; I especially like the leading line of \u201cyou haven\u2019t changed\u201d as a setup for the god.<br /> <br /> Oh dang, they\u2019re really giving it their all in bargaining with this Boryg in the\u2026 characteristic Lunar manner. I particularly like how there\u2019s no rancour in Pearl\u2019s observations, just pragmatic acknowledgement before getting right to business. And it\u2019s great to hear one tempering their threats and demands with the idea that, if they wanted to, they could simply claim the god\u2019s iconic power for themselves. Also really fun to make the point to an immortal being to not merely end their existence, but any hint of legacy; it\u2019s not something I would have been looking for, but Fangs has been doing a good job in several places of examining the consequences of gods literally, verifiably dying.<br /> <br /> <br /> All right then, Shining Pearl you go ahead and make a nice comfortable place for yourself right there next to Sazay, you\u2019ve earned it.<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, next up there\u2019s somebody playing cards with some of the satrapy\u2019s local heavies. I think a couple of those card games sound as though they\u2019d be distinctive to the North. And I often like when fantasy settings have a bit of ambiguity, such as whether or not the presence of a magic dagger is a threat or is literally capable of uncovering cheating. Hmm, interesting that Tegama appears to be here openly, but then, he was a mortal nobleman, so I\u2019m taking it that he can still partially operate under his own identity while keeping his secrets.<br /> <br /> Ahh, no, Tegama wasn\u2019t there openly, and was indeed using magic to not seem out of place despite the lack of an introduction; another great low-key description of Charms in action, combined with a good feel for a character coming to their senses. It is a curious approach for a Full Moon, mind, although with how Favoured Attributes work they can of course be more varied, and Tegama\u2019s background probably lends itself to a social edge.<br /> <br /> Ah, it took me a few moments to realise that the references to cavalry lines are because Tegama proposed playing a Marukan-themed card game. A nice description of drawing a weapon from Elsewhere, and then it\u2019s a fight scene. Ah yes, I believe that there had been a reference before suggesting that Tegama practiced Single Point Shining into the Void Style, glad to see it showing up here. It\u2019s a good fight scene overall, with my enjoying particularly the description of how Ledaal Haraku is calling up a protective whirlwind, and it\u2019s great how Tegama is effectively fighting without needing to shapeshift (lining up with how enamoured I became with the Dexterity Charms).<br /> <br /> <br /> It\u2019s a nice fiction, although I have to admit that Tegama winds up being the weak link in the signature circle; not bad, just not outstanding. The one distinct thing I like is how he maintains a cordial attitude at all times, which feels like a nice synthesis between his upbringing and his new role. Certainly, it\u2019s good to have a different kind of Full Moon than might be expected.<br /> <br /> <br /> All right, and now that all of the pieces have been put into place, we\u2019re given a fiction in which it all comes to a head; the planted bodies and the groundwork of conflicting accusations arising from high society and spiritual double agents. A nice little mess that they\u2019ve created. I wonder what further part they might play in it.<br /> <br /> Ah yes, I\u2019d forgotten that Tula had been out there training guerrillas, so they get to stroll in and pick off the weakened infighting forces at will. Delightful. Still like how she\u2019s commanding in bird form, as well as sparing a thought to getting civilians out of the way.<br /> <br /> And Sazay there gets to continue her reputation as a little shit-stirrer. Of all the things she did, I\u2019d have to say my favourite was taking a tax collector\u2019s shape in order to steal official documents; it speaks to an interesting sense of foresight, since if they win, she gets to make sure that the administration can continue functioning smoothly, and if they need to retreat, can help make sure that it doesn\u2019t. Mind, after the manner in which she and Silent Pearl met, it\u2019s also a treat to see the idea of the two of them fighting side by side as wolves. Silent Pearl\u2019s action of luring troops out to meet their death with cold gods is simpler in comparison, but after how they had conducted themselves in their own piece, it has a certain gritty texture to it that I think Lunars being portrayed as commanding spirits in the past has lacked.<br /> <br /> And finally there\u2019s Tegama, living up to the name of his Caste as he fights his way towards their primary target.<br /> <br /> <br /> Yes, this is a nice bit of payoff after all of that setup. I find the descriptions of the fighting throughout streets, and Sazay\u2019s view of the attempts to keep control in the halls, to be very reminiscent of the best parts of The Black Company. It\u2019s nice to see the activity of the Silver Pact in action, and I\u2019m looking forward to their epilogue.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oh darn, the Cathak satrap got away. Still, it\u2019s hardly all been for nothing, and if one wanted to follow the story of this circle, they\u2019ve even been set up with a personal antagonist. I like how Tula\u2019s priority in particular should be that the enemy march home with nothing to show for it, in disgrace. And for a Cathak, that\u2019s got to sting especially (even if she could very well have fellows who overtly praise her prudence).<br /> <br /> I\u2019ll admit, in the manner in which I\u2019ve been reading these, I\u2019d lost the thread a bit on where the factional divides lay, so I appreciate having a reiteration of how the circle\u2019s grievance is with the forces of the local prince as well, since they had been collaborators whose recent animosity was provoked by Lunar provocation. Which leaves Tula in the awkward position of needing to decide what to do with captives. Well, I suppose if one\u2019s going to take responsibility for starting a revolution, one needs to accept everything that comes with it. (I adore how the fiction is focusing on some of the messy and difficult aftermath of a successful uprising).<br /> <br /> Sazay continues to be my favourite with her eagerness for murder, although Tula\u2019s assessment that they can cause more damage by sending a satrap back to have to answer for failure, and uncertainty about the extent of Lunar activities, raises her in my esteem a bit. I like the interplay between these two, and how Sazay is ribbing her new friend a bit for association with her old one.<br /> I enjoy this vignette of the scene in the aftermath of the battle, and its focus on the various activities of the people; a nice reminder that it\u2019s not all about the Exalted, and their lofty conflicts, there\u2019s some mud and sepsis down there as well. Huh, they\u2019re burning those official records? Why even bother saving them, then? Eh whatever, if it gives the people something to crowd around and make a big symbolic gesture of their liberation.<br /> <br /> Aww, Silent Pearl tending to the exhausted Tula in the middle of her ministering to the families of the dead is super sweet. I also like the contrast between their actions with the stone god and serving here as a tender for the wounded. Nice window into a bit of their backstory, and a sense of obligation to the much younger Lunar who found them. Dawww, and they\u2019re trying not to be and look scary in front of some young people, who make the effort to offer a token of thanks, that\u2019s adorable, I\u2019m genuinely grinning over it. <br /> <br /> And what\u2019s Tegama doing amidst all of it? Hmm, doesn\u2019t quite seem suited to matters of state, but is probably the most qualified out of the four of them, even if the majority of it needs to wind up with the mortal leaders. And a meeting with a Dynastic prisoner, this should be interesting. Ah, we don\u2019t get the specifics of that, but Tegama\u2019s presence is elaborate on for being a Lunar with experience in installation of a new government and knowing what assets to inquire after. Oh, but she does get a bit of a word in; I like how brazen she is directly to the face of a deadly Lunar, although it\u2019s apparent that he\u2019s good for sparring with more than just his sword, with that riposte about how Realm retaliation is likely to be delayed by the succession crisis.<br /> <br /> And Sazay\u2019s there starting to put together the story she\u2019ll tell of events. It\u2019s cute how she considers embellishing it a bit in her own favour, but silently relents when Pearl actually comes along; my initial take was that she felt a bit of a threat, but looking back it feels more like an honest decision to properly respect her other new friend.<br /> <br /> (Hmm, a few accidental misgenderings of Silent Pearl, those can be fixed in post)<br /> <br /> Finally a note on how they\u2019re\u2026 not quite formalizing their circle, but solidifying their commitment to one another. It\u2019s an interesting choice how Tula is looking to apply her talents elsewhere, although it makes sense; this whole line of fiction has been about building up the story of Kulinth, and while it\u2019s not exactly finished, it <i>is</i> resolved. It\u2019s more fitting to Lunars to not be tied down to one place anyway. I do love how, for how ad hoc this whole thing is, Sazay resolves to spruce it up a bit in the retelling.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Yesss, this has been a good sequence of fictions. It started off lower key than the Dragon Blooded got, but it\u2019s escalated very nicely, and there have been very evocative steps along the way. Strong snap shots of what each Lunar is doing in accordance with their Caste or other associations, consistent sense of their powers at work. And it built very effectively to a major final war scene and effective sense of what there\u2019s to do afterwards. It\u2019s a good story and a good look for the Lunar Exalted. Reading through all of these has been a solid finisher for what has been an exceptional book; I\u2019ll collect my proper final thoughts on it tomorrow.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-27T18:24:46-07:00","id":1300438,"title":"","content":"The Lunar Exalted have always been kind of the black sheep of the game line, haven\u2019t they? First Edition had to kind of jury rig them into the setting, and decided on an approach that was a bit too much pulp and not quite enough revival, and Second Edition was too concerned with being a reaction to how that was received and not enough with really figuring them out. It\u2019s certainly something that stood out to me, because the concept of animalistic shapeshifters has a certain personal resonance, at the same time as they were one of the earliest things that I found something off about; ages ago, before I even participated in the forums, when it was all still the preliminary research via the White Wolf Wiki, there was that moment of \u201cwait, exactly how does the shapeshifting help with all of this social engineering stuff?\u201d<br /> <br /> And in the years since then it\u2019s been such a unique source of contention. So many arguments about what they could be or should be, attempts in some semi-official sources to explore the edges, writers and developers trying to continuously promise that there was going to be a major overhaul that would finally realise their potential\u2026 heh, it\u2019s funny to think about how, for me specifically, the agenda for Lunars that emerged out of Masters of Jade has been around for far longer than any of the previous uncertainties, and even that\u2019s something that\u2019s had time to evolve (as well as needing to be constantly defended).<br /> <br /> Still, all of that wait has been worth it, because the book really knocked it out of the park.<br /> <br /> Given what I said before about the resonance of animal shapeshifters, it\u2019s immensely satisfying to me that the ultimate result didn\u2019t stray away from that, and finally brought together everything that makes it so compelling as a kind of Exalted. I\u2019ve also got to say that there\u2019s something really beneficial to Lunars having finally emerged in this environment, for what it lends to the picture of their patron deity and how they interact with the Chosen, as well as the application and comments that it\u2019s able to make about what mutability lends to identity.<br /> <br /> The Silver Pact is a part that has been talked about for so long, such that the basics of their agenda and methodology aren\u2019t really much of a surprise (although the detailing of the specifics is very fine). But what this book really did that was transformative for them was sweeping away the last vestiges of the World of Darkness Garou in their iconography and social structure; to make them feel coherent at the same time as it emphasizes variety by way of removing a uniform ceremonial structure. And more than that, how it uses the shahan-yas not merely as compelling figures who lend credibility to how the Pact promulgates itself, but as nuclei of intrigue and society. We\u2019re given so many cross-sections of their group to show us the variety of Lunar Exalted, how they\u2019re varied personalities and purposes complement and clash against each other, which really helps them to feel present and meaningful in the setting. There\u2019s something to point to within the actual Lunars book in the form of \u201cthis Lunar or that Lunar\u201d rather than just the nebulously defined Lunars as a whole.<br /> <br /> Then there\u2019s the Dominions, which would damn near justify the entire book on their own if the rest of it had been wanting. That they\u2019re more than just a solidification of the idea of what the Lunars are up to in the Threshold, and how varied their approaches can be in a manner that challenges their enemies to form a playbook against them. More even than just a number of interesting new locations added to every part of the setting, every one of them on par with the much vaunted First Edition introduction of An-Teng or Second Edition\u2019s expansions of the Octet, and at a fraction of the word count (helped by them being remarkably on point, made especially noteworthy for how they\u2019re packed with <b>so much</b> detail; there\u2019s culture and history, there\u2019s fashion and factions, there are interesting personages to make the focus of intrigue and adventure, and there\u2019s somehow still room to define them by their relationships with neighbours, who still wind up being interesting snapshots of the larger world). No, the grandest thing it does is, as Vance put it in an article with Escapist magazine, challenging the premise of what barbarianism is, as it is culturally defined both in and out of character, and has been represented in its genre. It does such an amazing job of taking all of these things that, in so many other settings goes broad and arch even when trying to be complementary, and gives them such depth, nuance, and goes out of its way to acknowledge their capacity for ingenuity within their own context. I especially love when it keeps having examples of how being a common culture, whether a centralised empire or a disparate group of associated nomads, doesn\u2019t make them homogenous. And then it goes and gives so many of the associated Lunars places and relationships with those societies more sophisticated than \u201cI\u2019m the strongest/have supernatural powers, so everything I say goes\u201d; it takes their societies and local leaders seriously, and provides guidance on how players can do the same. The Dominions are an insight into the setting that I\u2019ve been waiting seven years for.<br /> <br /> Even something as banal as the Traits chapter has something to recommend itself; a total overhaul of shapeshifting to give it more value and meaning than just vaguely gesturing at the idea that turning into a wolf is helpful for tracking, mutations that are more interesting and worthwhile than they\u2019ve ever been, and a Great Curse that emphasizes Lunar themes by way of twisting them, in a manner resulting in compelling tragedy that might just outdo that of the Solars.<br /> <br /> And the Charms. What more can I say about the Charms that I didn\u2019t realise as I was reading through them? They\u2019re magnificent. They reveal tremendous amounts of character for the Lunars, both as a whole and the specificity of the Castes. They\u2019re filled with a delightful amount of variety even for character concepts focusing on specific Attributes (never mind the permutations that can be found in cross-pollinating). They\u2019re remarkably powerful without feeling a need to compete directly with the Solars, meaning that even in cases where they distinctly lack something that those other Exalted have they don\u2019t feel short-changed. There\u2019s a lot in there that is freaking terrifying, which I recall being the first thing I said I wanted the Lunars to be in the wake of Masters of Jade, although it\u2019s also nicely offset by a few things that are deeply moving. And best of all, they didn\u2019t need to give in to every sentiment that felt as though the very concept of the Lunars was unworthy of them; it got to bring them to life, harnessing animal references without being bound beneath them, without needing to resort to a lot of really esoteric weirdness. It\u2019s hard to effectively summarise or rank the triumphs of this Charm set, but one that I have to end on is the point of how they took everything that should have felt as though it was a weakness of the idea of Attribute Charms (the sharply limited number, the not necessarily clear line of distinction from being Ability Charms with serial numbers filed off, and so on) and replaced it with a strength in a manner that is virtually seamless.<br /> <br /> After that, everything else in the book just feels like a bit of a victory lap; a few neat martial arts and some sorcery (although the initiations there took a few things unique to the Lunar background and mythology even from prior Editions and really made them pop), some more very nice Artifacts (which still manage to fulfil the duty of characterizing the mythology of the Lunars, such as the spiritual landscape they navigate, the trials that they\u2019ve faced, and the relationships underlying the Silver Pact), and rounding it out with some more detailed pictures of a few characters that gets to be the staging ground of not only redeeming some problem elder characters, but placing their power into a context that is meaningful without being completely overwhelming to the setting.<br /> All of it tied together by another compelling narrative of some like-minded Exalted in action and union.<br /> <br /> It feels like\u2026 like having had a nagging feeling for so long, and then somebody finally comes along and provides the lexicon with which to describe and understand those feelings, and it\u2019s so eye-opening. You see character concepts you\u2019ve been imagining for ages, and finally have the clear tools to realise them. And then you look at the tools themselves, how they fit together, and entirely new concepts can leap fully formed out of them. What more can be asked for in a game book, in this game book that people have waited for longer than there even was a Third Edition, what more than this?<br /> <br /> I\u2019m overjoyed to have seen it. There\u2019s nothing more to say.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-27T19:45:45-07:00","id":1300451,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://www.escapistmagazine.com/v2/2019/03/15/exalteds-new-lunars-fight-the-concept-of-barbarians/\" target=\"_blank\">The relevant article, for those who haven't read it yet.</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T02:05:08-07:00","id":1300499,"title":"","content":"Isator, I wanted to say that I've enjoyed your read-through immensely. As much as I loved this manuscript, reading your own vision of it magnified it and helped me think about why I liked it so much.<br /> <br /> So thanks for the effort you put in, it was really cool to follow you around!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Ultimate Jade-Screened Exalted Scholar, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1304239-my-next-campaign-tdhr-peach\" target=\"_blank\">THDR : Future Dragon-Blooded campaign</a>, and other links.<br /> If you like Ex3, you can support <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/holdenshearer\" target=\"_blank\">Holden</a> on Patreon!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/541-letipex","name":"LeTipex","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar541_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"902"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T08:26:48-07:00","id":1300555,"title":"","content":"Hey Isator, I appreciated this thread. I can't speak for the other writers, but I know I thrive on feedback. Thanks.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Hey, check out my first original RPG, <i>Post-Mortem, </i>here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/307131/PostMortem</a><br /> <br /> Or read my Exalted novella <i>The Silence of Our Ancestors</i> here: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/304805/The-Silence-of-Our-Ancestors-A-DragonBlooded-Novella\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...looded-Novella</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1961-dex-davican","name":"Dex Davican","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1961_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"362"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T08:42:07-07:00","id":1300558,"title":"","content":"I, too, enjoyed this thread. There's nothing quite like another's perspective to make you realize your own thoughts on a subject (indeed, it's the reason I so frequently try and bounce game ideas off people; they never quite feel fleshed-out until I've talked to someone else about them).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T03:05:45-07:00","id":1301047,"title":"","content":"Just jumping in to add my thanks for this thread. Its been a pleasure to see your perspective on the manuscript as it came out. Cheers for taking the time to type it all up and share it with us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6230-katana1515","name":"Katana1515","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}